      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last the Rev. J. Gardham Reed occupied the pulpit, morning and evening and gave two eloquent sermons to large congregations. . He also conducted the -United Bible Class in the afternoon, when Miss Eva Miller sang the "Sanctuary" with excellent taste, also contributing the "Gift," both being heartily appreci- ated. At the evening service Mr. Roberts sang "The Gates of the West," Mr. Fred Podmore presiding at the org,ttn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Confirmation at Warrington Parish</head>
        <p>Confirmation at Warrington Parish</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Dr., Chavasse) administered the rite of confirmation to 238 candidates at the Warrington Parish Church, Before and after the laying on of hands his Lordship delivered mpressive addresses. The number of candidates was made up as follows :—Parish Church 74, St. George's Mission 57, St. Clement's Mission 80, Clergy Daughters' School 15, Grammar School 0, and extras (outside Warrington) 8, making a total of 238.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of this society on Sunday, the speaker ...,W11.5 Mr. Ellams, of Manchester, who spoke upon the •ecessity of manliness in the church, faithfulness in life, and opposition to all forms of evil, either in word or action. The proceedings were presided over by Mr. Healey, who briefly addressed the meeting. Mr. B. Farrar (hon. secretary) read a letter from Mr. T. John- son, of Manchester, thanking the Wycliffe P.S.A. for their timely and generous gift towards his ragged school and its work. Mr. Farrar also reported that five new ifternbers had been enrolled, making the membership for the first month 455. He extended a hearty welcome to the new members. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mas. Bac. con- tributed an organ solo. The service was also taken part in by Mr. Bannister and Mr. J. Hawthorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Rev. A. G. D. Gee on Ritualism.</head>
        <p>Rev. A. G. D. Gee on Ritualism.</p>
        <p>At Emmanuel Church, Bold-street,on Sunday morning the Rev. A. G. D. Gee preached on "The need of the Reformed Church." The rev, gentleman said such a church was necessary because of the sacredotalism in the Church of England argkthe introduction of the con- fession and mass. The OW had come when all Protest- ant Evangelical Churchmen should leave the Establish- ment onaccount of these practices. In the evening Mr. Gee spoke upon "The creed for the Reformed Church," and pointed out the attitude of the High Church party towards Dissent, and alluded to" various secret socie- ties" in the Church. A large number of figures were given as to the use of vestments, incense, altar lights, the eastward position, &amp;c. In the year 1882, 1,662 clergymen used the Eastward position, but in 1901 the number had increased to 7,397, while Father Black, an extreme Ritualist,in a sermon preached in 1896 said that from 1,200 to 1,500 clergy of the Church of England were in the habit of hearing confession. At St. Bartholomew's Church, Brighton, in 1898, 10,000 confes- sions were heard. In conclusion he said they, as mem- bers of the Reformed Church, had no personal feeling against the Ritualistic party; it was merely a question of principle. Amongst these Ritualists there were several very zealous and active servants of Christ. The cause of the spread of sacredotalism was the inactivity and apathy of the Evangelical party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>St. John's Church, Runcorn.</head>
        <p>St. John's Church, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Sunday school anniversary serviceswere held on Sun- day at St. John's Presbyterian Church, Victoria-road, Runcorn. The preacher, morning and evening, was the Roy. J. Pulford Williams, of Cardiff, formerly minister of St. John's. A service of song was held in the after- noon, when :elected music was given by the scholars and ...;hoir. An address was delivered by Mr. Williams. A gervice was also held on Monday evening. The services were well attended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Confirmation at Runcorn Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Confirmation at Runcorn Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Dr. Jayne, the Bishop of Chester, conducted a con- firmation service at Runcorn Parish Church, on Monday evening. The candidates numbered 167, 95 females and 72 males. They were from the parishes of Runcorn (in- cluding St. Michael's), Holy Trinity, Weston, Weston Point and Halton. There was a full attendance of clergy and a large congregation. Briefly addressing the candidates the Bishop suggested the thoughts which should occupy their minds at this important stage of their lives and the resolutions they should make in the ser- vice of God and the church. After the imposition of heads, a very impressive ceremony, the Bishop addressed the coilfirmees with further words of good counsel:</p>
      </div>
