      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BAPTIST CHURCH, COLBORNE</head>
        <p>BAPTIST CHURCH, COLBORNE</p>
        <p>STREET.</p>
        <p>Special Services and Lecture.</p>
        <p>Special services were held at the above church on Sunday last, when Dr. T. Witton Davies, B.A. Ph D., F.R.A.S., Professor of semitio languages and 8.A., Testa- ment literature at Bangor University, preached morn- ing and evening. The subject of the morning service was " Prayer," and in the evening the preacher spoke of our talents and urged faithfulness in using them. The church was well filled at this, and also at the musical service in the afternoon, when an admirable programme was given by members of the Earlestown Baptist Choir, and a short, though very interesting address, was de- livered by the chairman,Mr. W. Bindloss. Next Sunday evening the Pastor, Mr. Dunnico, will continue his ad- dress on "Revelation."</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON PALESTINE. ' MR. PARKER AND THE EDUCATION ACT. "My tour in Palestine" was the subject of an inter- esting lecture by Dr. Davis, delivered in the Baptist Church, Golborne-street, on Monday, evening. The chair was taken by Mr. C. E. Parker (president of the Free Church Council.)- There was a large congregation. In introducing the Lecturer, the C A IRM AN said he supposed the reason he was asked to take the chair that evening was because he occupied the position of presi- dent of the Warrington District Free Church Council. He desired to see a closer union between the different Christian Churches—(hear, hear)—a union of purpose and effort in the great work of winning the world for the Lord Jesus Christ. There was one matter which perhaps had given a good deal of vitality to the action of the Free Churches during the past year; that had been, as they knew, a bill which was suspicious in its operation, reactionary in its effect and corrupting in its tendency. (F-lear. hear.) He was not going to speak about the Education Bill, but out of what they thought was an apparent eN'il there might come some good, and if it be that it had had the effect of drawing the Free Churches together to work in unity of purpose and love it would not after all be such a bad thing that this bill had been introduced. He was glad they were to be -taken away that night from the realm of conflict and controversy and taken in thought to that land which had been made sacred to us, eternally "sacred to us, by the fact that our Lord and Saviour tabernacled there in human flesh. It gave him )the Chairman) great pleasure to introduce Dr. Davis, of Baptist College, Bangor, to deliver his lecture on " My tour in Palestine." Dr. DAVIES, who had a hearty reception, at the outset congratulated Mr. Dunnico on the splendid work he was doing at Golborne street and expressed he hoped that h. was going to do better still in the future. He was glad to have the President of the Free Church Council at the meeting. The free churchesfwere bound together by the love they had for the common Master—the great principle for Ewhich they stood and which they were prepared to defend. Dr. Davis then proceeded with his lecture. In an interesting manner he.described the chief points of interest in the Holy Land which he visited during his tour on horseback ten years ago. The. lecture was rendered much more instructive and in- teresting by the use of a number of diagrams and the wearing of a number of Eastern costumes by six mem- bers of the congregation. These costumes were typical of tikose worn by a well-to-do lady in the East, a wealthy civilian, a gentleman farmer and his wife and a fellah or labourer and his wife. In describing the Jerusalem of to-day he said it was a very dirty place. During the dry season the refuse from the houses was allowed to accumulate in the streets and remain there until the rainy season when the floods carried away the refuse which stank badlyas they might readily imagine. In referring to the ancient temple of Jerusalem, the lecturer said the mosque of Omah stood on the site and near to it were Solomon's stables and the Jews wailing place. With reference to Cilvary, which was the most sacred of all pla,ces in the world, no one was absolutely certain where it was. Three places were pointed out as the place of the Crucifixion. The Roman Catholics claimed the site of the Church of the Holy Sepulchre as that on which. Christ was crucified but this was inside the city and could not be the place as Calvary was outside the walls of Jerusalem. The most probable place was outside Damascas Gate on ahill on the right hand side, resembling in formation a skull, and by the side of which there was a road along which people were always passing by. This was the traditional place of execution and here, he believed, the great Divine transaction took place. Reference was made to Gethsemane, which was Enow a Roman Catholic enclosnre, and to such interesting</p>
        <p>, Biblical places as Bethany, Jericho, Bethlehem and bridge.- But -as-- --a-ma-tter of fact. Mr. Hannaford Jacobs well, which the lecturer visited. At the close of senior—who will rive evidence—has not seen his his lecture, he exhibited a lumber of curios which he ,-, on brought from the Htly LO N, including the prayer s for over six weeks." se carpet, tear bottle, wine skins (described in the Bible "It would em then that -Air. William Hanna. as bottles), lady's veils and a crown of thorns. In ex- ford did not come to Dartbridge," said the Coroner, hibiting the garments worn in the East, the LecOrer ." for if he had he would certainly have stayed at pointed out that the " hem " of the garment was in his father's house. reality a tastle which was fastened to the end of the But Tait was prepared for this objection. "Not garment. necessarily," he said, coolly. "Mr. William Han: ford and his father were not on good terms. , The Rev. H. DITNNICO proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer and the chairmen. Ile said they were pleased to see Mr. Itarker with them, not simply because Also the journey—as will be seen by that letter— he was the president of the Free Church Council, but was made with the object of seeing Mr. Tremewan because they looked upon him as one who was everandnot Mr. Hannaford, senior. lam certain that seeking to improve the civic, religions and social life of Mr. William was in Mr. Tremewan's study on the the community. (Applause) tor. Davies was,he thoOljbt night of the 15th January, some time after ten the best all-round man he had ever met,and no professor o'clock I leave it to the intelligence of the jury." was more loved by his students than Dr. Davies. (Hear, A Juror (impervious to flattery): "Mr. Willram hear.) Hannaord might have been ;n the etedy at that The Rev. J. S. HUG FIES saconied; and the votes were time. He might not have committed the crime." unanimously passed. Tat: "There was bad blood between Tremewan Dr. Davies and Mr. Plrker returned thanks and the and the young man on account of Miss Tremewan. meeting terminated with the benediction. The Coroner protested: "It is not fair to intro- duce Miss Tremevran's name," said he sharply, and be was supported in this contention bv the jury. They nearly all knew Dora, and dia not wish her to be dragged into the matter. But Tait persisted dauntlessly. "Unless this matter is threshed out," be said, "and on these lines, the truth will never be arrived at. I say that Mr. Tremewan ordered Mr. William Hannaford out of his house becalm he oWected to the attentions which the young A competition, which it is hoped will become a re- man paid his daughter.. I say also that Mr. Wil- cognized annual affair—vie., an eight miles cross- ham called on the night of the 15th at the house, country race among picked teams from England,lreland, and—in my opinion—quarrelled with the deceased. Scotland and Wales—took place at Hamilton Park, lam sure that he strangled him, and—" Glasgow,_ on Saturday. Among those who participated were A. Shrubb, South London Harriers, winner of the There ensued a violent quarrel between witness and Coroner, in which the sympathies of the public English cross-country champilmship, 1903; S. J. Robin- wirre Tait, the latter. Tenby objected to the theory and the Inspector son, runner-up in the same event and a previous holder held bluffly to life at the honour; W. A. Mercer, Farnworth Harriers; J. or Hosker, Farnworth Harriers, Northern Counties opinion. He was so , positive and so insietent, champion. The race was run over four circuits of the . that when he stepped down many people began course, covering a distance of close on eight miles. to think that Will might have killed Tremewan. Shrubb shot away with the lead; but at , two Miles he Some even suggested in whispers that Dora might and Daly were running abreast. At four miles Shrubb have urged him to commit the crime. • led by 100 yards, Daly and Edwards following in the The servants of the deceased then gave evidence. order named; and at six miles Shrubb was still well None of them had heard a noise during the night, ahead, Edwards then being second. Running with ease to the end Shrubb scored. a hollow victory by nearly and they were all in bed—at the special command - 300 yards from Edwards. who beat Daly by 80 yards for of therr" master —at 10 o'clock. Mrs. Beelowen, second place. At the finish the pcsitions were-1, A. who knew more about the inner life of her master Shrubb, South London Harriers; 2, T. Edwards, Man- than anyone else, stated that she knew of no , chester Harriers :3, J. Daly, Galway Harriers ; .1, A. ona likely to murder him. Aldridge, Kent Athletic Club; 5, S.J. Robinson, Salford " What about Mr.. William Hannaford ? " asked Harriers; 6, A, W. Mercer, Farnworth Harriers. Best the Corener, looking up from his notes. time 46min. 22 3 ssecs. The team placings were as Mrs. Bedowen did not believe e, word of what</p>
        <p>follow: Mr" Tait had said. She never heard Tremewan 1, England: Shrubb, 1• • Edwards, 2; Aldridge, 4; say That he expected a visitor on that night, much Robinson, 5 ; Mercer, 6 • I:awson, 7 ; total.. 25 points. 1 - Jess that the visitor wao Mr. William Haimaford. 2, Ireland : Daly, 3 ; 'Hynes, 8 ; Creedon, 13 ; Whyte, It was not true that William and Tremewan had 16; Ctrrtis, 18; M'Caffrey, 20 ; total, 78 points. 3, Scotland: Crosbie, 10; Ranken, 14; Ure, 17 :A. quarrelled. The quarrel was between the seniors; Hughes, 21; Reston, 22 ; Isfulrine, 23 ; total, 107 points. not between the young people. Certainly Mr. 4, Wales: Marsh, 12; Harris, 19 ; Turner, 25; Tremewan had asked Mr. William not to eall at Palmer, 27; Davies, 28 ; Thoruas,29 ; total, 140 points, the house, but he had not ordered him out of it; and his reouest was made quite, cirilly,</p>
