      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING, International Contest,</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING, International Contest,</p>
        <p>FARNWORTiI COAIPETITORS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 3, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>EY</p>
        <p>Bedowen heard the conversation. The knew nothing about the letter- making the appointment. The door of Miss Tremewan's room was certainly fastened, but she could give no reason for its being so. Tremewan was in his usual spirits on the fatal evening. She did not hear any noise during the night, and she had retired with the rest of the servants at 10 o'clock. It was seven in the morn- ing when she entered the study. Deceased was at his desk; the window was wide open. In her opinion he had left the window open while writing and some tramp had entered to kill him. The Coroner did not agree with this last supposi- tion. No tramp could have murdered an active man like Tremewan save by lulling him into a state of false security, and taking him unawares. On the face of it the tramp theory was impossible He asked if .Mrs. Bedoweri knew of any enemy of Tremewan's likely to kill him.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bedowen did not know. Tremewan was a hard man, and got on with few people, but she knew of no one so vindictive as to take life. She was quite sure that Mr. William Hannaford was innocent, in spite of the proofs against him. She saw no letter making the appointment, and when the letter was shown to her, hinted that Tait might have • prepared it himself. In fact, nothing was to be got out of Mrs. Bedowen, and when she stopped talking she left the' case pretty well where she found it. With all her willingness she could throw no light on the subject. Treme. wan was dead, but who had murdered him Mrs. Bedowen could not say. She dissented from the theory of Mr. William Hannaford's Mrs. Tremewan was not called, as she was still ill, and moreover there was nothing she could say likely to prove the guilt of any person. But there was a sensation when Dora Tremewan. stepped be- fore the jury. It was on this witness that Tait relied to prove his case, and he resolved to have her rigorously cross-examined. She was dressed in deep mourning, and her appearance was the signal for a paurrour of sympathy. At the re- quest of Tenlry she raised her veil, displaying a white, anxious face. She was alone and unsup- ported. Prom Mr. Hannaford was not present. She had to rely entirely on herself.</p>
        <p>Tenby questroned her-very gently, sympathising, as was ,but natural, with her distress. But for Tait, he would have shirked going into the matter of her relations with Will, but he was forced to do so with the cold eyes of the Inspector on him. Also Tait prompted him. Dora deposed that Mrs. I3edowen had first told her of the murder. She went downstairs at once and fonnd her father •dead as the last witness described. The window was open. She did not know who could have killed her father. She heard no noise during the night- Here, prompted by Tait, the Coronet inter- rupted her: What of the noise at your door shortly after ten o'clock ?" he asked. "I certainly did hear a noise then," replied Miss Tremewan, reluctantly, "and I got up to see what it was. I could not open the door," and then she went on to state how Mrs. Bedowen had found that the door was fastened, and in what fashion. It was no use concealing this fact, as Tait already knew of the matter. Coroner: "Why was the door fastened 7 "</p>
        <p>Witness: "1 can't say; I know of no reason." Coroner (prompted by the Inspector): "Might it not have been to prevent your interrupting the moeting between your father and Mr. Hanna-. ford ?"</p>
        <p>Witness (reluctantly): "I did not know that there was any meeting." Coroner: "What about this letter? Is it in Mr. William liannaford's writing ?" Witness (examining the letter) : "Yes. It is his writing. But I knew Tiothing of this at the time. My father said nothing about Mr. Hanna. ford coming to the house. 1 never thought such a meeting would take place." Coroner: "Did you not tell Inspector Tait that such a meeting might have taken place on the assunaption that for such reason you were fas- tened in your room ?"</p>
        <p>Witnesi; (with hesitation) : "Yes. It was the only reason that I could think of. But I never thought for a moment that Mr. Hannaford did come to the house. It was mere guesswork on My part." Coroner: "If you had known would you have come down ?"</p>
        <p>Witt Tess "It is possible. I might have feaged a quarrel." Coroner: "Then there was bad blood between deceased and Mr. Hannaford ?" Witness: "Not exactly bad blood. My father did not approve of my being engaged to Mr. William Hannaford on account of his enmity with Mr. Hannaford, senior. But there was no open quarrel." Coroner: "Have you any idea of why Mr. William H,a.nnaford called Witness: "No; I do not even know if he did call."</p>
        <p>C6roner : "According to this letter he must have called."</p>
        <p>Witness (in a low voice) : "I knew nothing of the letter at the time." Coroner: "Did you hear any voices in the filt udy ?" Witness: "No; my room is at the corner of the house. However loudly people were con- versing in the study, I could hear nothing." Coroner: "You had no idea at the time that :Mr. William was in the house ?"</p>
        <p>Witness (emphatically) : "I had no idea." ' Coroner: 'Do you think now, that he Wae there ? "</p>
        <p>With es (hesitatingl : "In the face of that letter it would seem so. But I cannot be sure."</p>
        <p>Coroner: "Why did you wire to Mr. Hanna- ford ? "</p>
        <p>Witness' : "I wished to know if he was in town ? " Coroner: "And your reason ?"</p>
        <p>Witness : "I thought that as I had mentioned to Mr. Tait about my door being fastened, and had suggested a reason, that he might suspect Mr. Hamraford."</p>
        <p>Coroner: "And you wished to prove his innocence ? " e _</p>
        <p>There wns no answer to this question. Dora looked down and flushed.</p>
        <p>Coroner: "Remember you nre on your oath, Miss Tremewa.n. Do you suspect Mr. William Hannaford of----'"</p>
        <p>Witness (interrupting hurriedly) : No, I do 'not suspect him." .</p>
        <p>Coroner: " Dor you think he was in the Eons:. on that night ? " _</p>
        <p>Witness (firmly): "I cannot aftswer that question."</p>
        <p>Coroner: "You must "</p>
        <p>Witness: "I decline tn."</p>
        <p>Teuby, supported by Tait, tried to bully the girl into making a reply. But Dora held to her decision in spite of the excitement, which was great.</p>
        <p>Suddenly there was a commotion at the lower end of the room. Leaning on his father's arm appeared Will Hannaford, ill, and with his head bandaged. "DOn't ask her any more," he said, hoarsely, "I was in the house."</p>
        <p>doing so. My son LI mnocent. .ditt he irrstattri on coming on account of Miss Tremewan, and by doing so has proved himself to be truly my Don."</p>
        <p>"Then you knew he was in the neighbour- hood ?" said Tait, rubbing his hands. "Wait a bit," said Tenby. "He may have come from London."</p>
        <p>"Not in this condition," retorted Tait, sharply. "No, Mr. Tenby ; this young gentleman has been hiding somewhere about here." "How dare you say my son has been hiding ?" cried Mr. Hannaford, savagely.. „ _</p>
        <p>"Re has every reason responded the In- spector; "but I don't wish to make things harder for you than is possible, sir. You know that your son is accused of-"</p>
        <p>" N'ot of murder,", interrupted Dora, looking up wildly; "I won't hear that he is accused of the 72urder. It is not true." _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"At all events, he is accused of having beon the last person to see your father alive, Miss Tremewan.</p>
        <p>" No," said Will in a. stronger voice, and opening his eyes which had been clociexi; "I was L 1,?&amp; the last person who saw him." Who did then, sir." "The man who killed him."</p>
        <p>There was a murmur went through the room. "Can you tell us that man's name ?" demanded the Inspector, sceptically.</p>
        <p>"I cannot. All I knOw is that I left Mr. Trp- mewan alive and well at a quarter to eleven in the night of the 15th of January. In two days I heard he was dead. I have been at Avon-</p>
        <p>"In hiding," put. in the Inspector, who evi- dently did not believe the tale.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hannaford made a gesture. of anger, but Will nodded. "You can put it that way if you like," he said, trying to pull himself together, "but I was not hiding on account of Mr. Tre- mewan's death. It was because I did not wish my father to know that I was in the neighbour- hood."</p>
        <p>"You should have gone back to London, sir," snid Tait nith a shrug.</p>
        <p>"I could not. I met with an accident, a 3 you see."</p>
        <p>"Accident !" The Inspector's _face was a study,</p>
        <p>"At all events, Mr. Hannaford did not me-eb with his hurts at the bands of my father," said Dora, looking indignantly at Tait. "You heard that the doctor said there was no struggle."</p>
        <p>"That is true enough, Miss. All the-same--" Here Tenby, who had been growing restless, interrupted the garrulous man in Ws snappy way. "We have had quite enough talk of this sort," he said, "and all this sort of thing is out of order. As Mr. William Hannaford is here he may as well give his version of what happeneri on that The Inspector nodded, and ostentatiously took out his note-book. The father saw him and scowled, but was tor.: diplomatic to say anything. He knew 'that his son was in a dangerous posi- tion, and thought. it just as well not to prejudice the authorities against him. • Whatever Will said he was bound to be arrested, OT to put it in a more polite way, detained, until the case was threshed out Dora would have spoken; but a word from Will made her hold her -peace. "Let me tell my own story," he said, settling himself in his chair. "I have no reason to conceal what I know." • _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>".We'll see about that," murmured Tait with another shrug. "Won't you have some brandy, Will ?" asked Miss Tremewan. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>, He shock his head impatiently, and clasped her hand. "Leave it there, he said, "the touch of your band will do me more good than all the spirits in the world. "Sir," he added looking at Tenby, "I am waiting to be questioned, and I promise you beforehand that I will tell you all I know."</p>
        <p>---.`"-Wait," said his father, sharply, as Tenby opened his mouth, "I wish to say a few words. My son was at Avonleigh, and wrote that he wished to see m. As I had forbidden him to come to Dartbridge I was much astonished at getting the letter. went over to him, and found that he had met with an accident. Already he .had heard of the death of my late friend Francis Tremewan. I told him that Miss Tremewan was to give evidence at the inquest, and in spite of his condition lie insisted on coming forward, I hope that his willingness to a,ppear• will weigh with the•july, and ~vith those," added Hannafori, fixing his eyes on Tait, "with those, I say, who seem t.o think him guilty of a crime which he could not possibly have committed." Several of the jury nodded sagely, to intimate that the plea of the father would .have due weight with them when they came to consider the case of the son— But Tait was still unconvinced. He closed his thin lips tightly, and his cold eyes looked colder than ever. However, •he said no- thing, but twirled the leaves of his note-book, and glanced at • the Coroner. That gentleman woke to a sense of duties, and addressed Will. "The best thiint you can do under the circum- stances, sir," said be, "is to tell us all that took place on that night. If necessary I can ask you questions afterwards." "And I am sure he will answer them all," said Dora, eagerly. .</p>
        <p>- . Will frowned. "I can't promise to answer everything," he said, "there are some things-" "If you are innocent, sir, you must make a clean breast of it," put in Mr. Inspector, in his "metallic voice. "Now, sir."</p>
        <p>"I sin innocent," replied Will hotly, "and I shall tell what I think fit. Had I been guilty, as you seem to infer, I could have stayed away, The mere fact that I am here shows that I did not kill the man." "There is some sense in that chain/ said a juror, looking round. Tenby made an imratient gesture. "The story if you plea:se," said he. Will nodded, and began to speak. At first his voice was weak, but as he warmed up it grew strong. Every now and then Dora gave his hand an affectionate squeeze which seemed to. coinfort him greatly, and to, give him courage. And this is what he related to a breathless audience.</p>
        <p>"I went up to London some sir o- seven weeks ago, and lodged in Bloomsbury. While there I wrote. to Miss Tremewan on matters which do not concern this case. Mr. Tremewan was not trit wily with me, but that was less on my own account than on my father's. You must excuse my saying this, sir," he added, looking at Mr. Han.naford, "as I must my so much to make my case clear," His father nodded. "Everyone knows that Mr Tremewan and •I were not the best of friends," he said, stiffly; "but 1 never wished him ill. Gq on." No one made .any comment on this speech, and Will, after a •pause, continued quietly. "In town a certain circumstance came to my ears which I thought it right that Mr. Tremewan should know. As he was unfriendly with me and with my father I hesitated to write to him. And, in fact, I could not very well itut it in</p>
        <p>wilting." "What was it you wished to see him about ? asked Tait, pointedly. "I cannot tell you," replied Will; "it con- cerns no one but Mr. Tremewan, and he is dead."</p>
        <p>"Had what you knew anything to do with his death ?" persisted Tait. Will paused, and bit his lip. that questicn either sir." Tait shrugged his shoulders in a deprecating way, and would have asked another question, but that Tenby, ever on the watch to snub him, shut him up. "For heaven's sake let him tell his story iv his own way._ We'll be here all day else." And with another shrug, Tait ac- quiesced, but to all appearances against the grain He made a note in his book.</p>
        <p>"I can't answer</p>
        <p>Will meanwhile proceeied. "I wrote to Mr. Tremewan and told him I wanted to see him on particular business. Yes, that is the letter," for Tenby had thrust it forward. "As you see, sir, I made an appointment for ten o'clock- on the night of the 15th of thig-Month." "A strange hour," said Tenby, looking at him keenly.</p>
        <p>"‘ Well," said Will uneasily, "you see, sir, I knew that my father would not be pleased to know that I was visiting Mr. Tremewan, so I intended to keep my visit secret." "Added to which," put in Mr. Hannaford, in his deep voice, "that for reasons which have nothing to do with the case, I told my on not to come to Dartbridge. I don't think we need go into that matter here."</p>
        <p>- Tait looked as though he thought otherwise, but Tenby replied hastily, "No ; certainly not. CTo on, Mr. William Hannaford," and Composed himself to Listen.</p>
        <p>"I therefore intended to keep my visit secret," repeated Will, "and in reply to me, Mr Tremewan showed that he understood." - -</p>
        <p>"Have you that reply ?" asked Tait, look- ing up. "No, I left it at my lodgings in London. I cap sena for it."</p>
        <p>"Thank you. Meantime please keep matter in hand." "If you will be good enough not to interrupt me," replied the young man, who found Tait irritating. "Well, gentlemen, Mr. Tremewan in his reply said that he would see me at the time I mentioned. I left London in the morning, and took a. ticket to Avonleigh, which is the next station, as you know, to Darthridge." It seemed unnecessary for Will to explain this, but the jury were so stolid that he wished to make things dear. "I got off at .Avonleigh, and waited there at the inn till nightfall. I had brought my motor bicycle with me, and came, over to Dart- bridge on that. It. was a rainy, night, if you remember, and I had anything but a pleasant ride. However, I had to keep the appointment, and I kept it. It was about a qualrter past ten o'clock when I arrived at the Gray House. "You were late," said Tenby, while Will stop- ped to draw breath. Only fifteen minutes, and that was not bad considering the state of the roads; with an ordi- nary bicycle I doubt if I could have got over at all. Mr. fremewan was expecting me. The window of his study was open. I left my machine concealed amongst the heather, and entered the house." "I saw you," said Dora, and then could have bitten off her tongue for her indiscreet exclama- tion. . "Oh, you saw him, did you ?" cried Tait with a shrewd glance "I understood yOu to say that you saw nothing." "I did not tell you what I saw," replied Dora, and would have made a still more foolish speech but tat Tenby intervened. "This is the evidence of Mr. William Hanna- ford, at present. Please do not interrupt, Miss Tremewan, and you, Inspector, be silent.' Tait gave a stiff nod, and looked at Dora with no very friendly expression of countenance. He did not like the public rebilke she had been the means of procuring for him. Will glanced at both, then continued without making any corn.. mcnt. "I don't know that there is much more to tell," he said with an effort, for he was growing weaker and his excitement was dying away. "Mr. Tre- mewan and I had a conversation and parted the best of friends. As I said before, it was a quar- ter to eleven when I .took My leave. I went out into the rainy night, and Mr. Tuesiewan shook me by the hand. I say this to show that he had no ill feeling, and that there was no reason I should have killed him. That is all I know Of the affair."</p>
        <p>"Tait sniffed incredulously. He believed that there was a good deal more to tell, and that Will was keeping the gist of the matter back. How- ever, after his late rebuke, he left Tenby to make any objection. "How did you meet with your accident ?" said Tenby, after a pause. "I fell off my machine," replied Will, "or to speak the truth, sir,, I was thrown off by running against a rope stretched across the road." "Why, what do you mean, ?" asked Tenby, 'while all looked astanishel</p>
        <p>"What I say," replied Will with an air of ex- haustion; "some distance down the main road I ran against something and was shot forward on my head. I lay on the road stunned for a time, and only came to myself when the rain began to drench me. I expect if it had been a dry night that I should have lain till the morning. At all events the rain revived me. and I got up stiff and sore. My head was aching terribly, but I had sense enongh to examine my suiroundings. I then found that there was a rope stretched across the road. It was tied on either side to gorse bushes. I think it must have been one of the ropes used by those who collect gorse to tie up their bundles, but I can't understand how it came to be stretched across the road. It, certainly was not there when I :rode to Dartbridge, as hour earlier. I expect someone put it there on purpose. I have no idea who could have done so. I -did not know that anyone saw Me go to the house, nor am I aware that I have any enemy who, would do such a thing." "How could you see all this if the night was dark and rainy ?" _ _</p>
        <p>"I lighted the lamp of my machine," replied Will, coolly; "and I was SO angry at the accident that I made a thorough examination, to as to find out how the rope came to be there." "And did you find out ? ' asked Tenby,:,rather stupidly it must he confessed. "No; how could I in so oursory an e;c‘mina- tion ? All I know is that the rope was. stretched and tied on either side of the road to the gorse bushes. It was a regular trap, and must have been laid for me. 1 cannot guess who the blackguard was. I untied the rope and fhtng it into the gorse, as I did net want anyone else to meet with an accident. My motor was quite useless, smashed and twisted. I had to walk with it, ill as I was, to • Avonleigh. There I waited till the dawn, and went to the inn. I did not wish t:o go to the hat during the night lest the folks there Might think it strange." "'And it was strange," murmered Tait, making a note, "and highly suspicious." "What happened next ?" asked the Coroner. "1 lair there ill, and the woman of the qnn nursed me." "What is the name of the inn?" The Coach and Horses." "I know it," replied Tait, scribbling the ad- dress. down. 'Well, sir, and so you remained there ill.. How did you hear of the murder?" "Information came to the people in the inn. The woman who nursed me told me that Mr. Tremewan had been murdered. I was horrified by the nelfs, and communicated with\my father that he might come and see me. I \recognised that if my visit were to become known I might be in danger of arrest. MY father came and told me that owing to what 'Miss Tremewan said. to the Inspector, I was suspected. I then de- cided to come here and give my evidence, as I knew that Miss Tremewan would try and shield me." " Ah," said Tait; "then she knew of your</p>
        <p>visit." "No sir; she did not. No one knew of my visit unless it was the man who stretched the rope across the road." 'Did you see any one on that night beside, Mr. Tremewan ?" "No, I saw no one." "What was your business with the deceased ? "I decline to tell you," replied Will firmly "it has nothing to do with you. It was a private matter." Tait smiled acidly. "In a case like this, Mr. Hannaford," he said, "all private things become public. We have your version of this meeting. How do we know that it is th-*Z true one? ",I:My -son does not tell lies, sir ?" cried Mr. Haihnaford. "Anyone will tell- lies to save his neck," re. tested the 'lnspector. "Your son admits that he was not on good terms with the deceased, ami had a meeting with him under the most suspicious circumstances." "What do von mean by suspicious ? " "Well," drawled the Inspector, "on the face of it a meeting at ten at night is suspicious. No onlv that. but Mr. William Harmaford stops al a station beyond Dartbridge so as to pay his visit seerretly. He says it was on account of his father., How are we to know that? And the . next morning the man he came to see is found ; dead. What do you make of that ?" "I admit that circumstances are against me," said Will, while his father stood dumbfounded. "All the same I am innocent. I left Mr. Tre- mewan in the best of health, and we parted good friends." "Did he expect to meet with his death ?" asked the Coroner.</p>
        <p>"On the contrary he was making plans for the future." "But what was your business with him ?" harped Tait, who was determined to know this much. "I can't tell you," replied Wilk "Do tell, darling," whispered Dora in agony. "You don't know what he may do if you don't. "I don't car 6 what he does," said Will aloud; "I won't tell. As to the rest, I plifee myself in the hands of the jury. Circumstances, I repeat, are against me. But lam innocent of this crime. Take me out father." He was growing faint, with 90 much talking, for he had lost a good, deal of blood from the wound in his head. Hanna- ford and Dora took him into the next room, where they revived him with brandy. Meanwhile • the jury deliberated.</p>
        <p>- Stever had there b' such a scene. The men Could not agree, and the Coroner could not reduce them to order. At length, after a long talk and much argument, they brought in a verdict of . ".Wilful Murder" against Will Hannaford, and he was arrested by the triumphant Tait. "Will, Will, my darling ! " cried Dora, kissing hi A pale cheek before them all; "you are inno.. , cent, and I will prove your innocence !"</p>
        <p>To be Continued</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FERGUS H UME.</head>
        <p>FERGUS H UME.</p>
        <p>Author of " The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," " The Pagan's Cup," "The Crime of the Crystal," "Woman—the Sphinx," etc.. etc., etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>AT THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>There was great excitement at Dartbridge, when Er inquest on the body of the late Francis Tre- vnewan took place. He himself was so well known, ltis death had been so mysterious and tragic, and there were rumours of such surprising develop- ments in connection with the affair that. people came froiri far and wide. It. might have been market day at Dartbridge -so crowded was the little town. Reporters came from Plymouth,and one enterprising Landon journal even sent a special correspondent to detail what promised to be as strange a case as ever happened 'outside the pages of a detective novel. The members of the Fourth Estate had quite a gala day, and were tumbling over one another in the efforts to gain the earliest intelligence for their respective journals. The one theme of conversation was the murder, and everyone speculated as to who might be the criminal. But no one could suggest any likely person. The inquest was held in the coffee room of the London Arms, and after the jury had inspected the body at the Gray House, they returned to tha hotel to take their seats. The Coroner, a bluff, stout, red-faced gentleman called Tenby, with a short temper, and an obstinate mouth, made a speech, and explained the duties of those called upon to pronounce on the matter. Dr. Pascoe was the first witness, and gave his evidence in a manner at once concise and clear. He deposed that the deceased had been strangled with a piece of window cord about half-past eleven on. the night of the 15th of January. Death must have been almost instantaneous. Few auestions were asked of this witness, as his evedence was purely tech- nical. The real interest of .the case 'began with the free and unfettered speech of Inspector Tait. He had apparently made up his mind as to the criminal, and spoke with all the assurance of an official mule. At the outset he detailed how he had been sum- moned to the Gray House by Mrs. Bedowei, and how he had found the deceased seated at the desk with the cord round his neck. • He thought that this had been thrown unexpectedly over the head of the victim, and that he had been killed before he had time to call out. or make a struggle. This statement agreed with the evidence of Pascoe as to the death being an instantaneous one. Then</p>
        <p>Tait proceeded tO. startle the Court. . "I should my," he added, "that the person who killed Mr. Tremewan was a friend." Coroner: "Why do you say that Inspect° • On what grounds " Tait: "Tremewan was seated at his desk, with his face to the window. This was open, but no one could have entered without being immediately seen by the deceased. Had a. stranger appeared. Tremewan would have risen to his feet to ask the reason of such an intrusion. As he did not—" Coroner: "One moment. Mr. Tremewan might have risen and then have sat down." • Tact: "He might have done so, certainly, but I do not. think he did. At all events 'the visitor was a friend, because if Tremewan had not trusted him he would not have let him get behind him." Coroner: "How do you know the assassin got behind Mr. Tremewan Tait: "Beca•use the doctor says that the death waa,instantaneous and without a. strroggle. My theory is that the assassin secured the cord of the curtain unbeknown to his victim, slipped behind him and threw the cord over his head before he had time to call out. Apparently Mr. Tremewan so trusted his visitor that he did not even turn his 'Lead to see what he was doing. I fancy that the assassin, to throw him, off his guard, was walking up and down the room. In fact, Mr., Tremewan may not have been on his guard at Al. Therefore, from all these circumstances, I am certain that the assassin was a trusted friend of the deceased." - Coroner: "All this is theoretical. Guess-work is not evidence." Tait (angrily) : "I am not guessing. lam deducing evidence from facts. So far as I can see, all the evidence in this case must necessarily be circurrbstantial. Mr. Tremewan had a. visitor, juid one whom he knew well." Coroner (ironically) : "Perhaps, Mr. Inspector, you can give his name?" Tait (with assurance) : "Certainly; I am Coming to debt. By this letter, sir (letter produced and handed to the Coroner), you can see that Mr. Wil- liam Hannaford made an appointment .with the deceased for 10 o'clock on the night of the 15th of January. According to Dr. Pascoe the murder was committed at half-past 11 or thereabouts." There was a sensation, and the Coroner looked rather astonished. He had not expected Tait. to be quite so Well prepared. It was certain from the letter that Hannaford had made an appointment, and all circumstances led to the supposition that it had been kept. To clinch the matter, Tait related how Dora had been fastened in her 'room so as not to interrupt the interview, how he had followed her to the Post Office and had obtained the addres of Will. "I also sent a wire, gentle- men," continued the Inspector, "but it was to Scotland Yard. According to my instructions a detective called at the. lodgings of Mr: Hannaford. He learned that Mr. Hannaford had left for Pad- dington Station to catch the morning train—ap- parently to Dartbriclg,e." "You can't be sure of that," said an intelligent juror. "Ever, train that leaves Paddington does not go to Dartbridge. Mr. Hannaford might have come down to see his father." Tait: "In that case he must have come to Dart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHAPTER X.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER X.</p>
        <p>A SURPRISING STORY.</p>
        <p>For quite a minute there was a dead silence. Everyone stared at Will as though he had horns, for certainly he was the last person expected to appear, and especially at so dramatic a moment.' He was a tall, fair, handsome young fellow, with a militazx, carriage like his father, whom indeed he g-ready resembled. At. the present moment he looked Pale and ill. His eyes had dark circics under them, and the bandage round his head was stained with blood. Even„ the. neat blue. serge suit he wore showed signs- of violence, for it was torn in several places, although the rkqits had been hastily mended. He - looked as if he had met with an accident.</p>
        <p>"Are you Mr. William Hannaford ?" asked Tenby, looking at the young man in a doubtful manner, for he was unacquainted with his ap- pearance. _</p>
        <p>- Will nodded, and slipping from his father's arm, sat down. Mr. Hannafcrd stood beside him rigidly upright, but his face was haggard, and he had an anxious expression in his eyes. How_ ever, he was too proud to show his feelings, and might have been cast in iron BO stolid did he look Dora, who had all the time been silent, scarcely able to believe her eyes, suddenly re- covered voice, and ran to kneel beside the mar, she loved. "Will, Will," she murmured taking ,his hand, "why have you come here ? " "I could not let you face this thing alone," he replied in a low voice. "When my father told</p>
        <p>"He would ame," said Haimeford Quietly. "Ts Ives not my wish."</p>
        <p>"Yu Ilia not, want him to come, sir ?" pub in Tait.</p>
        <p>The old man turned to the officer with a haughty air. "No, I did not, sir; for I know well enough that you will probably arrest him. "Deed hardly eay that I take exception to your</p>
