      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ROYAL YACHT, VICTORIA AND A1.8.T. AND HER ELEGANTLY FURNISHED _DINING-ROOM. • –</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL YACHT, VICTORIA AND A1.8.T. AND HER ELEGANTLY FURNISHED _DINING-ROOM. • -</p>
        <p>THE KING'S TRIP.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Hir Majesty left England on a cruise which is partly a pleasure trip and partly of an official nature. He sailed from Portsmouth. on the royal yacht Victoria and Albert, escorted by the cruisers Venus and Minerva; and the party consisted of some members of his ...personal suite and a few intimate friends, who included M. de Soveral, the Portuguese Minister. The cruise is expected to last a month at least, and the King will • come into contact with the Portuguese, Spanish, French and Italian authorities, if not with the rulers of each of these four countries. The first port of call is Lisbon, which should be reached on Friday, and there His Majesty will be the guest of his great personal friend, King Carlos. The visit will last several days, which will be replete with state func- tions and gorgeous entertainments, with the exception of 4'Sunday, which, at the King's express wish, will be a dies non.</p>
        <p>THE LIBERAL FOR CANBORNE.</p>
        <p>The Liberals could not have secured a more likely successor to Mr. Caipe than Sir Wilfred Lawson, and they are confitent that his defeat of the Unionist candidate, Mr. Arthur Strauss: on April Bth, is as good as established. The register, however, has undergone great changes, and the Unionists are very hopeful. Mr. Caine's majority was only ioB, and many of the miners who were then at home have returned to South Africa. But both parties have at- tended closely to registration work, with the result that the register now contains the names of 8,668 voters, as against 7,998 in 1900, an increase of 670. The Union- ists claim a ma- jority of these. Mr. Strauss was first in the field, and apparently re- cognising that in this stronghold of Nonconformity the Liberals would keep the Educa-</p>
        <p>tion Act well to the front did his best SIR WILFRED LAWSON. in his first speech</p>
        <p>• to belittle its de- fects, and declared that he would make the South African settlement his battle .cry, because of its bearing and effect upon Cornish miners who had gone back to South Africa. to resume their former labours in the mines. But Sir Wilfrid Lawson and his supporters persistently and vigorously refuse to allow the more immediate issues to be thus obscured.</p>
        <p>7 ....J.'. T(l4 ' 1 Kid, ‘k 4N) 11?- \1,1))((' 7/ ((( I) sA,II /, •</p>
