      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MISS HOLLAND'S DISAPPEARANCE.</head>
        <p>MISS HOLLAND'S DISAPPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>Up to the time of writing no trace of the whereabouts of Miss Camille Holland, who disappeared from the Moat Farm, near Saffron Walden, close on four years ago, has been found; but the police are still searching dili- gently. She was a woman of between fifty and sixty, but looked young and attractive for her age. As she is slightly eccentric in some of her ways, her disappearance did not cause her relatives active alarm until recently, when it was found that nearly all her money had been drawn out of her bank by what are supposed to have been forged cheques. Upon inquiry no sign of Miss Holland could be found, andj no news of her has come to hand since May, 1899. Meanwhile, Dougall, the man arrested on the charge of having forged her name, has been up before th e magistrates at Saffron Wal- den and has again been re- manded. He was in court on Friday, and the Treasury said that it would MISS CAMILLE HOLLAND. necessit a t e -several remands to hear the ease fully. The magistrates, therefore, after having heard the opening of the case remanded the prisoner till Thursday, refusing bail. Meanwhile public interest in the case is increasing rather than abating, and each report or scrap of news is busily snapped up. Certainly one of the most dis- cussed questions of the moment is the where- abouts or fate of Miss Holland.</p>
