      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FAMOUS OLD HOSTELRY.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS OLD HOSTELRY.</p>
        <p>Much disapproval has been expressed at the refusal of the Dorking magistrates to renew the license of the old King's Head Inn, which is generally believed to have suggested to Dickens one of the most famous of Pickwickian inns, 'y, the Marquis of Granby. There is no* is of Granby in Dorking, and Dickens is ,c 1 to have borrowed the sign f t-</p>
        <p>name: Marqi</p>
        <p>THE OLD KING'S HEAD, DORKING. IMMORTALISED BY DICKENS AS THE MARQUIS</p>
        <p>OF GRANBY " IN " PICKWICK."</p>
        <p>ham and fitted it for the purposes of'his story to the King's Head. The belief is strengthened by the fact that an article published in "All the Year Round" during Dickens' editorship of that journal stated as a fact that the King's Head was the original of the Marquis of Granby, kept by that shrewish lady, Susan, wife of Tony Weller. It was here that she entertained Mr. Stiggins ; it was here that, after her decease, Mr. Stiggins was ducked by her widower.</p>
