      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SHAMROCK 111,</head>
        <p>SHAMROCK 111,</p>
        <p>The optimism of the designers of Shamrock 111. is shared by Sir Thomas Lipton, and it is Pleasing, from a British point of view, to know that experts are hopeful that this year will see the cup triumphantly carried back to home waters, Shadirock 111. differs in two material points from her pre- decessors. The form of Sham- rock I. was the result of years of thought and ex per ie nce, while that of Shamrock 11. was • the result of numerous ex- periments made with models, in a tank especially fitted to deter- mine the com- a/Hoar SIR T. LIPTON HAS APPOINTED parative resist- TO SKIPPER SHAMROCK 111</p>
        <p>CAPfAIN 808 WRINGE,</p>
        <p>ance of moving bodi es of similar type through water. These experiments Went so far that eleven models, with sixty-four alterations, were made to arrive at the best form to drive through and over the water. Shamrock 111. is the outcome of all these elaborate investi- gations, and the well-wishers of Sir Thomas may be said to be quite justified in being sanguipe. Shamrock 111. is exceedingly lean under wafer, and, being longer than the old boat, it follows that she will glide through and over the waves much more easily, though it is not unlikely that her flat floor and wide beam. will make her appear to disturb the water—to splash it more than Columbia did 4</p>
        <p>• • - 1111MIUM (• •‘ Ita k "*"k</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LATEST CYCLING SENSATION.</head>
        <p>THE LATEST CYCLING SENSATION.</p>
        <p>r A new looping the loop performance will shortly be seen in London, Compared to which Diavolo's feat was mild and unexciting. Two mighty iron wheels, each 47 feet in circumference, with a flat rim 3 feet wide, are set on a standard of bridge iron. They suggest models of the great wheel at Earl's Court. Though each weighs nearly half a ton, they turn on ball bearings made so beautifully that if a bluebottle alights on one of the wheels his weight will turn it. Two intrepid and skilful riders mount their machines on the inside of the rims, and start pedalling. The effect is that the cyclists remain stationary, while the big wheels revolve in the opposite direction, just as the wheel the white mouse</p>
        <p>A LOOP IN WHICH THE RIDER ATTAINS A SPEED</p>
        <p>OF 120 MILES AN 11017 R</p>
        <p>turns when he takes exercise. Pedal is hard as they like, the cyclists cannot ride an inch up. the inside of the great wheels. They are not trying to, however. This is only the overture. Sud- denly they put on their brakes for an instant, and are carried backwards half-way up the sides of the big wheels. Then they release their brakes, and pedal for all they are worth. The impetus given by the descent of the steep .slope of the wheels, plus their pedalling, give them such lightning speed that they are carried up the opposite side of the revolving rim, and right round the inside of the wheels. Instead of coming off when they descend, however, they go round again. Good performers can loop this loop thirty or forty times straight off..</p>
