      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>"FIGHTING MAC'S" TRAGIC END.</head>
        <p>"FIGHTING MAC'S" TRAGIC END.</p>
        <p>The sudden tragic death of Sir Hector Mac- donald, the great Scottish General, was received throughout the country with great sorrow, as for nearly a quarter of a century he has been one of the great heroes of the nation. The day before it had bze,-1 announced in a telegram from Colombo that 'certain serious charges—namele -s, yet not criminal under the code of that island—had been brought against him, and that a desire for a consultation with Lord Roberts. on, the subject had pronwted his sudden and mysterious voyage to England. Upon is advice, after a special audience with the King himself, Sir Hector Macdonald kit England again for Colombo, via Paris and Marseilles, to face the court-martial which circninstan-</p>
        <p>ces had rendered it imperative to call. In Paris he,put up at the Hotel Re- gina in the Rue de Rivoli, and upon seeing the prema- ture disclosure of his terrible posi- tion in the news- papers, he shot himself in his bed- room. The nature of the charges, the reasons for his suicide, and all the sadder aspects of the case it would be ungenerous and improper to enter into; but perhaps it would have been a wiser thing if his friends had not raised the question ot removing the remains to his native land. The less publicity given, the sooner all gossip dies down; and the country wishes, After it has got over the painful shock oV this sudden suicide, and tile strange circumstances sur- rounding it, to remember only the great soldiet in the pride Of his success. The body was brought back to England on Saturday, and at King's. Cross for Scotland on Sunday after- 'noon in the presence of a large and sorrowing crowd,</p>
        <p>THE LATE SIR HECTOR</p>
        <p>• . - • (!fl-J</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>hI.kCDONALD</head>
        <p>hI.kCDONALD</p>
        <p>The xi-T:xanyiner) Friciay. April 3. 1903.</p>
        <p>WHAT THE NURSE SAID. Light on a L3neashire Problem.</p>
        <p>The ever-present problem of what can be done to ha- prove the position of the sick and suffering receives some elucidation in some remarks made to a" Liver- pool Mercury" reporter by Mrs. Rosa Carney, whose experience as a nurse and wide reputation among the poorer class of the sufferers to whom she has ministered lend to her utterances a degree of interest and import- ance which cannot be easily exaggerated. . _ .</p>
        <p>1- If publicity can be given to; certain hints given to , our representative by Mrs. Carney, many readers who have not had the opportunity of receiving her experi- ence at first hand may be benefited by them. Speaking at her house, 88, Albert Edward-road, Liverpool, Mrs. Carney said :—"I have myself sometimes had spells of illness and it is owing to the use of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people that I pulled through a very severe attack of influenza recently. 'I was always a strong woman, and not liable to be ! depressed by the signs of illness," continued Mrs. Car- ney; "But there was no doubt of the seriousness of the form of influenza that took me. For six weeks I lay in bed, and my life was in danger. In the course of my work Iliad seen the good effects of Dr. Williams' pink pills, and I determined to give them a trial. They put ' new strength into my system, and under their influence I soon found that my strength, which had been sapped by the fever of influenza, was coining back. I kept on taking invariably pills, for my experience shows that influ- enza leaves what is scientifically called sequelw or an after complaint of one _ kind or another. But, thanks to Dr. Williams' pink pills, nothing of this I kind occurred in my case, and I regained my normal vigour and activity without a check. The hint is worth having, for it is more in what it leaves behind it than in its own immediate symptoms that, the danger of I influenza conaists."</p>
        <p>Perceiving in her own case the wonderful strengthen- ing power of Dr. Williams' pink pills, Mrs. Carney again tried them during another recent period of in- different health. "I suffered from bad headaches, and wasn't able to go about my work as industriously as usual," she continued. "So I tried Dr. Williams' pink pills again purely as a tonic, and they proved more than satisfactory. In a very brief space of time I got rid of the troublesome headaches, and now my confidence in. the curative qualities of Dr. Williams' pink pills is so great that I invariably recom- mend them to people suffering from the kinds of Illness that seized me. Indeed, I have found from what these people have afterwards said that they have worked wonders in all kinds of cases in which the blood has needed purifying. For bracing up the system to fight colds, and for banishing indigestion and kindred com- plaints, I consider there is no better remedy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Presentation of South African and Long Service Medals at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Exceptional services rendered at home and abroad by members of the Ist Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South isuicashire Regiment, were fittingly recognized on Mon- day evening, when Captain Fairclough and the men who constituted the third South African contingent were the recipients of the usual medals granted by the Govern- ment, and several volunteers who have been connected with the battalion for the period which entitles them to the long service medal were presented with that decora- tion. The ceremony took rgace at the Drill Hall in the presence °Ca good muster of volunteers and members of the genet-al public. Colonel J. C. Ridgway, V.D.. who recently resigned the command of the battalion, made the presentation. It was thought appropriate that he should discharge the duty, as he was in command of the bAttalion when the members of the third contingent volunteered and were accepted for active service. Colonel J. D. Buckton, who is in temporary command of the battalion, presided and a number of officers were in attendance.</p>
        <p>Colonel BUCKTON delivered a brief speech and said the South African medals would remind the wearers of their experiences during the campaign. The long service medal was a decoration to which every recruit might aspire. To obtain one a volunteer had to serve a long time but if he did his duty and served his king b. nd country long and faithfully he too might some day cCnie forward to receive the long service medal. As the men who were to receive medals that night had served under Colonel Ridgway's command he (the speaker) thought he could not do better than ask the Colonel to make the presentation. He thought the active service man would like to the receive medals from the hands of the officer who was in command of the battalion when they volun- teered for active service and when they returned.</p>
        <p>Colonel RIDGWAY' said he was very pleased to come amongst them once more and to see such a good mus- ter. Probably many of those present were recruits who had joined the battalion this year. If they were, he could only say that they were a very good lot of men. He Nvishoa them all success in their service in the battalion. It gave him very great pleasure to present the medals to Captain Fairclough and the men of the third contingent, because they went out to the Cape whenthe battalion was under his command. He (Colonel Ridgway) hardly thought he should be leaving the battalion so soon but he did not feel that his health was equal to carrying on the duties and therefore he thought a younger man ought to take his place. He trusted that in future they would have an officer in command with whom they could work as thoroughly and comfort- ably as they had worked with him (the speaker). It had always given him great pleasure to be amongst them in camp, They had had many happy times together and he had never found them unwilling to perform the duties allotted to them. They had always tried to do their duty and he had tried to do.] the same in pulling them through the various movements. With regard to the South African co ntingents,they would have the satis- faction of knowing that they had shown what the Englishmen could do when the country required their services. They left comfortable homes and situations at a time of great danger and their action was entirely voluntary. They had hard times, but he was happy to say that nearly all of them returned safely. Of the third contingent, two had lost their lives, i Private Richardson died n South Africa and Private Littlewood passed away after his return home. Colonel Ridgway referred to the fact that a collection was taken from the Volunteers with'the . object of placing a memorial in the Parish Church to tke men in the Ist South Lancashire Regiment who had fallen in South Africa. Perhaps they would wonder why nothing further had been done in the matter. A sum of money had been collected, but owing to the Ist South Lancashire Regi- ment being abroad it had rather delayed matters. The money collected from the volunteer had been handed over to the officer who had charge of the memorial scheme, and sooner or later they would hear something about it. It would be gratifying to see that the volun- teers were remembered with the line battalions, and he was onlysorry that the memorial had not ',en erected before he left the battalion.</p>
        <p>Alluding- to the veterans, the Colonel said they had earned the medals for their 20 years' service. There might be some who felt like himself that they had volunteered long enough. if they had done so there was an opportunity of joining the veteran company,and if at any time they were called upon to protect this country it would be the duty of those who were in that company to take charge of all in that drill shed. They had lately heard it said that it was very necessary they should have a larger force of volunteers prepared to defend the country in case of invasion. That was so, but there was another way of looking at the matter,and that was that they had come forward voluntarily to learn their drill and to be prepared to assist in the defence of the country when needed, but it was only right that something more should be done—that all young men should be expeoted to pass through the ranks of the Volunteers or Militia. There would be no hard- ship in it, and it would be a good thing for the country and for those who took part in the work. The medals were then distributed, the first recipient being Captain Fairclough. The other members of the South African contingent who received the decorations were :—Sergeant C. J. Thornley, Corporals E. Sewell, T. Green, J. R. Oxley,A. Rogers; Drummer J. Bramhall Privates C. Blackburn, J. Bird, J.Burrows, E. Clarke, -A. J. Crozier, T. Davies, T. H. Dutton, M. Foster, S. Johnson, J. Lawton, J. Lee, G. Lewis, W. Mc.Whirter W. Morgan, 11. B. Morris, R. Osborne, H. Roughley, R' Thomas, M. Walsh, J. Woodheatd, J. F. Wade, and J. Whelan.</p>
        <p>Colonel Ridgway announced that the relatives of the late Privates Richardson and Littlewood could have the medals which the deceased volunteers should have re ceived on applying at the orderly room. The volunteers who received long service medals were: Sergeant Taylor,Lance-Sergeantsßennett,and Hampson, Lance-Corporal Wright and Privates Massey and Dodd. After the distribution the colours of the battalion were brought into the room and shown to the inen. The volunteers presented arms and the band played the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Lifeboat Saturday Demonstration.</head>
        <p>Lifeboat Saturday Demonstration.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held in the Town Hall, on Monday evening, it Was decided to hold a Lifeboat Saturday Demonstration in Warrington on Saturday, the 20th of June. In the absence of the Mayor, Mr. Councillor John Roberts presided. He stated that it was now seven years since Warrington had sent a contribution to the -funds and proceeded to sptrak upon the noble services rendered by the lifeboat men. After some discussion it was decided to hold a demonstration on the 20th June, and a large and influential committee was appointed to make- the necessary arrangements. :Mr. Edwin Bradshaw, • chartered accountant, Was appointed hon, treasurer, and Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), hon. secretary. The Committee meet on Monday evening. A ladies' committee will shortly be formed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henley is the first great poet to write upon the joys of motoring. His poem, a long one, entitled "A song of speed," will appear in the forthcoming number of "The World's Work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL,</head>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL,</p>
        <p>" Crown !ng the Temperanze Queen "</p>
        <p>at Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>In enlisting the children in the cause of temperance the Warrington Band of Hope Union is carrying on a praiseworthy wark, and it was gratifying to see from the crowded attendance at the festival held in Parr Hall on Thursday last week that public interest in the work is not lacking. The spacious hall was filled in every part and the efforts of the children upon whom the task of entertaining the assembly mainly devolved met with the most cordial appreciation. The festival afforded evi- dence of the vitality of the Band of Hope movement in Warrington, over 209 children taking part in the pro- ceedings. Mrs. Henry Roberts presided and after the singing of the hymn "-All people that on earth do dwell" • and the offering of prayer by Mr. Sherburne, delivered• a brief address. She said it gave- her very great pleasure to see so large a gathering, more especi- ally as the members of the audience were present to lend their support to the temperance cause. The Qbjeet of the festival was the encouragement of the United Bands of Hope and to provide means for them to carry out their work. As it was some time since a similar festival was held some of her hearers might ask what the United Bands of Hope had been doing. She would remind them that the summer before last the Union undertook an extensive canvass of the • town in support of the Children's Bill and did their little best to help that measure through Parliament. During last spring a very successful meeting was held in that hall in support of what was then known as the Government Liceasin. Bill, but what happily was now the Licensing . Act. The Band of Hope Union took a very large part in the work she had mentioned. During Coronation week a very successful temperance gala was held and she thought the children as well as the adults who took part in the proceedings enjoyed themselves. She was sure they would agree with her that some good • temperance work had been done inWarrington by the Union. (Applause.) The entertainment consisted oil a cantata entitled "Crowning the Temperance Queen," which was admir- ably given. There.wasa chorus of 200 voices, under the able conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce Mr. F. Podmore, presiding at the organ with his usual skill. The most had been made of the opportunities for pic- turesque effects which the cantata affords, and the chil- dren and their instructors alike are to be congratulated upon the complete success attained. The platform had been brightly decorated, and in the centre of the gallery was a small throne. The theme of the cantata is easily told. The reign of the Temperance Queen is drawing to a close and her eubjects gather to render her thanks, and witness the Coronation of her saccessor. At the outseta herald enters, and in an appropriate song extols the virtues of the retiring queen, and tells the audience of the happy object of the assembly. Then enters the retiring queen attended by guards, maids of honour, and train bearers, all in pleasing costumes. Love, Peace, Industry and Thrift walk by her side. 'l:he Queen having been escorted to the throne the Queen elect enters, her attendants being Faith, Hope; Pleasure and Mercy. Sorrow, Want, and Cruelty seek to gain admission to the Court. but meet with a stern rebuff, being told that "their King is Bacchus, whoSe strong band with blood of millions slain, is red." _ _ _</p>
        <p>The-crown was removed from the head of the retiring queen and replaced by the well-won wreath of bay. After the chorus "0, arise!" there was an interlude during which an excellent musical programme was sub- mitted. A pianoforte solo was well executed by Miss Fanny Leicester. Miss Carrie Hastings, a promis- ing vocalist with a sweet voice, sang "The swallows" in exceedingly creditable style and was rewarded with a hearty encore. An action recitation "The shoeblacks" by Masters Charles. Joseph and Arthur Nurse was a very amusing item. Miss J. Broom-, head was responsible for a mandoline solo "Spring-- song," and Miss Elsie Spruce sang "The children's home" with good effect. Master T. Barton, Cert. R.A.M. gavel), very enjoyable violin solo, and the recitation "The foolish maiden" by Miss Edith Cole created considerable merriment and called forth hearty applause. - The girls of Padgate Band of Hope went through a, pleasing scarf drill. The cantata was then resumed, the retiring Queen after delivering a neat little speech vacatihg the throne. At this stage Fear and Injured Innocence who have been forced to fly from home seekadmission to the Cour.; and are heartily welcomed. The invitation song " Come, 0 Come "having been rendered the Queen elect was con- ducted to the throne with due ceremony, crowned and invested with the sceptre, and welcomed in song and speech. Afterwards there was anothei interlude for miscellaneous items. A pianoforte solo "Evening Bells" was played by Miss Lena Duckworth and Miss Carrie Hastings had thehonour of asecond recall for her effec- tive interpretation of "-The Voice of the Father," res- ponding with" Home Sweet Home." An interesting feature was the dialogue "Like Mother" by five girls of Buckley-street Band of Hope. Miss I. Broomhead and Miss Ashbrook contribute a mandoline and violin duet " Caroletta polka" and Miss Elsie Spruce sang "Children asleep" in good style. Master Barton pleased the audience with another violin solo, and an item which was decidedly popular was the song " The Tramp," by Master lkfcCollam. So well did heficquit himself that a vigmous encore was accorded. ThNigirls of Padgate Band of Hope gave a pretty tam- bourine drill which was well received. The last stage of the cantata was now entered upon. The new Queen addressed her subjects and homage was paid and fealty sworn to" her Majesty" by the retiring Queen, the attendants, and Fear and Innocence. The battle song with chorus " Then awake" was rendered. Book prizes were then presented to the ,following members in recognition of their work in selling a large number of tickets for the festival :—G. A. Whitfield, John Wardman, Barbara Oldroyd, Ada Furnival, and May Parry. After closing proclamations by the heralds, who enjoined their hearers to " Come and lend your aid till the world be sober made," a highly inter- esting and enjoyable entertainment was brought to a close with the singing of the National Anthem. - A mead of praise must be accorded to the children for the manner in which they sustained the parts allotted to them. The Queens conducted themselves with becoming grace and dignity, and the other per- formers who have bean alluded to acquitted themselves, both as regarded singing and elocution, in a manner which left little to be desired.</p>
        <p>The characters were sustained by the following : Retiring Queen,tHettie Burgess, Hill Cliffe and Stock-; ton Heath; Queen Elect, Barbara Oldroyd, St. James' First Herald, Harry Griffiths, Wycliffe; Second Herald, Stanley Jones, Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath; Page Boy, Frank Egerton, Friars' Green; Doorkeeper, George Arthur Whitfield, Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath •' Love, Polly Hackett, Cockhedge Mission; Peace, Minnie Whitmore. Ellesmere-street; Industry, Lilly Kendrick. Bank Quay Mission; Thrift, Dorothy Lawton, Sankey Bridges; Faith, Elsie Wright, Brick-street Mission; Hope, Lilian Hall, Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath ; Pleasure, Clara Mason, Howley-lane ; Mercy, Nellie Allen, Brick-street Mission: Maids of Honour, Alice Dumbell, Ellesmere-street, Lucy Hill, Howley-lane, Ruby Witts, Wycliffe, Polly Thompson, Bank Quay, Ada Furnival, Brick-street Mission, Jessie Ellison, Hill Cliffe an Stockton Heath, Emily Sankey, Howley-lane, Alice F .Ikes, St. James', Edith Jones,St. :James', Maggie Shaw, Howley-lane, Nellie Mather, Ebenezer, Nellie Ruddyard, Sankey Bridges, Eva Hor- rocks, Bold-street, Jane Harrison, Buckley-street, May Parry, Bold-street, Polly Kinder, Sankey Bridges, Alice Dagnall, St. ,lames': Train Bearers, Hilda Spruce, Legh-street, Eva Howard, Elles- mere-street, May Isherwood, Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath, Emma Pricey, Sankey Bridges; Captain, Albert Spruce, Legh street ; Guards of Honour Leo Walton, Bold street William Pacey, Sankey Bridges, Joha Hewitt, Buckley street, • William Wilde, Sankey Bridges, Harry Colly, Wycliffe, John Wardman, Silver street, Albert Bailey, Hill Cliffe andmaStockton Heath, Stanley Adams, Buckley street, Jam ff Corbett. Cock- hedge Mission, Beale Wakefield, Bold street, Walter Martin, Silver street, James Little, Legh siireet ; Sorrow and Want, William Dennett, Friars Green-; Cruelty, Alfred Taylor,Bank Quay Mission; Fear, Charles Roylance, Bank Quay Mission; Injured Innocence, Emma Kinder, Sankey Bridges; The arrangements had been carried out by the fol- lowing committee :—Messrs Thomas Dale, F- W. Ross, G, Whitfield, and Mesdames Whitmore and Davenport. Mr. F. Gandy, was -n energetic hon secretary and the duties'of hon assistant secretary were discharged by Mr. Henry Woolley.</p>
        <p>REMARKABLE CURE IN LIVERP,OOL.—Tn another col- umn of this issue Will be found 'full particulars of a most remarkable recovery of a Liverpool woman, and the cure is attributed to Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. The affair has created a lot of talk in the neighbourhood. The pills are praised by all classes of people, from the lowest to the highest, for the way in which they have cured paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas,consumption of the bowels and lungs,anmmia, pale and sallow complexion, generalmuscular weakness, loss of appetite, plapitationg, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness and hysteria. These pills are a tonic not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are sold by chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Com- pany, 46, Holborn-viaduct,London,E.C.. at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s 9d. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars a.Te not Dr. Williams' pink pills ; accept them only in the pink closed wrappers as above described.</p>
        <p>Shamrock ITT. had another trial on Wednesday with Shamrock T. and behaved extremely well under iull sail in a twenty knot breeze. The first actual trial race be- tween the iwo yachts will take place nt Weymouth next Monday.</p>
        <p>A Poplar coal porter and his•wife decided to (lie to- gether, and took poison. The woman died. but the man lived, and at the Old Bailey on Wednesday he was sen- tenced to death for the murder of his wife.</p>
        <p>As the result of a toy pistol accident at Ponkey, near Ruabon, an Oldham youth named Howard died on Wed- nesday. The bullet had entered the head near the - eye and could not be extracted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LOCAL FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>LOCAL FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE appearance of that unique little "Diary and (3om- L panion "issued by GBO. 0. BUSSBY &amp; CO.. for the toe of Cricketers (who:lh may be obtainea from Sports ealers, Booksellers. or railway dookstalls for 64), reminds us of the coating season in tnticipation of which Geo. G. EUSSEY Sc Co.. !sive preptred a " reaord " stock of Bats. We re told that the timber that they hold for Rat ma clog aloes excee is 1 CO) tons • Glorious Cricket follows the dritisu Flag everywhere." (1).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Warrington were at practically fall strength on S ttardty for Cie return match with aunslet, the latter team also being well represented. W. Goldthorpe was the•chief absentee. Boisterous weather again prevailed, a high wind blowing from goal to goal. There were at)out 5,09 spectators and Warrington won the toss. The teams and officials were :</p>
        <p>Warrington: Hallam, back ; Fish, Isherwood,Dicken- son. and Harris, thre-quartar backs ; Edmunds and, Duckworth. half backs ; Morrison, Boardman, Harmer' - F. Swift, Jolley, Luat, Forster, and Coleman, forwards. Hunslet : Place, back; O'Brien, Evarson, H. White- I ey, and J. Whit ?,1 ey, thraa-quarter backs; A. Goldthorpe and Burnett, half backs ; Walsh, Brooke, Ramage,Will- cox Tanningley, Wray, Shooter, and Wilson, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. H. Crompton, Bradford. Touch judges, Messrs. E. Gordon, Widnes, and G. Robinson, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>wilbert Goldthorpe kievel oS against the wind and Jolley failed to reply. A free tick to Warrington was very well replied ta, but the next minute the ball went, through the hands of Edmunds. Duckworth, and Isher- wood,to Fish, who kicked over Place's head, and follow- ing up scored within a couple of minutes of the start. Dickenson failed at goal. After this good start play for a while was contested at centre, when Harris, following up a kick by Edmunds got possession and romped over, Dickenson again failing at goal. Six points in four minutes was good business for Warrington. Duck- worth kicked over the full-back's head for Harris to follow up, but the ball just rolled in touch before the wing man caught it, otherwise he had no one to stop him. The game continued in Warrington's favour, but Hunslet began to make full use of touch, and this caused the play to slow down considerably. Eventually good passing took Hunslet near Warrington line. Burnett dashed over, but. Goldthorpe failed at goal. The Warrington forwards were badly beaten, and but rarely did the ball come out at their side. Half-time: Warrington six points, Hunslet three points. Dickenson restarted, and play was fairly even, the ball coming out on the Warrington side rather more fre- quently against the wind than before. Hunslet could not make much headway. Duckworth soon came into prominence with a clever run from the scrummage and pass to Dicke.nson. The latter sprinted powerfully with Harris in !attendance, and nearing the he was doubled in and scored under the goal. Fish added the extra points. Long kicking soon took Hunslet within striking distance of the home line. Goldthorpe vainly tried to get hts backs, going and at last dropped the usual goal\ Warrington responded with great dash and brilliant pass- ing between Duckworth, Dickenson, Isherwood.and Fish ended in a try by the latter,aftera run half the length of the field. Hunslet again attacked, and Goldthorpe droppeda second goal. Final.—Warrington 2 goals 4 tr\ies (16 points). Hunslet 2 goals 1 try (7 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The return m'eettini-Orthe—io-i;;M-s-t-OOIZ place aPOld- hun,before 4,030 spectators. Frater started, and his side were early on called to defend their lines, but nothing of moment occurred in the first quarter,in which Runcorn had the best of the exchanges. From this stage up to half time Oldham more than held their own, and Civil made three efforts to dash over at the corner, .but failed. Cash eventually managed to score at the corner, and Oldham led at the interval by 3 points to nil. From the resumption the visitors were penned in their own half, and could not cross the centre line., Ferguson made two ineffectual drops at goal, and thel forwards tried to dribble over, but the defence proved quite safe. The game was very tame and uninteresting throughout. Result :—Oldham 1 try (3 points), Run - earn nil. Teams :</p>
        <p>Oldham—R. L. Thomas, back ;11. Spottiswoode, Tyson, P. Thomas, and C. Civil, threequarter backs ; A. Les and J. R. Lawton. half backs; Frater, Ferguson, G. Wilkinson, J. H. Wilkinson Owens, \Towles, Ellis, and Topttm, forwards. Runcorn—Price, back; Moran, Hesketh, Butterwor th, and Jones, threequarter backs; Jolley and Richardson, half backs; Walker' Reece, Gayter, A. Richardson, Washburn, Moulton. Woods, and W. Houghton, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Kennedy, Broughton Rangers. Touch judges Messrs. P. Taylor, L3igh, and T. L. Brimelow, Salford. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>BRADFORD v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Very little interest was taken in the meeting of these teams, the attendance at Park Avenue being poor, Playinc, with the wind strongly in their favour ,the Bradford team were in the first half able to make them- selves pretty well safe from defeat. At the outset the visitors pressed, bat afterwards play was all in Bradford's favour. The first try was obtained by Robertson, after a pretty boat of passing between him, Cooper, and Moiby. Thalist-ntmed failed to land the goal. From a line forward rush Turner addedian unimproved try,and just before ,half-thn_e Cooper dropped a goal. _</p>
        <p>- Bradford defended against the 'Wind iii most of the second half. Widnes, however; showed up very poorly, and although they did a considerable amount of attack- ing they failed to make use of their opportunities, and in the end Bradford won by 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points) to Widnes nil. Teams : Bradford.—Eagers. back; Mosby, Heseltine, Dechan, and Cooper, three-quarter backs; Marsden and Foster, half-backs; Broadley, F. Smales, Grayson, Lewis, Wright, Turner, Robertson, and Dunn, forwards. Widnes.—Tilley, bank; Hughes, Taylor; Whaley, and Peacock, three-quarter Igr.--ks; Douglas and H. Taylor, half-backs; Aspey, Lynch, Leyshon, McDonald, Riley, Rigby, Power, and Webster, forwards. Referee, Mr. R. H. Moore (Oldham). Touch judges, Messrs. T. F. Wilkinson (Leeds) and G. W. Gibson (Keighley).</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. Itlayed at St. Helens. The Rangers' thrAe-quarters got on the move, but Foulkes managed to kick into touch. St. Helens came to the half-way line, and Willie James got away, but he was well-tackled by Barnes. Sam James tries to find an opening for Barrett, but Liveraedge tackled him smartly. Willie James found an opening for Wilson, who scored. W. James kicked a goal. Hogg scored another try, James adding another goal. W. James again gave the ball to Wilson, who got over at the corner, but this time James failed at goal. Wilson kicked down, and, securing the ball twain, romped over under the posts, W. James kicking another goal. Half-time score --Broughton, 3 goals and 4 tries ; St. Helens, nil.—On resuming, St. Helens at once took play to the Rangers' line, and Smith scrambled over, but the try was not allowed. Once more St. Helens got to the visitors' line, but Wilson relieved. Jones, the new St. Helens three-quarter, had a beautiful opening, but he passed when he might have dashed over. Briers scored for St. Helens, but no goal was kicked. Result :—Broughton Rangers, 3 goals and 4 tries (18 points) ; St. Helens, 1 try (3 po;nts).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>HALIFAX v. SWINTON,</head>
        <p>HALIFAX v. SWINTON,</p>
        <p>At Halifax. Halifax, two goals and three tries (13 points) ; Swinton, nothing. '</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Salford. Salford, one goal and four tries (14 points) ; Leigh, nothing.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. WIGAN. At Huddersfield. Huddersfield, one try (three points) ; Wigan, one goal (two points.)</p>
        <p>HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>At Hull. Hull Kingston Rovers, three goals and five tries (21 points) ; Brighouse Rangers, one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>BATLEY v. HULL.</p>
        <p>At Batley. Batley, two goals and two tries (10 points); Hull, one try (three points.)</p>
        <p>OLDHAM v. ST. HELENS. On Tuesday at Watersheddings. Oldham 2 goals, 3 tries, 13 points; St. Helens 2 goals, 4 points.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. HUNSLET. Played at Huddersfield on Wednesday. Huddersfield 1 goal 2 tries; Hunskt</p>
        <p>Legh street Primitive Methodist Sunday School. On Tuesday evening last the final Band of Hope entertainment of the season was given in. the above school. Mr. J. T..llowlands presided, over a crowded ' audience, and delivered a capital address, advising the children to be staunch teetotalers. Mr. E. Hairs also spoke. Miss Broomhead then commenced the concert ; by a well played mandoline solo'. Miss Carrie Hastings the brilliant girl soprano then sang " The Swallows " for which the audience gave a viciferous encore later ! contributing " The Voic3 of the Father 7 and was equally successful in the effort. _Miss Elsie Taylor a violinist of great promise, ren- dered with brilliance and dharm two capital : violin solos, each being heartily appreciated. I Mr. Walter Ford delighted the audience with two Icleverly played clarionet solos" Kell Row "and "The 'Adieux, being encored for each item. A number of prettily attired girls from Padg,ate Who have been care fully trained by Miss Wood contributed two Pleasing I drills,scarf and tambourine, being enccOd for the last I exceedingly pretty item. Miss Ethel Taylor and Mr. Fred Podmore were the accompanists. Recitations were given by Miss Ed th Shaw and Master Harold Mairs. A dialogue was given by five little girls from Buckley-street entitled" Like Mother " and was an aecaptable item. Mr. E. Mairs moved a vote of thanks to all the performers who had so kindly come to assist, and a special vote was given to Mr. Henry Billingham., the energetic secretary, who has for so I long a time carried on the Band of Hope at Legh-st, einglehanded. The singing of the Doxology brought this most successful entertainment to a close. k</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"Johnson always keeps his word." "Why shouldn't he? No one will take it."</p>
        <p>TIME improves everything but women; they, of course, have been perfect from the beginning. CLARA : "What a pretty bonnet you have on." Irene: "Yes, but I'm over my ears in debt for i t. " _ _</p>
        <p>"WHAT is the proper way of addressing the Admiral of the Fleet ?" "'Your warship,' of course."</p>
        <p>SHE : "He says he loves me: yet he has only known me two days." Her Friend: "Well, per- haps, that's the reason, dear." PERDITA "Was it hard for him to propose?" DoroLhy : "It might have been. But you know I was with him." Ltrruz GIRL (to visitor) : "Don't you think I look just like mamma ?" Her Mother: "Hush, dear; don't be vain." _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>" I couldn't sleep last night. I was forced to get up and walk about." Insomnia, eh ?" " Yes ; the baby had it."</p>
        <p>A 3IAN never realises how much valuable advice his neighbours have to give away until he announces his intention to build a house.</p>
        <p>"ARE they smooth Boers—the fellows who are fighting in South Africa ? " "Yes. But the papers say that many of them are being rifled." LITTLE CHARLEY: "Papa, what is broadminded- ness ? " His Dad: "Agreeing with headstrong people when you know they are wrong." Ma. OLDBOY : "I am a self-made man, sir. I began life as a barefoot boy." Kennard: "Well, I wasn't born with shoes on, either." "LITTLE JOHNNY HENPECK: "I don't think Santa Claus is married." Mr. Henpeck: "Why not?" Littly Johnny Henpeck: "If he was, Mrs. Santa Claus wouldn't have let him stay out all night." "ARE you fond of music?" "Awfully." "Do you ever go to the music halls?" "Never." "Oh! May I ask why?" "For that reason." DocToß : "Did your visit to the seaside have the desired effect, madam ?" Mrs. Fullpurse "Oh, yes, doctor, both my daughters are married."</p>
        <p>BAcpx : "I suppose, like most women, your wife wants the earth?" Egbert : "Well, yes; but I have learnt that she doesn't want it on her best carpet." His HONOUR: "You are charged with stealing chickens. Have you any witnesses?" Prisoner:, "I have not. I don't steal chickens before wit- nesses."</p>
        <p>LANDLADY: "You'll have to pay in advance, sir. You are a stranger." New Boarder: ".oh, that's all right, ma'am! I'm an easy fellow to get acquainted with." MISTRESS "Mary, I was almost sure once last evening while the policeman was in the kitchen that I heard a sound very much like two people kissing." Mary: "Did you hear it only wanst, mum ? ' Mistress: "Yes." Mary: "Then it wasn't us." No Hunnr.—.-She : "I will send your presents back to-morrow." He "No hurry. I don't expect to be engaged _again for a week or so.' IN SELF-DxFENcx.—Piano Manufacturer (hotly): "Why didn't you show off that piano, instead of making such horrible noises on it?" Assistant (apologetically): "Those ladies live next door to me, and I was afraid they'd buy." NOT SURPRISINO.—" Wriggs was just complain- ing to me that he finds it almost impossible to get any subscriptions to that hospital he's interested " l'm not surprised. The idiot announced publicly that the list would not be published." - " BILKINS got married rather suddenly, didn't he?" "-Yes. Somebody gave him a railway pass to Bournemouth for two, and he didn't want to waste it."</p>
        <p>"How can you," they said to the departing soldiers, "be so joyful when you are going into deadly strife?" "But," they replied, are we not simply in transports?" HE: Nice dog—very! Have you taught it any new tricks since I was here last ? " She, (sweetly) : " Yes ; it will fetch your hat if you whistle. .</p>
        <p>THE ACME. —Mistress : • "We are to have distinguished visitors this evening, cook. I wish you to prepare the dinner with as much care as if —as if—" Cook: "Yes, ma'am." Mistress : "As if your sweetheart were to have a birth- day."</p>
        <p>MRS. GOODE : "You are the sixth man who has asked mt for something to eat to-day." The Tramp (sadly): "I s'pose so. If the competition in our line gits any wuss, some of us'll have to go to work."</p>
        <p>AccoIINTING FOR IT.—" Bridget, how did it happen that whep we came in last night after the theatre there was a policeman in the kitchen ? " "Sure, mum, Oi don't know; but Oi think the theatre didn't last as long as usual." HYDE PARK ORATOR; "My friends, if we were each of us to turn and look ourselves squarely in the face, what should Nire each find we needed most ?" A Voice from the Crowd: "An india- rubber neck, mister!" MUCH ADO. Growell (in cheap restaurant): "Here, waiter, are these mutton or pork chops?" Waiter: "Can't you tell by the taste ? " "No." "Then what difference does it make which they are?" "You'll. become used to the motion of the yacht soon, dearest," said the young husband, soothingly. "It's your home, you know, for the next month." "Then what ails me, I suppose," replied the wan bride, trying to smile, "is home- sickness." -</p>
        <p>DRIVER: One of my horses is very queer this morning, sir." Mr. Nleek; "Indeed! "Which one ? " Driver: "The off 'un." Mr. Meek: "The orphan? Ah ! perhaps the poor animal is thinking of its parents "This is my son Frederick, Mr. Fosdick," said Mr. Glanders, proudly, introducing his five-year- old boy to his caller. "Well, Frederick," said the caller, "do you obey your mamma?" "Yes, sir," replied Frederick, promptly; "and so does papa." LIVERYMAN "I am afraid, sir, I must ask ru to pay in advance for the hire of the horse." Amateur Rider: "What's that for? Are you afraid that I shall come back without the horse?" Liveryman: "Oh, no, sir! But the horse might come back without you." • OLD LADY: "I want a watch that won't tick so loud." Clerk: "They all tick like this, ma'am; •there's no other kind." Old Lady: ". Oh! I know better. I've heard of them silent watches of the night' ever since I kin remember." JUDGE: "Have you anything to say before sen- tence is passed ? " "Prisoner: "Nothin', only I wouldn't be here if it wasn't for ignorance of the law." .Judge: "Your ignorance of the law is no excuse." Prisoner: " 'Tain't my ignorance I'm talking about. It's yburs." LITTLE GIRL: "Please, ma'am, Johnny Smart is making mistakes in his writing lesson." Teacher: "How do you know ? " Little Girl: "There's three capital S's in the copy to-day, and he's makin' L's." Teacher: "You can't see his pen." Little Girl: " No'm, but I can see his tongue." "Is that a new rug your mother has, Bobbie?" asked Mrs. Plumer of the youthful Bings. "No," said Bobbie; "that's one she brought in from the other room. But," excitedly, "you mustn't take it up." • "Why not ? " "'Cause there's a hole in the carpet." FIRST LADY: "I saw your husband meet you in the street yesterday, and I noticed that he removed his hat while speaking to you. I admired him fer that. Very few men do that." Second Lady: "I remember. I told him in the morning to have his hair cut, and he was showing me that he had obeyed." HE WAS ONE.—Noah Tall: "What's the matter? You look savage." E. Z: Mark: "I feel savage. I bought a pet monkey for my boy yesterday, and the beast died this morning. The dealer simply swindled me." Noah Tall: "Ha ! You should have consulted • somebody before buying it. Next time you want a monkey, send for me." • ACKNOWLEDGED.—The death of a beloved wife in a Virginia (U.S.A.) town was touchingly re- ferred to by the bereaved widower, who, in his local paper, inserted the following "card of thanks" to his friendly neighbours: "I wish to express my most heartfelt thanks to the minister, choir, neighbours, sisters and brothers of the various orders, and all those who so kindly assisted in the sickness and loss of my wife."</p>
      </div>
