      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>TIL B. Government THE GOVERNMENT'S cannot be kept from BUTTERING OF interfering w ith IRELAND. trade. It is always endeavouring in one • way or another to protect the public from itself: for somebody's private advantage. On 'Tuesday it procured the second reading of a Bill, the primary object of which is to raise the price of butter. Incidentally it is another part of the. policy of killing Home Rule by kindness. There was a previone.'Bill for compelling dealers in butter imitations to dye their commodity blue or brown or green or some other intimidating colour. What is now proposed is that all butter contain- ing more than 16 per cont.of water shall be treated as adulterated. An exception is to be made in favour of Ireland. Irish butter is very frequently salted to excess, but the object of putting too much salt in butter is to attract moisture and make the butter gain in weight. It is a rather shabby trick played on the butter consumer. Why, then, if the Bill is a good one should it not apply to butter produced in Ireland ?</p>
        <p>But that the Bill is not a good one—that it has singularly few merits and a great many faults was proved with much conclusiveness by Mr. HUDSON KEARLEY, the able member for Davonport, who may be credited with very thorough knowledge of his subject. The Bill, as Mr. KEARLEY contends, is not honestly described. It is professedly a Bill to protect the honest producer and trader in the interests of the 2onsurner, and it is really a Bill to legalise adulterated butter made by ancient and faulty and often by far from cleanly methods. There is no uniformity in the measure. It gives all sorts of extraordinary exemptions, as Mr. KEARLEY pointed out, and these exemptions are detritneptal to the interests of the country and of the consumer. The most extraordinary exemption of all is that of Ireland. We import L'20,000,0D0 worth of butter annually, and the whole of this is to be subject to a test established by the Bill except that portion of it which comes from Ireland. Now a rigid examination of Irish butter would probably be greatly beneficial to- Irish agriculture. The Irish are admirable butter-makers, and yet much of the butter which comes from Irelanct cannot be used for purely domestic purpses. Great quantities of it, as we have said, are loaded with salt. An actual layer of salt is generally placed between each churning of the butter, which is packed in crocks. Various other devices have been from time to time immemorial employed to increase the weight • A horsehoe has before now been found comfortably stowed away in the middle of a fine pound lump of Irish butter. This is a downright dishonesty, of course, but the original offender cannot be traced on account of the peculiar conditions of the Irish butter trade. Irish butter is in far too many cases full of hairs from the cow, and so otherwise excellent article is spoiled for the table The quality of other Irish butter is destroyed by the use of bog water in the dairies. This 18 what Mr. KEARNPS means when he speaks of ancient and faulty methods. It would cer- tainly be to the advantage of Ireland to change her methods of butter-making at once; ana yet the Government proposes to place Iler under a most-favoured nation clause by grant' ng her exemption under a measure which, it admitted, will kill the trade in the entirely close and wholesale milk flavoured butters. But the more serious aspect of the WI is it</p>
      </div>
