      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>interference with an important branch of trade and its tendency to increase the great army of 'spies iind inspectors. Under the present Adminis- tration we are drifting into the condition of some Continental nations, where at least one out of four persons met in the streets wears a uniform. Are We to have a buresuoratic England as there is a bureaucratic France and a bureaucratic Germany? , This is what will come to pass if the present " Government remains much longer in power. And the objects aimed at are all appallingly trivial. To She examples. There is the keeping out of prison- made brushes, the trade in which articles of corn- Tneroe amounts to a few hundred pounds a year; and then there is the Merchandise Marks Bill, Which has been of great advantage to the foreign Producer, but has deprived us of no inconsiderable share of our re-export trade. Now we are to strike a blow at a quite wholesome article of • (I(lrsestic consumption, exempting Ireland in vir • tttle of the new comp Act between the Irish rnem- uers and the Government. Where, it may well be asked, ig this kind of thing to StOp ?</p>
        <p>BY a majority of</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE ON eighty, the Housq 4UNICIPAL TRADING. of Commons on</p>
        <p>Wednesday resolved to appoint a Select Committee to join with a of Lords Committee which is to consider mid report on the Powers that ought to be be- Stowed on local authorities in regard to municiphl -enterprise. Mr. JOHN Buntai and those who share his views are probably right in supposing that the ehiect of appointing this committee is hostile to 'what is called municipal trading. The axiom that ' truth was never worsted in an honest inquiry has flo application in this case. Truth may suffer con- siderable injury through a prejudiced inquiry; and though Mr. BALFOUR may be sincere enough in believing the motion adopted by the House mi Wednesday to be of a quite impartial Character, there is good reason for fearing ' that the projected inquiry has been devised with the hope that it may result in recommends- ti mu; that the present powers • of municipalities ' shall be restricted. There was nevettheless a -great deal of force as well as subtlety in some of -the PRIME MINISTER'S arguments. Could anyone -lay, MR. BALFOUR inquired, that in the near future gas is going to retain its position as a source of light, heat, and power, or that fixed lines of tram- 'mays will be the permanent form by which transit le towns will be facilitated? Certainly, nobody 'With knowledge and judgment will commit himself to so limited a view of the possibilities that are immediately before us. Precisely. MR. .1/ALFOUR'S difficulty must have presented itself to • Most of us. Some municipalities have ',committed tbemselves,at the moment to enormous expenditure °la electric tramway systems. That is the typical 'instance. May not tramways become an obsolete 'Method of locomotion in a few yettrs' time? The Motor car may have come into general use, So iieh improved as to meet with universal public ;▪ PPrnval. The municipalities would in that case, the PRIME MINISTER points out, be placed in a •t-,,• c)st awkward dilemma. They would either have c°niess that they had made an unwise expendi- -I:'re ef the ratepayers' money or they would con- lice to use methods and inventions which were t4i"sedly behind the times. But how is Pardin.int committee of the two houses of 11,k- larnent to help the municipalities against inx like these? It can see no further into the tire than the rest of us. It has no special Ineanb'of determining whether gas will shortly be superseded as an illuminant. It cannot declare on any grounds of certainty that the motor ear will before long become something much more important than a costly and dangerous toy. There is no special capacity for direction in such matters in a joint committee of the two Houses. It can l'ec•oinmend that matters shall be left as they are, er that on the grounds of such philosophic doubts la those of Mr. BALFOUR the municipalities lilould not be permitted to take risks. This would !tit an end, should Parliament come to the same :inclusion as the cornmittee,ta what Mr. BALFO Ira v ;nits to be a new,important, and interesting de-. °l'ment of social life. Such committees must pae:l,earily be suspect in their origin, elareent being constituted as it is with e°Mmercial and legal interests greatly :'reh 0 r°l3clerant. The appointment of the bartlin, ittee is in reality an incident in the it„„ le Which is being fought out between the local trrnorities and certain great trading and indus- fal concerns. The municipalities must either 1"0 the hands of gas, water,electricity supply, ZI tramway monopolists or they mat take r. litters into their own hands, together with such ISke as those which Mr. BALFOUR indicates. Som nf the▪ great towns and cities are already tied hard to the monopolists. Their condition is 13'1(4 to be envied. They can only free themselves .2 a vastly greater expenditure than would he 7quired for simply providing themselves with uleans of lighting, water supply and locomotion. by should Parliament interfere to tie them 1111:' still .more lightly? Why should it inter- re at all? Surely the right policy is • let the municipalities work out thek own 4 detiny. The local authorities understand the needs and circumstances. They act under "iirect responsibility to the ratepayers. If they '4.tni_ttice mistakes those who bear the loss have shared blame. We may be en the eve of great dis- !everies which may alter mest things, but we can -In no way be certain on the point. The invention lei wireless telegraphy may teach us to think any- to be possible, but who on that ground :onld advise municipalities to hand themselves .4ver to the monopolists, placing fetters on thern- elves that might indeed be broken by some nn- ,lsected discovery or invention, but which would be far more likely to remain?</p>
        <p>The terrible accident which has occurred in tbe, V!? mobile race at Nice has a certain connection 1‘" the forthcoming contest in Ireland for the ~ °rune-Bennett Cup. Count Zborowski, who was ;118 of the victims, was well known in this country, was looked upon as a sure competitor in the 'l?sh race. In fact, only last January, in company w!tb Mr. S. F. Edge and Mr. Charles Jarrott, the i i°lenere of recent important events, he was n irelend inspecting the course proposed. It WRS alen who made. only a month ago, the 1,-"Rizestion that the three cars which are I° represent Great Britain shall ha painted .411 emerald green, out of compliment to ; e country which is lending its roads for the iistee. It iS understood that the Irish course been inspected with the utmost care, and has declared to he a thoroughly safe ,one. No after the deplorable affair at Nice, full tire- urtione will he taken. and in particular. accurate 13.74.,5_Wi1l he made shewing. for the benefit of corn- 'tin's all turnings which may he considered 2. be in the leesti degree dangerous to cars going st tremendous speed. It is net enough that ante-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN DOCKS.</p>
        <p>Work will be suspended at Runcorn Docks and Bridgewater Wharves on Good Friday and Easter Mon- day. Craft will howeyer be able to pass through the canals and docks as usual. A SUNDAY SCHOOL VETERAN.</p>
        <p>In a current issue of a well known Sunday paper appear portraits of four veterans in Christian work who have been awarded the paper's distinguished service medal. The first portrait is that of Mr. J. W. Bazley, a Sunday school official and local preacher for upwards of 43 years at Runcorn. OVERTON HILLS, MERSEY VIEW. 'The above popular pleasure grounds, of which Mrs. Parker Hoose is tho proprietress, will re-open on Good Friday and Easter Monday. As in former years there will be the usual attractions in the form of swings, bowling, shooting galleries, &amp;c. On both days the War- a,ington Borough brass band will play for dancing until r late hour. For fuller particulars see advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SUCCESS OF A RUNCORA POST OFFICE OFFICIAL.</head>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A RUNCORA POST OFFICE OFFICIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Smith, son of the late Mr. John Smith, Parker-street, Runcorn, has been appointed Postmaster of De Aar, South Africa. He started in the service of the Post Office at Runcorn as a telegraph lad. He was appointed to the Halton Post Office, and subsequently went as a telegraph clerk to Birkenhead. From there he proceeded to South Africa as a clerk, and after long and faithful service has well earned his important pro- motion.</p>
        <p>TNTERWORKS FOOTRIALI. COMPETITION. On Saturd.ty evening the Alum Works met W. H. Moore and Co. in the above competition. In the first half the Alum contingent kicked two goals and in the second half landed another goal thus winning by six points to nil.—On Wednesday evening the Co-operative Society met the Manure No. 7 team on the Runcorn football ground. The game was a very stubborn one and ended in a pointless draw, the Co-operative men haying slightly the best of the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>DRUNKEN WIDNES HOME.</head>
        <p>DRUNKEN WIDNES HOME.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. Brown and Mr. T. Cook, at Widnes, on Thursday, John Reynold, described as a hard-working man, but too fond of his beer, residing in Warrington- road, was charged with neglecting his children, Joseph fifteen; Edward, twelve; James, tea; Mary, six; Paul thi-ee; and Margaret, one, on divers days. Mr. Knowles prosecuted on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, and enlightened the bench on the state of the shocking home caused by drink. He was a hard-working man, but he would not provide for his children proper maintenance. The home was destitute of furniture, without even a piece of crockery, and not even a bite of food.—Sergeant Rat- cliffe, Police-constable Greenhalgh, Inspector Ho bden, and Police-constable Cummins all testified in strong terms to the prisoner's conduct.—The Chairman said not only had he neglected his home, but it had come out in the evit%ence that he had abused his wife by giving her a cat nose and two black eyes. He had been up five times before for similar offences, and was this time sent to gaol for three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>LECTURE BY MR. R. D. TIMMINS.</head>
        <p>LECTURE BY MR. R. D. TIMMINS.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Engineering and Scientific Association of Ireland Mr. R. D. Timmins, electrical engineer at Messrs. Guinness's, son of Mr. Walter Timmins, of Trinity House, Runcorn read a paper (illustrated by views) on " Well and Runcorn, boring, with note on water divining." In the course of an interesting paper he dealt with the many difficulties experienced in boring and the various devices used in the work. He showed many of the tools used, and explained temperatures, strata, &amp;c. Referring to the use of the "divining rod" in the discovery of water, he said it was a" V" shaped hazel, measuring from 12 to 24 inches in length, and about a quarter of an inch in diameter. The rod was held with the point down- wards in the hands of a person possessing the power who walked over the ground where water was required and the place was indicated by the rod turning upwards in his hands. Personally he did not care to place too much faith in the "diviner," but on a few occa- sions he had seen successful demonstrations of the powers of these persons. He went on to describe'the boring of a well for the supply of water to Gainsborough a work which was carried out by Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons, of Runcorn. It was commenced in 1895, and on April Ist, 1897, an unfortunate accident caused the boring tool, weighing 45 cwt., and 500 feet of rope, weighing a ton and a half, to be left in the borehole. After 22 months' labour these were recovered, and the well successfully carried to a depth of 1,515 feet, and now an ample supply of water was obtained. A discus- sion followed the reading of the paper, and a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Timmins. PRESENTATION TO P.S. GREGORY AT RUNCORN. • -</p>
        <p>. An interesting event took place at the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn, on Monday evening. This was the presentationto P.S. Gregory, of Altrincham, formerly of Runcorn, of a handsome drawing-room timepiece. a pair of bronzes, and a purse of gold. ' The clock bore a silver plate, on which was inscribed " Presented to P.S. Gregory, together with a purse of gold, by his many Runcorn frienis in recognition of the courtesy and ability with which he fulfilled his difficult duties during the eleven years he was stationed at Runcorn. March, 190.3."—Councillor T. Dean presided, and amongst a numerous company were P.S. Gill, Captain H. Hill, junr., J. Ellis, J. W. Heath, Messrs. R. Briscoe, H. Grundy, A. Grounds, F.Hancock,T.Bate, D. Littlernore, C. Watson.—Councillor Dean made the presentation, and expressed the hope that the clock would always tell Sergeant Gregory the correct time, and remind him of his many old friends in Runcorn. Might he live long and enjoy , every success. (Applause).- -Captain Ellis also made some appropriate remarks.— P. S. Gregory, who was cordially greeted said he cune to Runcorn a raw recruit'. He had previottsly been a gardener for Colonel Cotton Joddrell at .Nantwich and he entered the police force with the intention of im- proving his position in life. His aim had always been to act uprightly and straightforwardly and that his efforts were successful was testified to by the fact that at the end of six months service he was made detective at Runcorn and that position with the exception of a few months he had occupied until he went to Altrincham. He had always endeavoured to help the law abiding people of the town and to bring to justice those who made a practice of preying upon the general public in\ 'a variety of ways. The fact that he came back to Run- corn under such pleasant circumstances that evening showed that hehad won the good opinion of those whose good opinion was worth having. He heartily thanked Mr. Dean and those associated with him for the good feelings which had prompted thorn to make him the splendid testimonial. He should ever regard the clock and bronzes as the most prized of his possessions and he would hand them down to his family with pride.— Votes of thanks concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WILFIN, DAMAGE TO AN EARLESTOWN HOTEL.</head>
        <p>WILFIN, DAMAGE TO AN EARLESTOWN HOTEL.</p>
        <p>Richard Ince was charged at Earlestown with doing wilful damage to the Royal Hotel, Cross-lane. Earl's- town. Mr. Charles .Owen, licensee of the Royal Hotel, stated that prisoner c tme into his house on Saturday and went round begging. When he was refused he said he would get something and—picked up a stone and threw it at the vestibule door.but not breaking the win- dow. Witness put him out, but prisoner would not, go away and threatened to break another window if they sent for a policeman. Eventually he was taken into custody. ,A fine of 5s and costs was inflicted and pri- soner was ordered to make good the damage or go to prison for 14 days' hard labour. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Ad”*.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>mobilists should be left to find out these for them- selves in the preliminary spins which probably al of them will make. A serious accident in Ireland is not to be risked if any foresight can prevent it. Apart from the loss of life and the suffering that may ensue, the committee are bound to consider the great shock which would be caused to public opinion in these isles. It is not too much to say that a catastrophe similar to that at Nice would postpone for years any possibility of the amend- ments of the Locomotives on Highways Act, on which the leaders in the automobile world would have set their hearts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By ,1 CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>It was a great day in Runcorn on Saturday. Despite the bleak weather there was a good mustering of towns- folk to see the opening of the Victoria Memorial Infirmary—better known as the Runcorn Cottage Hos- pital. It is pleasing to think that the ideal, which has been before the people's vision for so long a time, has at length been realized. Unhappily a small debt of some £4OO remains on the building—which should be cleared before handing it over to the Urban Council. If the workingmen only join lands with a long and a strong pull the building will speedily be free from debt.</p>
        <p>The Medical o%cer's report in another column is well worth reading. It is a masterly piece of composition and deals with the tramp and infant mortality questions very thoroughly indeed. The only pity is that it is to be feared the caustic remarks made by the doctor about the verminous tramp will not be read by the persons in question. They may, however, be productive of some benefit especially if other medical officers adopt the same attitude. The tramp problem is becoming more important every day and it is high time• legislative measures were initiated preventing the roaming of disease-eaten vagrants.</p>
        <p>The contests in Heath Ward and Bridgewater Ward, where Messrs. Meadowcroft and Paynter are aspirants for civic honours, proceed in a very lackadaisical manner. The fact is there is comparatively little public spirit in the town, and ratepayers do not care who repre- sent them so long as expenses are kept down..</p>
        <p>The amalgamation proceedings are steadily going forward. As can be judged Halton and Weston are decidedly averse to join Runcorn. It, will be a good thing for the town if the County Council agree to the Urban Council's overtures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above - authority was held on Wednesday morning in the Connell Chamber, Mr. S. Taylor presiding. Present :—Messrs. W. Hand- ley, D. Bisbrown, J. H. Salkeld; J J. Shaw, M. Sweet, man, J. Wilson, E. Imison, J. Pritchard, J. W. Light- burn, E. Gatley, T. Dean, S. Walton, R. H. Posnett, F'. Wiswall, with Messrs. F. H. Lake (law clerk),. E. Marshall (secretary), Dr. McDougall (medical officer), and J. Wilding (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE TOWN</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE TOWN</p>
        <p>It was reported that the deaths for the past month numbered 21 and the births 50. For the corresponding month last year the deaths were 18 and the births 41. TRE MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee's minutes which were of a formal character were moved by Mr. T. Dean who eulogised the report of the medical officer,which appears in another column. Tho tramp question was a burning one and he was pleased that pr. McDougall had alluded to the matter. There would not have been smallpox in the town had it not been for the tramps who came in the town. He was glad Dr. McDougall had taken the initiative in directing public attention and hoped other medical officers would do likewise.</p>
        <p>Ak ART AT THE TF.CHNICAL INSTITUTE. 1111 Technical Instruction Committee recommended - that application be made to the Board of Education for the occasional use of the present School of Art rooms when not required for Art instruction, for other educa- tional purposes. Thecommittee also recommended that application be made to the Board of Education request- ing them to nay the Science and Art Scholarship grants direct to the Council instead of to the parents of the holders, the Council to deduct the amount of school fees and then to hand the balance to the holders' parents. The minutes were approved on the motion of Messrs. Wilson and Dean. ' A LIBRARY QUESTION. •. _ _</p>
        <p>circular was read from the Library Association ask- ing the ,Council to present a petition to Parliament in favour of the Public Libraries Bill 1903,which propbses to remove the existing statutory limit of the library rate to one penny in the pound. Mr Bisbrown, chairman of the library committee. thought the letter ought to lie on the table. It had been before his Committee and as the library was in an efllicient condition, they had, no need to petition for increased rating powers. Mr. Wiswall was of the same opinion. Mr. Imison was of opinion that the coinnuaication should be forwarded to the Library- Co:nmittee with a request to consider the matter and report. .The Council had aright to expect to be guided by the Library Com- mittee in Such an important matter. ,He moved that theletter be sent to the committee. Mr. Handley seconded. After considerable discussion Mr. Imison's resolution was lost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the annual meeting be held on Thursday morning the 16th April at 10.30. . MEMBERS' ATTENDANCES.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Posnett it was agreed that a record of attendances of the members of the Librat y and Technical Committees be tabulated and laid before the special meeting of the Council which is usually held before the annual meeting. Mr. Posnett state:l thit there wer6 inlay tnem')ers .who had not attended the committees and that the Council in making their selections for the committee year should have the information to guide them in making the selections.</p>
        <p>AN EDUCATIE*I QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A communication was received from the Board of Education asking the Council to state whether they intended to appoint an Eilucation .Committee under Section 2 of the Act 1902, or whether they intended letting the powers lapse. The communication, on the motion of Mr. was referred to the Technical Instruction Committee. THE EXTENSION OF RUNCORN'S ROUNDARIEi.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Halton Parish Council held on Tuesday evening, Mr. Povey in the chair, a letter was read from Mr. E. Marshall, with reference to the exten- sion of Runcorn's boundaries and asking 'the Parish Council to meet the committee of the Runcorn Urban Council who have the matter in hand. On the motion of Mr. Stokes, the Council went into committeerand at the conclusion it was announced that the Parish Council were ready to meet and confer with the committee or the Urban Council but the decision would be submitted to a meeting of ratepayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BYA CORRESPONDENT].</p>
        <p>The fatal assault case which was dealt with on Mon- day by the magistrates raised an unwarrantable amount of excitement in Earlestown, which was intensified at the funeral. The police were ill-advised in not clearing the Cemetery of the disorderly rabble (chiefly women) who congregated there, with no other purpose than to annoy the"relatives of the deceased woman. Their dis- graceful behaviour proved that they had not attended out of sympathy for the dead, but to vent their feelings upon the living. What earthly good such conduct could achieve it is impossible to conceive, and it, is a pity those who indulged in the practices had not the good sense to consider the sacred spot they were congrega- ted in.</p>
        <p>Although the Education Bill has now come into operation, it will not be adopted in the Newton district for some little time yet, and it is hoped the Committee will not be chosen haphazard, but with full considera- tion of the abilities of each proposed member for the position. Efforts are being made, by amalgamating with Haydock, to. make the Newton district an educa- tional area under the Act, but whether this will benefit the ratepayers ornot I cannot. say. The Council will no doubt act for the best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FOOTBALi,</head>
        <p>FOOTBALi,</p>
        <p>Earlestown had a bad time of it on Saturday, but they were placed in an unfortunate position by their players. They had chosen a strong representative team but at the last moment, three of their best players, Limond,tiough- ton and Williams failed to out in anappearnice. Sub- stitutes had to be found, and even then they had to take the field with only ten men. Consequently they never had a ghost of a chance and the score at the finish of the game was 5 goals in favour of Workington to Earles- tOwnill.Had the team which was originally selected taken the field, it is quite probable Earlestown would have secured a couple of points. Workington have yet to visit Earlestown to replay an abandoned fixture, when the result will peobably be reversed.</p>
        <p>Earlegtown have no match for to-morrow, but they still have several games to play to complete their League fixtures. These will doubtless have to be played bithe evening, but it is hoped the finances will not still&amp; in consequence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Confirmation Service at Wargrave.</head>
        <p>Confirmation Service at Wargrave.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the Right Rev. Dr. Clmvasse Lord Bishop of Liverpool,held aconfirmation service at Wargrave Church, at which 36 candidates presented thomselves. There was a large congregation present, who took considerable interest in the proceedings. Prior to the principal ceremony His Lordship delivered an earnest address setting forth the obligations about to be undertaken by the candidates. After the laying on of hands, the Bishop- gave an inspimting address in which he exhorted all those who had been admitted to the church to carefully observe the promises they had made before God.</p>
        <p>Count Zborowski and his chauffeur were killed in the course of a motor-ear race between Nice and La Turbie on Wednesday. While going at fIS miles an hour their car a new one, crashed into rocks at a turning in the road, both the occupants being thrown out and the car wreaked. The Count was a well-known motor-racing man, and had been over the course before.</p>
        <p>TT. M. AstrroN's LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY•STREET, WfiningtOn.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Examiner Friday, April. 3, 1903. RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Friday, April. 3, 1903. RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Board was held on Tuesday, Mr. Pritchard in the chair.—Present Messrs. E. Imison, J. Littler, S. Taylor, R. H. Posnett, W. Handley, W. Lightburn (clerk), A. Lightburn (assistant clerk). .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>A. NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>A. NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>On the motion of.Alessrs liaison and Littler the Rev. Father Hugh Don Levi was made a member of the Board in place of the Rev. J. Chambers who lately resigned.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the school management committee held February. 17 were read and confirmed. The committee consisting of Messrs. Pritchard, Handley and Imison appointed by the Board to deal with the correspondence from the Board of Education with reference to the extensions of the Victoria-road boys and girls school and the pupil teachers central class, reported to the school management as follows : "That an intimation from the Cheshire County Council has been received informing the Board thatthe first day of July next has been fixed as the day on which the Education Act of 1902 conies into operation in this area. that the Board of Education after an interview with the Clerk still refuses to approve of the alterations to the Victoria-road Schools having for the object increased accommodation therein; that no boys pre- paring for Standard I be transferred from the .Victoria- road boys' school to the Mill.. Brow infant school in order to relieve the overcrowding; that in view of the near approach of the Board's duties being trans- ferred,to the County Council no alteration in the room now occupied by the ptipil teachers' centre be recommended. Recommended that Mr. A. E. Parr be recognised as first assistant in the Victoria-road Boys' School, vice Mr. J. Brown resigned. That Mr. H. Bowker be appointed assistant master at the Victoria- road Boys' School. Duties and salaries to commence on the Ist April the appointment subject to one month's notice on either side. A letter was read from Mr. Connell resigning his position as assistant master at the Victoria-road Boys' School. Recommended that the salary of Margaret Hill and Margaret Hillige be in- creased.</p>
        <p>MR. LIGHTBURN'S SCRVICES.</p>
        <p>----- The following reference to the Clerk was unanimously recommended to be entered on the minutes "The Run- corn School Board desire to place on record the appreciation of the value of Mr. Lightbura's work since he became the Clerk five years ago. His ,life long experience of educational administration together with his shrewd far, seeing business- qualities ha,v.e made his tenure of office remarkable in many way§ especially for the better attendance of children, which had resulted in considerable educational and financial benefit 'to all concerned. They also desire to record that it was their intention to make substantial addition to his salary had they re- mained in office but as the education Act; of 1902 had transferred this however to the County Council, they with confidence recommend its re-adjustment to their successors who will reap the fruit of his experience and knowledge of educational work with the locality. There has been no change in Mr. Lightburn's salary since his appointment although the work has very con- siderably increased." _</p>
        <p>Mr. 1-fa,ndley, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, spoke of the good work ,p,e complistied by the local teachers centre, At the recent examinations, out of ten connected with the centre only one had failed,.</p>
        <p>while five teachers from other schools not connected had failed to pass the examiaations.—ln a lengthy and lucid speech, Mr. Handley eulogised the work of the Clerk in connection with the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Imison and the Chairmln also spoke highly of Mr. Lightburn.'s work, to which encomiums he suitably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HEALTH OF RUNCORN. Dr. McDougall's Annual Report. Suggestions Re the Tramp Question.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF RUNCORN. Dr. McDougall's Annual Report. Suggestions Re the Tramp Question.</p>
        <p>Dr. Alcaaugall,Medica,l Officer of Health to the Run- corn Urban Council has issued his annual report upon the health of the district for the year ending December 31st 1902. It is a voluminous reportAwell written, bristling with interesting facts and solid information. Space forbids our giving more than the following ex- tract.</p>
        <p>NOTIFICATION OF INFECTIOUS DISEASE</p>
        <p>Two hundred and sixty-nine notifications were re- caiyed during the year, notifying two hundred and sixty-seven cases': of preventible disease, as compared with one hundred and eighty-four notifications received during the preceding year, or an increase of eighty-five cases, and for the year 1900, ninety-seven notifications were received or an increase on that year of one hun- dred and seventy-two cases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MORTALITY RETI.TRIsTS.</head>
        <p>MORTALITY RETI.TRIsTS.</p>
        <p>Two hundred a-tici eighty-nine deaths from all causes were register:ld, having; occurred within the author- ity's sanitary district during the year, and for the year 1901 298 deaths were registered, .a decrease of nine. For the year 1900 351 deaths were registered. ,The infant death roll, although somewhat high, is represented this year by only 77 deaths of one year and under, a highly satisfactory return, ,and a decrease on the previous year of 32, and for once does not headOte list. Forty-seven deaths of one year and under •41Ve were registered. Twenty-seven deaths were registered in the same class during the preceding year.. Eighteen deaths of children of five and under 15 were registered, and .in the same class thirteen deaths were registered during the preced- ing year. Thirteen deaths of young persons of 15 and under 25 were registered, and in the corresponding class 16 .deaths were registered during the preceding year. Eighty-one deaths of persons of 25 years and under 65 were registered, and in the same class 79 deaths were registered during the preceding year, or an increase of two, but a decrease of 41 on the year 1900. Fifty-three deaths of 65 years and upwards were registered during the year.as compared with 53 deaths registered in the same class during the preceding year. Twenty-three inquests were held daring the year: seven of these _deaths were due to accidents, and 16 deaths were due to other. causes.</p>
        <p>THE PROFESSIONAL TRAMP. HOW TO DEAL W ITH HIM.</p>
        <p>The nomadic character of the professional tramp is proverbial, and little fault could. be board with this propensity but for the danger to the com- munity in general. The, tramp is very much averse to three things, viz., cleanliness, labour in any form, and restriction ou his liberty. He shuns the tramp ward of the workhouse, because he meets here with:two difficulties that are very repugnant to his feelings. He has to take his dip into the prover- bialjmutton broth bath,and for thehospitality of the work- house he has to perform the usuill task before he regains his liberty again. And therefore it is not a surprise that he wends his way towards the common lodging-houses of the townior locality,whichhe honours for the time with his presence. . There are no restrictions imposed on the visitor to most common lodging-houses; he has to pay the bed hire before he passes the turnstile to the dormitory, and when he. -gets up in the morning he is free to go his way and carry with him the vileness of the tramp generally, and possibly carry with him, to spread as he goes along, diseases that most communities are making supreme efforts. at great sacrifices to stamp out. This danger will continua until time is found to teach these " gentry " better manners. They are all travellers of a kind. The traveller with the long hair stiffers most on his/journeys, for heappears to be always living under suspicion, and usually the astute lodging- house landlady gives him a wide berth. He has invari- ably a large company travelling with him, and his fond- ness for their society forbids the possibility of shorten- ing the hair. The skin is covered with a crustaceous de- posit of many years duration and in appearance very .much resembling a travelling guano heap, and this state of things continues until an obliging official at some tramp ward insists or4the mutton broth bath once more. I have in the discharge of some public duty to visit frequently several common lodging-houses, and I have had to go more often recently, on account of the spread of a very loathsome disease, chiefly by the tramping fraternity. Local authorities have very little power over the lodging-houses, and they have none over the tramp, who invariably assumes an indignant and very defiant air if his honour and personal cleanliness are called in question. There is no possible hope of mend- ing matters until such time as a paternal Government will get in the humour to pass an Act of Parliament municipalising common lodging-houses and conferring upon municipal authorities very drastic powers in deal- ing with these very treacherous marauders, who are, if nothing else, a nuisance and adanger to every commun- ity. Surely every municipality . may be trusted with the powers at present exercised hylloards of Guardians. If the tramn \was compelled to wash and clean his skin, I like most civilized beings, and toperform a labour task for the hospitality he would receive, ittwould in a large measure put an end to the vagrancy , which is now se rife', and in any case, if we muistLwe such rovers going about, it would be preferable to have to deal with a clean tramp than a dirty one. The town of Runcorn has suffered more than once from the incursions of the lazy and dissolute tramp, and 'we had a recent example of this kind that might have proved very disastrous. Fresh legislation is absolutely necessary if the gentle- men with the long "hair " are to be taught to live any- thing approaching decent lives, and cease to be a nui- sance to themselves and a grave danger to every com- munity. REPORTS OF THE SCRVEYOR AND INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Wilding, surveyor, gives a satisfactory report under the Factory and Workshops Act,l9ol. The report of Mr. J. Percival, inspector of nuisances, is accom- panied by. a memorandum on disinfection. He cilis attention to imperfect disinfection in some cases that is inseparable from the iiermissive character of the general Act of Parliameht, and over which the local authority had no vetoing power. Mr. Percival also calk', attention to the reckless disregard of ordinary precau- tions in cases of scarlatina and other infectious diseases by some parents.</p>
        <p>PITDLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANCASIIIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>UNDER YOUR FOOT you want good Boots. Wear ALP TAYLOR'S well-known Boots.--L-Advt.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S MET TONM is the RING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STRF.ET,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>RUNCORN VICTORIA MEMORIAL INFIRMARY. Opening Ceremony.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN VICTORIA MEMORIAL INFIRMARY. Opening Ceremony.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Runcorn Victoria Memor- ial Hospital was opened by Mr. W. J. Crossley of Man- chester. There was a large attendance. Needless to say the opening ceremony—being the culmination of many hopes and fears—appealed to all the townsfolk who in the past have contributed so hand- somely towards the trection of the building. It is interesting to know that the " Cbttage Hospital question" first showed its head at a meeting of the Improvement Conimissioners (Dr. Steele in the chair) when a letter was read from the Liverpool Coroner urging the local authority to erect such a build- ing. The Coroner wrote owing to an accident having happened to a workman who .had seen conveyed to the Royal Infirmary where he died. Practically nothing was done in the matter until the Cycle Club grappled with the question. Parade after parade was held sums of £126, £33, and £34 etc. being raised. • At a later date a meeting of those interested in the proposal from the inception was convened by Mr. F. Wiswall, the then chairman of the Urban District Council, and at this gathering an intimation was re- ceived from Mr. H. Congreve that he would contribute £lOO towards the fund,, and the Ship Canal Company would provide £5O per annum towards the maintenance expenses for three years, on the understanding that Al5O per annum was guaranteed from other sources. Other offers announced at this meeting were:—Mr.' S. Taylor, £100; Mr. F. J. Norman, £5O; Mr. R. H.Posnett (on behalf of his firm), £2s;_ Mr. A. R. Norman, £2o' • Mr. F. A. Lake, £10; Dr. Steele, £10; Miss H. C. Bickham, £5. The meeting resolved that the sum of at least £l,OOO be raised for the establishment of a hospi- tal by public contribution, that on its establishment the Urban District Council should be asked to take it over, and to appoint a committee, consisting partly of non-members of the Council to manage it. The follow- ing gentlemen were appointed a Provisional Committee to deal with the matter :—Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, M.A. (vicar), Messrs. S. Taylor, F. J. Norman, A. R. Norman, W. Timmins,E. Imison,Dr. McDougall, Messrs. H. Congreve, P. R. Allen, R. H.Posnett, F. Wiswall, W. E. Dudley, H. Wright, James Frith, and W. Booth. Subscriptions and promises finally made the amount total to £2,009. The building and laying out of the grounds cost £1,500 and the land £5OO. The furnishing will cost some £3OO which with extras will make a deficit of some £4OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DESCRIPTION; OF THE HOSPETXL.</head>
        <p>DESCRIPTION; OF THE HOSPETXL.</p>
        <p>The hospital is situated in one of the pleasantest parts of higher Runcorn, and has a direct south frontage. ,The foundation is rock. It is an old Cheshire style of building on approved cottage hospital lines. The build- ing is so laid out that it may be extended oil the pavilion principle, and the intention is that the present premises will ultimately become the administrative block. There is ample land for extension. The whole is two or three quarter acres in extent. Not a little ingenuity has been exercised in the interior building to provide for the entire control of the hospital by three people. It was imperative that the building should be of .such character as to be easily managed by a small staff, and the difficulties which this condition presented have been most admirably overcome. Special attention has also been paid to the efficient lighting of the building, and here again a distinct suc- cess has been achieved. On the ground floor there is a women's ward, a men's ward (each of four bed accom- modation), a children's ward, an operating room (con- taining the latest form bf surgical sink), a bath room, a ward kitchen, and a day room and hall for convales- cents. The rooms are heated with Musgrave's heating and ventilating, stoves. On the staircase wall within view of the ground and ftrstfloors is a general communi- cation indicator. The first floor contains the matron's room, kitchen, scullery, bathroom and lavatory. In the roof storey are two excellent bedrooms. There is an independent system of watersupply for each floor. The whole building is on strict cottage hospital lines and is undoubtedly an ideal example of architectual and build- ing craft. Thehospital has been erected from the plans of Mr. J. Wilding, surveyor and water engineer to the Urban Council. Ile has acted as honorary architect,and deserves both thanks and congratulation upon the result of his work. The contractor was Mr. William Nickson, and the sub-contractor, Mr. R. Collinge.</p>
        <p>THE OPEI•IING. • The weather on Saturday was rather boisterous but at half-past three the streets presented a cpowded appearance when the public bodies of the town formed at the Town Hall. Headed by the Volunteer Bind the procession moved to the hospital where it was met by Mr. Crossley, Mr. A. T. Smith, Dr. McDougall, Mr. E. Marshall and Mr. J. Wilding. In front °lithe hospital the company stopped, Mr. S. Taylor (chairman of the Urban Council) presiding over the sub: sapient proceedings. Among those present were Revs. Canon Wood, F. R. KiHick. A. Wells, W. T, Coppin, J. B. Foster, W. Gay, W. A. Eyre and J. Osborne, and Messrs. F. J. Norman, E. lanison, R. H. Posnett, F. Wiswall, J. H. Weston, NV. Handley, J. Pritchard, S. Walton, H. Baker, D. Bisbrown, J. M. Frith, W. Booth, J. F. Moulton, A. ft. Norman, J. W. Lightburn, Dr. McLennan, Dr. Delaney, Messrs. S. Pope, T. Riley, W. H. Huntley (chief engineer 'Ship Canal Company), H. Congreve, CE., F. B. Greenwood. W: E. Dudley, E. Gatley, J. H. Kirkham, J. Fisher, G, Hewitt, S. A. Beckett, F. Beck, J. Turnbull, T. Dean. W. J. Cunningham, W. Hankinson, Horsfield, W, Shaw, F. Rawlinson J. J. Shaw, T. Bowyer, T. Boote J. Nall, M. Barker, R. Rawlinson, F.Cringhall,J. Taylor, W. Banks, J. Bowyer, J. Percival, J. Holt, J. A. Green, A. Cha.rlesworth, W. H. Harrison, A. Steele, F. Hayes and H. Nield.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor, after eulogising the work of Mr. Wildin!-, said that it was the Cycle Club - who had really set the ball rolling. By hard work they made nearly £lOO. But for the war they would have been in a flinch better financial position as the town had had to pay large sums to the families and widows of the soldiers. But the money had come principally front the workpeople of the town and he was proud of the fact. They had now a hospital which would be equipped second to none of its size, and one which would answer the 'purpose they had been seeking for many years. To give them a little encouragetuenthe might say•tha•t the Ship Canal Corn- ' pally hail promised .250 a year for three years towards its support, and he felt sure, having such friends as Afr. Crossley, Mr. Hunter and Mr. Cougreve at court, they need have no fear of the future. (Applause.) They were privileged in having Mr. Crossley with them. It • gave the committee and everyone in Rtmcorn .extreme • pleasure and gratification when he consented to open the hospital. (Applause.) He had great pleasure in presenting to An a silver key and asking him to open the Victoria Memorial Hospital. (Applause.) Mr. Crossley after having opened and walked through the building, made a most felicitous speech. He was pleased that the workingmen had taken the matter auto their own hands. They had paid for it and there- fore would have an interest in it. With the money at their disposal they had achieved a wonderful resutt. He hoped the hospital would be a source of blessing to all. He believed they were on the verge of marvellous discoveries and hoped - a cure would be found for consumption. With that hope and the hope that this hospital might be of the greatest value to them, for an important place like Runcorn must have something of the kind, as accidents would occur, and prompt treat- ment more than anything else would save lives and would always alleviate much suffering, with these hopes and the further hope that the institution would be a blessinc, to all the people around, he had pleasure in de- clarinithe hospital open and trusted that more silver kets would coma to endow it and make it more useful to them. (Applause.) On the proposition of Mr„ Sra:th and Mr. Congreve a vote of thanks was passed to ,Mr.Crossleyamid cheers, Dr. McDougall supporting. Mr. Crossley having replied, Mr. Hunter proposed and Mr. A. R. Norman seconded a vote of thanks to Mr. Taylor. In responding, Mr. Taylor said it was a red letter day in his life. He trusted that the building would soon be free from its debt of MO se that the Urban Council, as trustees, could take the building over. There was no doubt but that Runcornians would respond to the appeal. The procsAings terminated with the Volunteer Band playing tho National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
