      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>"THE COUNTESS" AGAIN. Another Appearance at the Warrington County Court.</head>
        <p>"THE COUNTESS" AGAIN. Another Appearance at the Warrington County Court.</p>
        <p>. Mrs. William Gilroy, of-Congleton who claims to be the Countess of Stamford and Warrington made•another appearance at the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C. Entering the box she said she appeared in reference to an application she made at the Court on March 19th. when His Honour advised her to move by motion in proper order. His Honour: I didn't advise you. What I said was that I would not hear you unless you brought your motion before me in proper order. The applicant: Yes, and that I was to get legal assistance. Tam very sorry to intrude again, but I have tried to get legal assistance and five solicitors have relived. I therefore have prepared a notice of motion myself and if you will permit me to serve a copy of the said motion upon George J. Knight, chartered account- ant or his solicitor, r. ULake, and fix time for hearing I shall be eply grateful. There is also an affidavit here to be kworn by myself. . . . _ _</p>
        <p>The affidavit was-then handed to his Honour, and the applicant said as a creditor she asked to see the files in the bankruptcy preceedings re James Liddy, deceased, music seller of Runcorn. She claimed that she was the heiress in fee simple. His Honour asked the applicant why she could not get a solicitor.</p>
        <p>The Applicant: I cannot give any reason Why these lawyers will not do anything for me. They are pre- judiced against me and don't like me. (Laughter.) His Honour: That is bad taste. (Laughter,) DO, you think it is because they fear there is not much in your case. The Applicant: It may be. Gould you recommend me a benevolent solicitor? (Laughter). • His Honour : There are so many, you had better select. (Laughter.) Mr. Jenkins said he might say in self defence that he had been exceedingly kind and gracious to Mrs. Gilroy and so far as he was able he instructed her what to do. She was doing very well so far. (Renewed laughter.) His.Honour : I will take these papers with me and let you know at the next sitting of the Court whether they are in order.</p>
        <p>Erhe Applicant: Th.en I cannot have these papers and act in any way until three W?,eks to-day. His Honour: You must not bring an action against me for detention. (Laughter.) The Applicant: Would you instruct the registrar to return the papers one way or another next week?</p>
        <p>His Honotir : No. Here you had better take your payers. I will not have them. The Applicant: You may have them until three weeks to-day. His Honour: No. Did you prove your debt against the estate of Jaws Liddy, deceased. The Applicant: That is rather a vague way of pat- lag it. (Laughter). Yes, my debt was put in. His Honour : Have yon proved your debt in bank- ruptcy. If not you'will not be able to get the file or inspect the file. The Applicant: Yes, I have proved the debt. His Honour: Where did you prove it. In the silence of your own chamber? (Laughter). The Registrar said he had searched the bankruptcy file, and there was no proof on the file of this lady. His Honour: Why didn't you get advice from a solicitor? You have admitted there are some solicitors who are benevolent.</p>
        <p>. The Applicant: I didn't admit they were. (Laugh- ter.) His Honour: This affidavit of yours is not sworn. You must put yourself in order. If you have recourse to the law there are certain recognised methods which you must adopt. I cannot detain the business of the Court any longer. The next case please. The Applicant then left the Court..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN MANSLAUGHTER CASE.</head>
        <p>THE EARLESTOWN MANSLAUGHTER CASE.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Committed to the Assizes.</p>
        <p>Arthur Travers, butcher, 27' Legh-street, Earlestown, 'who has been twice remanded ona charge of feloniously killing his wife, Rhoda Travers, thirty-nine years of age, on the 17th March, was again brought .up at the Newton Police Court on Monday on the afore-mentioned charge. Mr. • Unsworth, of Warrington, appeared on behalf of the prisoner. • Percy R. Gange, physician and surgeon, said that on March 16 he was called to 27, Legh-street, Earlestown, at six o'clock in -the morning, and there saw deceased lying on the sofa, partly intoxicated. Travers was pre- sent, and explained that his wife was falling off the sofa when he put out his foot, and it wiudedher. Later in the day she complained of pains in the stomach. De- ceased informed him that " Mr. Travers kicked me twice in the stomach." Prisoner was present, and denied it. Mrs. Travers improved until Saturday. On Sunday morning peritonitis set in. in the afternoon witness saw prisoner in the " shop, and he re- market: "My wife had risen to come across the kitchen ; I put_ out my knee 'to save her." ; Prisoner had previously been informed that his wife's condition was dangerous. Later in the evening prisoner ' made another statement to the effect that his wife came across the kitchen' and he thought she was going to scratch his face. He put out his feat, and caught her Iwith the flat of it in the stomach, which he had done many times before when he thought she was going for him when in drink. Death took place next morning about seven o'clock. - Witness and Dr. F. Dowling made a post-mortem erxamination the same afternoon. The primary cause of death WitS acute peritonitis. In h s opinion it was impossible for the injuries to be caused by slight violence. Considerable violence must have been used.</p>
        <p>Arthur Travers, prisoner's son, stated that on the morning in question his mother was lying on the sofa speechless, andas she tried to get up his father pushed her with the flat of his 'foot sufficiently to send her back on to the sofa. His father used two words as he pushect her, and the latter one sounded like " out." His father had no shoes on at the time. In answer to, the magistrates' clerk. witness stated that his fathersaid to deceased that if she kept going on "as she had done in years gone by he would swing for her." That was before his father pushed her. After being puslted on the sofa his mother could not get her breath. His father did all he could to enable her to get her breath, then sent witness for Dr. Gauge. In answer to Superintendent Cocker witness stated that his father and mother had been quarrelling ten minutes before his father pushed deceased. Prisoner's daughter also gave evidence. • George Farrell, residing next door to the Travers, said he heard a noise after midnight in Travers's house, but as such occurrences were frequent, he took little notice of it.</p>
        <p>Charles 4assey,labonrer, 129; Legh-street,Earlestown, stated that deceased was at his house. Witness stated that he did not come up at the inquest, because he was not asked. He had reason to remember the interview, because prisoner had been in his house tke night pre- viously. He was not a friend of his, but had dealt with prisoner four or five years. He had never seen deceased the worse for drink in his life. Prisoner had told wit- ness two different tales on Tuesday, and another on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Sergeant lAings stated that he and Inspector Foster made inquiries about allegations made by the prisoner about a constable having been with his wife, but the prisoner denied it. The mftgistrates committed the prisoner to the Liver- pool Assizes on a charge of feloniously killing and slaying his wife, equivalent to manslaughter. Mr. Unsworth applied for prisoner to be bailed out on his oim recognisances. He had obtained two sureties of £5O, and prisoner in £100; but owing to the great feeling against. him in Earlestown the two sureties had declined to come forward. He asked for bail on prisoner's own recognisances. Superintendent Cocker objeCted, and prisoner was not granted- bail. Appleton Rifle Club. _ _ _</p>
        <p>file Annual General Meeting of the above club was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Monday night last, there being a fair attendance of members. 'Mr. Colin Maclver was in the chair.—The Treasurer, in sub- mitting the accounts to the meeting, said that the construction of the range had "Jost rather more than was 'anticipated, and that there was a deficit on the range account of about .t6O. He pointed out, how- ever, that on the general working of the club there had been no deficit.--The accounts were then adopted. The Committee elected for 1903:are Messrs V.B.Davies Hugh Derbyshire, J. Ellison, Captain Goren, Rev. H. B. Lester, P. Pendlebury, .T. E. Seeker, A. Warburton, and J. it. Whitley. Mr. Nfaciver was le-elected Hon. 'Treasurer, and Sir. Voisey promised, as accountant, to take over the accounts of the club. A letter was read from Major Iloome resigning the Secretaryship, and a vote of thanks was accorded to him for his services both in the construction of the club, and as secretary. Mr. Segar Owen was appointed Hon Sec. for 1903. A programme for the ensuing year was submitted for approval, which consisted-Rrincipally of the holding of monthly competitions, :to be held on the last Thursday the or Saturday of each tallith ; and that e sores registered by , members on these days would be the basis of the handicap for the Club's medal, which latter Will, be competed for on Saturday, September 12th The programme for the ab ,ve com- petitions, was referred to the Range Committee •to Idraw up and issue. Several new members wer elected. .A vote of thanks was passed to the chair man, Mr. Mclver, for presiding.</p>
        <p>Chapman's Execution. "Klosowski, alias Chapman, will, be hanged at Wandsworth on Tne,,day next. The Home Secretary will not interfere with the death ,sentence. A Goon RESOLUTIoN.—Year Au' TANLott's Wonderful Boots.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ACCIDENT OR NATURAL CAUSES.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT OR NATURAL CAUSES.</p>
        <p>A Labourer's Death at a Warrington Works.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour, Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., Ada Gaffogiut, Wyrley House Farm, Great Wyrley. near Walsall, applied for £192 16s. 7d.. compensation from Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Howloy Timber Works, Warrington, on the death of her husband whilst following his employment at the works.—Mr. Graham Millward,barristerßirming- ham (instructed by Mr. Armstrong, solicitor, Walsall,) appeared for the applicant and Mr. A. Elliott, barrister, Manchester, represented the respondents. Mr. Millward said the accident which resulted in the death of applicant's husband occurred on December 3rd, 1902. At that time the deceased had been inthe 6mploy of the respondents for three years. The amount claimed had been agreed upon as representing his earnings for that period. Part of deceased's duty on the day of the 'accident was to stack timber. The work was of a rather peculiar character. There was a stack of timber tenni- twelve feet high and parallel to it was a sloping plank reaching nearly to the top of the stack. At the top of the plank there were two other planks fourteen inches broad which formed a little platform. Deceased had to carry baulks of timber twenty two feet long on his shoulder up the plank and when he reached the platform he had to swing the planks round and push them longways on to the stack of timber. Deceased had carried a baulk of timber up the plank and placed it on the stack, whenhe suddenly. fell backwards from the stack to the ground. He was picked up and after breath- ing two or three times expired. A post-mortem exami- nation was made by Dr. Lana'sdale, and an inquest took place. Dr. Laugsdale would tell his Honour that when deceased was picked up there was a slight contusion on one side of the head. On further examination it was found that the neck was fractured at the fifth vertabra and that an aneurism of the aorta was ruptured. The respondents contended that death was due to natural causes but he (Mr. Millward) contended that the fall caused it.</p>
        <p>Ada Gaffagan, the applicant, said that at the time of deceased's death she and her child were wholly dependent upon his earnings. She saw him at noon on the date of the accident and he was then in the best of health.</p>
        <p>Joseo Gaest, an emnloy4 of the respondents, des- cribed the accident. / He said that when deceased had delivered the plank he bent forward and then fell back to the ground. Dr. Langsdale said he was called in to see the deceased directly after he died. He found a contused wound ors the left side of the head. Subsequently witness made a post-mortem examination. The neck was fractured at the fifth vertebra and an aneurism of the aorta was ruptured. Apart from anything else, a broken neck was lita,l injury. Witness was of the opinion that in al/ probability the rupture occurred first,- and then deceased fellback in a dying condition. The exertion of lifting a plank might have caused the rupture. He hardly thought the shock of falling from the stack would cause the rupture. The anenristu was only a fraction of an inch from the heart.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Elliott: At the inquest wit- ness expressed the opinion that deceased would not have lived any longer if he.had not received the injuries. A. fracture of the neck at the fifth vertebra did not usually cause instantaneous death.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elliott submitted that no case whatever had been made out.</p>
        <p>His Honour : lam of opinion that the cause of death was the rupture. The only question that troubles me, is whether or not the rupture Was caused by the appli- cation of unusual force.</p>
        <p>Mr. Elliott said his friend had to prove that the cause of death was an injury resulting from anaccident. There Was not a tittle of evidence of any accident whatever.</p>
        <p>I Mr. Millward contended that the mere falling off the ;platform was an accident and it was possible that the fracture of the neck was the cause of death. The shock I when the man reached the . ground probably caused the rupture. His Honour come to the conclusion that death arose , from natural causes and there was no case for the re- spondents to answer. He gave judgment for the respondents. I Costs were allowed; but Mr. Elliott promised not to enforce them if no fnrthor proceedings were taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>AAI RC LANCE MATTERS.</p>
        <p>A First aid" class has been formed by Dr. Valentine, and is held each Wednesday evening at the Town Hall, Earlestown under the St. John Ambulance rules. About 40 members put in an appearance at the first lecture on Wednesday night, and the class is likely to be a decided success. •</p>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS. Very little interest appears to be taken, so far, in the forthcoming contest .f or the vacancy in Crow-la tie Ward, As previously stated the contestants are Mr. G. F. Clarke, who has been defeated on three previous occasions, and Mr. A. Evett, who has not previously been a candidate. The fight on the day of the election is likely to be a keen one, as there are divergent interests to be considered, but there is no doubt the Catholic vote will turn the scale. P.S.A. The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and dis- • trict P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall. Earle- street, on Sunday afternoon. The hall was fairly well filled and Mr. Williamson (sec.), presided. An interest- ing address on fishing was delivered by Mr. Gilby, of Ashton. Master Harold Roberts was the vocalist and sang "Angels ever brightand fair" and " The Children's Home" in an excellent rammer. Recitations were con- tributed by Miss Roberts. Miss Williamson presided at the piano and Mr. E. Smith at the organ.</p>
        <p>GOOD T MPLARS. The Hope of Elrlestown Lodge held their animal weekly meeting in the Co-operative Hall, Earle- street on Monday evening. They were favoured with, a visit of the Leigh Alpha Lodge. Bro. John Parr occupied the chair and delivered a short address. Songs where rendered by Sister Downs and Bros. I3attersby, Rogers and Kelly, and Bro. Oliver of Manchester. Sisters Parr and Hulme contributed recitations, one Candidate was presented for admission to the order. The meeting 'closed with the usual votes of thanks to the visitorsand the Chairman. ALLEGED THEFT OF A BA TI RElt OF BEER AT L E STO NV N. At Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Thomas Craghan,of Earlestown, was charged with steal- ing a barrel of beer, valued at £2, the property of William Melling.—Evidence was given by Eliza Single- ton, wife of the manager of the New Market Inn,Earles- town, to The effect that defendant came into that house on Tuesday, along with other men, but the barman re- fused to serve them. Defendant then used bad language, and on going into the yard later, Mrs. Singleton heard defendant say : "We cannot get it inside, so we will take it from outside." Defendant rolled a barrel of beer about twenty yards up the back street. Mrs. Singleton followed, got hold of defendant's coat. and told him she could have him locked up as he had no, right with the beer. He called her foul names,and upon a policeman coming up she gave him into custody.—De- fondant was remanded on bail to Newton Petty Sessions ALLEG ED UNLAWFUL WOUNDING AT Ea 11 LESTOWN. At Newton Police Court on Monday, James Barnett, residing in Sankey-street, was charged with unlawfully wounding, by stabbing, his wife*, Blandina, on Saturday. Prosecutrix stated that on Saturday afternoon her husband came home at mid-day. She was asleep on the sofa toid he roused her. She got him some food ready and he wanted her to go out for some beer and borrow a shilling. She refused to go. He was sit at the table eating with his knife in his right hand, and without. saying another word he threw the knife at her and it caught her on the head and then fell on. the floor.—Mr. Superintendent Cocker asked that prishner should be remanded to Warrington until Thursdiy, and the magis- trates acceded to the request.—Prisoner was brought upat Warrington on Thursday when prosecutrix varied her statement. She said sho threw a basin at prisoner who got up in eterrible temper"and he threw the knife on the table. It bounced and caught her on the fore- head and then fell on the floor. Witness gave information to the police.—By Mr. Crompton (who defended): She did not think prisoner had any intention of , wounding her. —Dr. Latham said the wound was .about three quarters of an inch in length and extended down to the bone He did dot think the wound could have been inflicted in. the manner suggested by the prosecutrix.—Dina Barnett aged nine years; daughter of the prisoner, Corroborated the proseentrix's version.—Prisoner, who reserved his 'defence, was couunitted for trial at Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
