      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BICYCLE STOLEN FROM THE CoNSERVATIVE CLUB- CULPRIT SENT TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court onWriday, Edward Kelly, Kean-street, was charged with stealing from the entrance hall at the Conservative Club at 7.15 on Friday evening a bicycle of the value of fourteen guineas, the property of john Lockett, licencee of the "Rope and Anchor" Inn, Buttermarket-street—The Chief Constable said Mr. Lockett, who was a member of the Conserva- tive Club, went there shortly before five o'clock on Prid ty evening and left the bicycle near the staircase in the entrance hall with other machines. Shortly aft4r seven o'clock he went for the machine and found it had disappeared. In the meantime Mr. WilliamMarburton, the manager of the British Workman Cocoa Rooms, Bridge-street, saw the prisoner enter the club and ask for Mr. Hayes, who was one of the officials. He was told that Mr. Hayes was not in but he remained in the vestibule, and knowing that he was a stranger Mr. Warburton kept him under observation. He saw prisoner take Mr. Lockett's bicycle, which was the best machine in the hall, and wheel it outside. Mr. Warburton was suspicious and communicated his suspicions to Mr. James Strettle, an assistant -schoolmaster residing in Scotland-road. They went in search of the prisoner and ultimately overtook him wheeling the machine in Bold-street. They asked him what he was doing with it and he said he was taking it to a gentleman's house. He could not, however, give the name or address of•the gentleman and they took hint back to the club. P.C. Houghton was sent for and took ! prisoner into custody. He then denied having stolen the bicycle and subsequently stated that Mr. Strettle had authorised him to take it to his (Mr. Strettle's) house. There was no truth in the statement as Mr. Strettle never spoke to the man. Prisoner was far advanced under the influence of drink, but not drunk. The machine Wits locked so that the handles could not be turned. Prisoner evidently did not know how to unlock it. Prisoner now pleaded guilty to the theft, but said he would not have committed it if he had not been under the influence of drink.—The Chief Constable said prisoner had been convicted several timec previ- ously and was nothing but a "sponger and loafer." He (Mr. Talbot) had received complaints of other thefts of bicycle lamps &amp;c., committed in a similar way.—Prisoner was sentenced to a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BA MIFF ASSAULTED AT WARRINGTON',</head>
        <p>BA MIFF ASSAULTED AT WARRINGTON',</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Friday, James Corbett and Selina Corbett, who reside at 771 lilumpton street, were summoned for assaulting Thomas Cowdell, 31 Legh-street, on the 20th inst.—Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant.—The evidence showed that the complainant who is a certified bailiff, had to levy a distraint at 285, Liverpool-road on the date. in question. At four o'clock he left there to go into Plumpton- -stre3t. On leAvino. the latter street he was followed by some boys. Defendants' boy, who was the ring leader, began shouting "Here's the ' bums ' ; keep your doors shut." Complainant went into the house in Liverpool- road and shut the door. The boys made such a noise that he came out and ran after the defendant's boy with the .object of frightening him. The defendants came upon the scene and thinking that the boy was being assaulted struck Mr. Cowdell in the mouth, knocking two teeth down his throat. P.C. Woods came up and saw the complainant bleeding from the mouth.—The male defendant denied the assault, but the woman ad- mitted it. Each defendant was fined in the costs. IMPEDIGG TRAMWAY TRAFFIC.--GARTER FINED AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Thomas Grice, a carter, residing at 85, Greenall's- avenue, Wilderspool, was carged at the Warrington Police Court, on Friday, before. Mr. H. Roberts (pre- siding) and other magistrates, with impeding traffic on the tramways on Wilderspool Causeway on March 19th. —Mr. S. Wilson, deputy town clerk, prosecuted, and said the defendant was a carter in the employ of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd. At 7.10 p.m., on the date in question defendant was on Wilderspool Causeway in charge of a horse attached to a dray. .When the vehicle was opposite St. James' Church the 7.5 car from Rylands-street came along but was unable to pass the dray as;defendant was driving on the track. The driver sounded the gong several times and the defendant looked round but absolutely refused to drive off the lines. The conductor went to the defendant and asked him to pull to one side. The road was very wide and there was nothing to prevent the defendant from drivimg to the side. He, however, con- tinued his course in front of the car till he had almost reached the Saracen's Head. He (Mr. ,Wilson) c mild not understand why the defendant acted n that manner. In fairness to Messrs. Greenall he lutist say that they had from the very first shown a de- sire to assist the Corporation in the efficient workingon the tramways. Mr. Broady, the manager of the men, was present in Court and could say that the Company did not countenance the defendant's conduct for a moment.—The magistrates imposed a fine of ss. • and costs the chairman remarking that they recognised that the defendant was acting entirely on his own responsi- bility—probably with a spirit of "cussedness "—and not with the support or approval of his employers. If the bench had to deal-with any further cases of de- liberate obstruction they would probably fine the ulprits pretty heavily—The Chief Constable said the obstruction was an almost daily occurrence.—The Chairman said that as a rule the people in charge of horses and carts in Warrington would in the Main make every pro- vision for the efficient working of the tramways.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. H.-Roberts (in the chair), Alder- man Smethurst, Councillor John Roberts, and Mr. Thornton. _</p>
        <p>Worrall, 56, Hoyle-street, was sum- moned for assaulting Thomas Hodgett, 4, Russell-street, Complainant stated that the defendant seized him by the throat, struck him several times, and threw him on his back. Defendant's dog set upon him and tore his .clothing.—Defendant said complainant had repeatedly insulted him and that was why he committed the assault. The dog was trying to separate them.—(Laughter).—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed. DOMESTIC TROUBLES.—John Flannagan,3B,Hopwood :street was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife Ellen Flannagan, 140, Birchall-street.—Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant and said both parties had been ma-ried twice and had children. Trouble had arisen bc:ause two of defendant's sorts enlisted.</p>
        <p>Customer First</p>
        <p>The way to do business is to</p>
        <p>put one's customer's interest first: be faithful to him. Fels-Naptha does that it goes far to make a grocer a good one.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
        <p>Defendant's-cruelty had been so great th.atßtis wife had been compelled to leave him: Some time ago he kicked her in the mouth and knocked two teeth down her throat. Onthe 14th inst one of defendant's sons who was only 18 years of age came home drunk. Defendant had gone to bed and complainant upbraided the son for his conduct. Defendant came downstairs with a belt and was going to thrash the son. Complainant went between them and he struck her with the buckle end of the belt.—The defendant alleged that his wife did not give his children proper food—The magistrates granted a separation order with ss. per week alimony—Edwin Horrabin, 31, Cartwright-street, was summoned by his wife, Emily Ann Horrabin, for persistent cruelty—Mr. IBrowne appeared for the complainant and said that about twelve months ago defendant assaulted his wife BO severely that her cheek bone was fractured and she still suffered pain from it. Defendant had also thumped complainant about the head and kicked he She had been under medical treMment for seven weeks in con- sequence of his illusage. Defendant used most filthy lang Inge when drunk and last Saturday he came home drunk and ran complainant out of the house. In cross- examination by the defendant, plaintiff admitted having locked the defendant out of the house at night—Ultim- ately the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>- MONDAY.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (presiding), S. Wright, T. Birchall, H. Thornton, and Alderman Smethurst. ONE OF THE NUISANCES OF SILVER-STREET.—Eliza- beth Kearns, 16, Silver-street, was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly in Silver-street.— P.C. Andrews proved the case.—The Chief Constable remarked that there were very, few members of the force who did not know defendant by name. She had been there 22 times, and she promised to sign the pledge every time she came. She was one of the nuisances of Silver-street. HAWKING WITHOUT A CERTIFICATE.—John Richard Vernon, or Gregg, 17, Gee-street, was summoned for peddling without a certificate.—P.C. Lewis proved the C tse.—The Chief Constable said defendanthad his certifi- cate taken from him by the magistrates last March for felony, and he was defying the Court by continuing to hawk. Defendant had the effrontery to tell him (g.r. Talbot) that he was not fit for hard work.—A fine of ss. and costs was inflicted. FIGHTING.—Thounes Kirkham, 92, Catherine-street, and Thomas Mulligan, 20, Edward-street, labourers, were bound over and ordered to pay 7s. each for fighting.— P.C. Reddington proved the case. DISORDER.—For making loud and unseemly noises, Thomas Moran, 28, James-street, and Michael Cunning- ham, 29, James-street, were fined lls, each on the infor- mation of Sergeant Smith. BAD LANGUAGE.—Rebeeea Glover, widow, 49, Eldon- street, and Mary Glover, wife of Arthur Glover, of the same address, pleaded guilty to using bad language and were fined ss. each.—P.C. Clough proved the case and said defendants used disgusting language while people were coming out of Sunday school.</p>
      </div>
