      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>COMMERCIAL FAILURES.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAILURES.</p>
        <p>According to the "Manchester Guardian Society's Gazette," the number of failures in EnOand and Wales gazetted during the quarter ending Saturday, March 28th, was 1,090. The number in the corresponding qvarter of last year was 1,077, showing an inCrease of 13, being a net increase in 1903, to date, of 13. The failures were distributed amongst the following trades; and, for comparison, we give the number in each, in the corresponding quarter in 1902 and 1901. 1903 1902 1901 Bankers — --T 2 Blinding and Timber Trades Chemists and Druggists Coal and Mining Trades 37 22 17 Corn, Cattle, and Seed Trades Drapery, Silk, and Woolen Trades Earthenware arid -Glass Trades 6 2 8 Farmers Furniture and Upholstery Trades Grocery and Provision Trades 229 201 178 Hardware and Metal Trades l5 20 24 Iron and Steel Trades 2B 38 50 Jewellery and Fancy Trades Leather and Coach Trades Merchants, Brokers, and Agents • .53 46 58 Printing and Stationery Trades Wine, Spirit, and Beer Trades Miscellaneous l4B 131 117</p>
        <p>Total; for England and Wales— 1.030 1,077 1,119 Scotland (including Trust Deeds Granted) Ireland (including Deeds of Arrange- ' ment - • 148 108 114</p>
        <p>Totals for United Kingdom— 1,553 1,5:33 1,569</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>GREAT AUSTRALIAN DISCOVERY.</head>
        <p>A GREAT AUSTRALIAN DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>Australia, that mysterious country of gold, has been the birth-place of many wonderful things. Foremost amongst them is the discovery by an eminent scientist, of a natural vegetable substance which acts more bene- ficially upon the liver and digestive system than any medicine yet di s ove!e:l. Chas. Forde,the discoverer of this substance, had long been impressed with the remarkable freedom from disease of the Australian natives. _</p>
        <p>On this subject the great Captain Cook wrote: "I did not observe (amongst the natives) any appearance of disease or bodily complaint or eruption cot the skin. Very old men without hair and teeth showed no signs of decrepitude and were full of vivacity." There must besome reason for this, and Chas. Forde, armed with the implements of modern scientific research, sought for that reason amongst the rich profusion of Australian herbs which had been the sole medicines of thenatives. After years of research he found himself the happy dis- coverer of a vegetable substance which acted on the liverand digestive organs differently and in superior, manner to anymedicine ktilw n. The best laboratories the most modern appliances, and all that science dictated as being best for the purpose was requisitioned in the compounding of this substance into convenient medi- cinal form ; and the result of it all was the production a few years back of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans—the most perfect Medicine of modern times. On their appearance Bile Beans—the product of modern scie!ve—had to compete against the ancient and imperfect medicines which had been before the public for nearly half a century, but the public soon decided in their favour, and Bile BeallS are now the most widely used family medicine : over threequarters of a million doses being taken daily. The superiority of Bile Beans lies in the fact that they do not merely purge, giving temporary relief only like the old-fashioned, so-called remedies of forty or fifty years ago. They act directly on the liver and digestive organs, strengthening and stimulating those organs to perform their natural duties. They- produce a gentle action on the bowels, curing or preventing con- stipation, cleansing the stomach and ridding the system of all impurities. - Do not ie misled by claims of half a hundred pills in the box, where probably four to six constitutes a dose, and the doses cannot be discontinued. One Bile Bean is one dose, and they can be discontinued after the, cure is effected. They are purely vegetable; they con- tain no harmful drugs, and they are the safest family medicine. Of all chemists at one-and-three-half-pence or two-and-nine per box. St. John's Presbyterian Church._ .</p>
        <p>On Monday exening the Literary and Social Society organised in connection with St. John's Church closed the winter's session by the holding of a highly success- ful social meeting in the schoolroom in John-street. There was a pod attendance of members and their friends in spite of the unfavourable state Of the weather. The minister, the Rey. .T. L. Milne, M.A., 8.D., occupied the chair and opened the meeting, after which the President, Mr. W. E. Rees, gave a short summary of theL..Society's work during the season then ending. He said they had first an address on " Books " by the minister' afterwards a lecture on "European Ethnology," by Dr. Parker, Stockton Heath, and an address on "Failures which succeed," by the Rev. E. H. Rees, of Wolverhampton. There had been papers on "Burns and his works," and on "Scott and his works," debates on the questions "Should Women be allowed to compete -with men in the labour market ? " and "Do popular amusements tend to elevate ? " two evenings set apart for, miscellane ms debates and one for the production of -a manuscript magazine. The attendance at all the meetings had been better than in the previous session, and the various subjects had. been well and intelligently disclosed. It was hoped that their next season would be still more suc- cessful. The evening's programme was then opened with a piano solo by Mr. Atkinson ; songs were given by Mrs. P. H. Brown, and Messrs. Millinl.3ton and Hunter, selections on the flute by Sergeant Major CoWan, and excellent recitations by Mr. Harness who was well received and heartily encored. The lady members of the Church choir gave creditable render- ings of the Scottish favourites, " Comin' thro' the rye," and "Ye banks and braes 0' bonnie Dcion." During an interval in the programme tea was served by Miss Barlow and other lady members of the society, and later in the evening there was a service of fruit. The proceedings terminated with the passing of the usual votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
