      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Stormy Meeting of Shareholders.</head>
        <p>Stormy Meeting of Shareholders.</p>
        <p>Secretary to be Suspended. New Directors Elected. No building society meeting in Warrington has within recent years been of such a sensational char- acter and attended with such stormy scenes as that which was held in Cairo-street Schoolroom on Tuesday evening. The occasion was the annual meeting of the Warrington Permanent Benefit Building Society. Most of the shareholders were pre- sent, including a large number of ladies, the room being entirely filled. Lively scenes were not wholly unexpected, in -view 'of the unfavourable balance sheet which had been issued, but a further disappointment awaited them, for at the outset of the meeting the Chairman announced that certain irregularities had since come to light, and it was necessary that the books should be forthwith placed in the hands of some competent person for the purpose of investigation. This announcement came like a bombshell on the meeting and angered the shareholders who gave vent to their feelings in applauding some severe strictures which were made on the management by several share- holders. . The society, it appears from the balance skeet was established in 1867 and incorporated in 1896. Its shares I are £lO each. In February of last year, a dividend at the rate of 2 per cent. was paid to the shareholders but , this year the directors not only do not propose to pay a ' dividend but ''in consequence of actual and appre- hended loss upon the properties in possession they have decided, as directed by rules 48 and 49, to make a charge to meet such actual or apprehended loss, and the amount of such charge has been fixed at the rate of four pounds per full share on all share or part shares." That is to say anyone holding a £lO share in the Society will have it reduced to £6. Tge directors proceed to state that on account of the above decision, they have determined not to pay any dividend this year. The statement of accounts shows that the amount to be wiped off by this reduction of £lO shares to £6 value is no less than £13,806 17s. 6d. The liabilities and I assets account stands as follows :—To liabilities to holders of shares £20,711 ss. 4d.: undivided profit (in- cluding reserve funds, but not including prospective in- terest) £2,502 9s. 5d.; reduction of value of shares from £lO to £ti per share, £13,806 17s. 6d. less property abandoned £2,263 18s. Bd.-211,542 18s. 10d.; total £34.756 13s. 7d. By balance less or outstanding on mortgage securities but not including prospec- tive interest: On .15 mortgages where debt does I not exceed £5OO, 4:2,804 15s. ; on one mortgage where debt exceeds £5OO but does not exceed £1,000,£650; on 28 mortgages on property of which the Society has bean upwards of 12 months in possession £3.1,202 14s. lid.; bankers' balance £99 35. Bd.; total £34,756 13s. 7d. The number of members registered is 362. According to the schedule published with the balance sheet the Society has advanced £29,660 on property originally valued at £34,312, the debt at the time it was , taken possession of amounting to £31,184. The amount included in the assets is £31,201. The gross income for the year is put down at £2,763, and the outgoings £1,690. The certificate of the auditors is as follows :—We, the undersigned, James Bolton, being a person who I publicly carries on the business of an accountant, at I No 45, 'Sankey street, Warrington, and William Dick, residing at 119 Bewsey road, Warrington, the duly appointed auditors of the aboveamentioned society, do hereby attest the foregoing accounts and statements,and certify that they are correct, duly vouched, and in ac- cordance with law; and we certify that we have and each of us has at this audit actually inspected the mort- ' gage deeds and other securities belonging to the society. in respect of each of the 44 properties in mort- gage to this society, referred to in the foregoing accounts and statements.—R. Proud, secretary; James Bolton and Wm. Dick, auditors. March 1903. The proceedings at the meeting werepresided overby Mr. James Kenworthy (chairman of directors). He was accompanied on the platform by the following directors: Messrs. John Critchley, Thomas Critchley,l John Dewhurst, Themes lace, George Legge, John Mather, P. C. Price, and Rd. Shackleton. The solicitors to the society (Messrs. R. Davies and Company) were represented by Mr. E. Draper. Mr. R. Proud (secretary) was present in the room for a short time but left before the meeting commenced. THE CHAIRMAN AND THE ALLEGED IRREGULARITIES. BOOKS TO BE INVESTIGATED. At the outset the CHAIRMAN said: I am going to read a statement to you to which I ask your very care- ful attention, and then you will see the position we are in to-night. Your directors much regret to have to in- form you that since the circulation orthe report and the accounts certain irregularities in the books of the society have come to light. Your directors hope that these irregularities will prove of a comparatively trifling nature, but under the circumstances they are of opinion —(Here there was considerable interruption as some members of the audience said they could not hear the Chairman's remarks. Mr. Kenworthy there- fore repeated his statement) Continuing he said : They are of the opinion that the best course to be adopted in the interest of all and in justice to all, is that the ,books of the society shall be forthwith placed in the hands of some competent person, for the purpose of investigation, and, although they would be perfectly willing to adopt any nominee approved of by the majority of the investing members they would suggest the name of Mr. Edwin Bradshaw as a gentlemen in every way well-fitted—(applause)—to satisfactorily carry out the proposed investigation. Your directors also suggest that the investing members should appoint three or four of their number as a com- mittee to act in conjunction with the directors in super- vising the carrying out of the investigation if such a course is decided upon. Under the circumstances it appears to your directors that the best course will be to adjourn the present meeting until the investigation is completed and the report lofi whoever is appointed to 'carry out the investigation is reeeived. On the receipt of such report a furthbr meeting, that is the adjourned meeting, will be called and full information as to the results of the investigation and the position of the soeiety placed before the members. Of course you all know that when anything of this kind takes place the least said the soon- estemended. "E(A Voice "Query.") We are not so sure of thb position as to make definite charges of any kind, but when we found these irregularities, I, as presi- dent of the society, acting under the advice of our solicitors, at once took possession of the books of the society, and removed them from/the office to the office of the solicitors. (Applause.) It is so recent, this discovery, that there has not been time yet to sum- mon a meeting of directors to consider the whole position, but a summons has been issued, and the direc- tors will meet very shortly now to take the whole sub- ject into consideration. But we suggest—l may say that this suggestion ddes not come from the managers or directors for the simple reason that although they have supported as far as possible the action I have taken in conjunction with the solicitors we have not been able to lay everything before them—that the best plan )01 be to nominate—and I do that as chairman—Mr. Edwin Bradshaw, accountant, to investigate the books, and, if you think fit, appoint a committee of three or four of the investing members who are not under notice to withdraw to join with the directors. We shall be very glad of your assistence in carrying through that investi- gation. I don't think it need be a very long process. Little things sometimes happen and bring these things to the surface as has occurred in this case. I move that Mr. Bradshaw be appointed the accountant to investi- gate the books of the society. Will anyone second? SHAREHOLDERS ASK QUESTIONS. AUDITORS NOMINATED. 1 Mr. JOHN COOPER: I ana in a position to nominate two auditors—Mr. Edwin Bradshaw, chartered account- ant, and Mr. W. H. Woodcock, incorporated accountant. (Applause.) ' The CHAIRMAN: As we cannot pass the report owing to these mistakes, the Chairman certainly will not move the adoption of the report under the present circum- stances, and he cannot ask the members present to vote for the adoption of the report which has been issued. Therefore, until that report is adopted your proposition won't come in. • Mr. COOPER: I simply mentioned that I was going to have done that. • The CHAIRM AN: I am quite prepared to receive any- thing of that kind, but for the present we had better confine ourselves to the business I have suggested. We are very anxious to meet you in every possible way, and I think that as you and I are both alike in suggesting Mr. Bradshaw, the best thing is for the members to vote for him. A SHAREHOLDER: Why cannot we have two? This is not for a general audit, but for a special purpose. • (Hear, hear.) I The CHAIRMAN : I don't think it will he necessary to appoint two for this special object. I Mr. S. WESLEY !DALLOW said he was aware they • would not be able to pass the balance sheet, but they ! would be able to elect the officers they required. ; (Hear, hear, and applause.) He would like to ask the , Chairman if he was going to allow people who had given notice of withdrawal theprivilege of voting that night? • ( Xpplause.) He was speaking not for himself. Ile had not given notice for his own money, but he considered • a great injustice was being done to members who had invested their money who were not allowed to speak at the meeting. When Rule 31 wagaccepted it was under.</p>
        <p>stood that when a reasonable time had elapsed after notice the money would be paid. (Applause.) There were members in the audience who had given notice for two or three years, and it was a gross injustice that they should not be allowed to have a voice in the meeting He proposed that everyone who was an investing mem- ber be allowed to vote that night. The CHAU-1:U.1.N : lam very sorry, Mr. Dallow, but the rules of the society must be carried out. Mr. DALLOW : lam quite aware the rule is here,but as investing members we don't ask you to give us any other power but to speak and vote. (Hear, hear and applause.) The CHAIRMAN: Whatever may be our personal feelings ix:Lithe matter we :Ire bound entirely by the rules (A voice : "Not at all.")' Mr. FRED TEMPF,RLEY : Have you stuck to rule 18, Mr. Chairman? Refer to the rule and see if you have stuck to that.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I must assert my right as chairman. Mr. TRUSLOVE ; What have the auditors to say about the irregularities: (applause). Are the auditors here? Mr. JAMES BOLTON: They are here.</p>
        <p>Mr. TRUSLOVE : Have you any renrirks to make as to the irregularities ? Mr: JAMES BOLTON: They have cropped up within the last few days.</p>
        <p>Ir. TRUSLON7E : How is it you have not discovered theta before.</p>
        <p>Mr. BOLTON: We cannot go into that in a public meeting. I think you would act wisely by deferring the consideration of the accounts until the adjourned meeting. Mr. TRUSLOVE : This is not a mitter that should be pissed over. Tne question should be asked. (Hear, hear,) _</p>
        <p>A SLIARPHIOLDER : Was the balance sheet audited before it was sent out. Have these irregularities some to light since. Mr. BOLTON: A few days afterwards. A SIIA.REEIOLDER Well ; they don't affect the balance sheet then.</p>
        <p>Mr. BoLTON : balance sheet.</p>
        <p>They are in cosettection. with the</p>
        <p>Mr. SINIMONETTE : Has all this been dons in a uniform way, or has something been done since . . _</p>
        <p>Mr. TIMI;kRLEY. Por the information of the Chair- man and the directors I may say that when the balance sheet came outa fewshareholders met and went through it and we were not satisfied at all. We met no later than Friday evening last, and decided to bring forward to-night the desirability of the appointment of a com- mittee to investigate certain figures shown on the balance sheet. Mr. Bradshaw is the gentleman we were going to have with us. The CHAIRMAN : Then I think the bait plan is to appoint Mr. Bradshaw. Does anyone second Mr. Brad- shaw's appointment? Ivtr. W. J. SHAW: Before we take any course The CHAIRMAN (interrupting): There ie.a proposi- tion before the meeting. Does anyone second it ? MR. SHAW TAKES UP THE ATI'ACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SEVERE STRICTURES ON THE MANAGENIENT.</head>
        <p>SEVERE STRICTURES ON THE MANAGENIENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. SHAW : The proposition is yours, no one else's. Continuing, Mr. Shaw said he would prbpose someone whose name would meet, with the approval of the mem- bers generally. (Applause). They believed that the society had not been conducted on fair and proper lines. He was not going to lay a charge of diithonesty against any particular individual, but inasmuch as the Chairman had said that thre had been certain irregularities, he would like to submit to Mr. Kenworthy and to the members present that the balance sheet which had been issued was in his opinion neither more nor less than a Board of Trade return. There was no one in that assembly, unless he was a professional auditor, who understood the composition and the make-up of the balance sheet. (Hear, hear and applause). He asked Mr. Kenworthy, as chairmen of the society, whether the present was the proper time for the irregularities to be found out after the balance sheet has been issued and sent out to be adopted? He certainly said "No." The auditors, must have missed some part of their duties or otherwise he (the Chairman) could not say that night the balance sheet was wrong. He (the speaker) made some remarks at a meeting held about two years ago, when he was assured by the chairman and his co-directors—Mr. Kirkconnel was present then— that the society was in a solvent condition. If it was in a solvent condition two yeas ago how on earth could they now arrive at the determination to ask the share- holders to forfeit a matter of 40 per cent, of the whole of the funds of the society? (Applause.) It was in- credible. It was beyond one's conception to think that within two years a men could stand there and tell them a truism • they all believed it and that day they knew it was a lie-ism. (Applause.) He felt sure as he stood in that meeting that the condition of the society was known by some gentlemen on the platform at that time. (Loud applause.) He further made a suggestion to Mr. Proud and to the chairman and directors that it was not right for the society,knowing its pogition,to receive investors' money. He asked them—he charged them to answer him with all truth„fulness and clear- ness—whether that had been the case. Had they received instalments within the two years. He considered that if they had received instalments during the past two years it was very unfair to those poor people who had invested their money. Last year the directors condescended to state in the report that they were in a position to pia- 21 per cent. What he wanted to know was this? Where did they get the money from to pay the dividend with? (Applause). Was it paid with moneys accumulated by the society? The Chairmen had spoken about the rules and had stuck hard and fast to them. Did they stick to their rules by providing the dividend from a true and honest source? He said not. Therefore the directors did not altogether stick to the rules, and if a concession could be made the other night surely there was nothing very ,detrimental about giving the people a chance of voting simply because it was said in a rule that they should not have that power. This was the annual meeting. They were present as the major members, the major representation of the society, , and surely to fortune they could deal with the rules to ' snit their purpose best. He considered that the direc- tors of the society had been remiss, knowing, in his , opinipn, that the funds of the society were not as they shouM be, in allowing the Secretary's salary to be paid-: he meant at the price which had been paid him. When moneys were being received by the society from a great number of depositors he admitted that the Secretary had a fair amount of work to do, but for the last twelve months he found from the balance sheet that he had only received a matter of £9; probably he had a few transfers to make. The directors might have stepped forward and have shown them some little encourage- ment on the balance sheet—that they were going to cut and curtail the expenses in some way or other. Twelve months ago he mentioned the question of the direotors' fees and the visiting fees, but he was pooh-poohed. He did not agree that any director should receive &amp; farthing for his labours. (Applause). When was there a change on the Board? it was now time there was a change of some sort. (Loud applause). They could not receive the report; it would be fatal to every one of them to receive "such a scandalous condition of a report," but they could do the other thing which was legal and right —if it was not done that night the time would have passed until another year had expired—and that was that the three retiring directors should have some opposition that night. (Hear, hear). They would try to propose from that assembly that gentlemen should be appointed who would act so that the members would know a little -bit more of the inner workings than they had done. Mr. Shaw then asked the Chairman if they were in a position that night to give notice for the termination of the services of their valuablesecretary. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN: I think I explained sufficiently the steps that have been taken in reference to that. Mr. SHAW: I take it for granted that I shall be in my proper position to-night if I move that the Secretary be suspended? (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN: We shall be quite willing to receive an instruction to do that work but the rules again come in. (Laughter.) The directors by two-thirds majority may suspend any officer of the society. If you decide he ought to be suspended we shall accept your resolution and carry it out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>iiit-SE-dRETARY TO BE SUSPENDED.</head>
        <p>iiit-SE-dRETARY TO BE SUSPENDED.</p>
        <p>Mr. SHAW: I have great pleasure in proposing that Mr. Proud's services be no further required and that he</p>
        <p>be suspended from the secretary's position. (Applause). The CHAIRMAN: May I ask you to put it in the form of a recommendation to the directors and it will be done in a legal form, so that no question can rise here- after. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. SII.A.W : The Secretary is appointed for a year? • The CHAIRMAN: No, sir; he has not been re-ap- pointed since his first appointment. Mr. SHAw afterwards moved a resolution to the effect that the directors be " authorized" to suspend the secretary. On the suggestion of the CHAIRMAN, Mr. SHAW altered the motion to read that the directors be "in- structed" to suspend the secretary,the chairman remark- ing that if it were put in that form the directors would have no option but to carry it out. Mr. DALLOW seconded, and asked what the secretary had been paid, and what was the rent of the office. The CHAIRMAN replied that the salary was fixed•when the secretary was first appointed. It was not a salary but a commission on the number of shares in the society. (" Shame.") As the number of shares went down his salary went down, and it was now at the lowest ebb it was ever at. (" Shame.") The amount was fixed by the society long before the present directors became managers. Mr. DALLOW : Can you give us anything like the ap- proximate amount? TheCFIAIRNIAN : As near as I can tell, about £l2O. Mr. DALLow : Do we p.ty rent for the office as., well ?</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : It is included in the £l2O</p>
        <p>Mr. DALLOW : What is paid for rent collecting ? The CHAIRMAN: We have two rent collectors—ono for Warrington and one for Widnes; 2A per cent. in Warrington and 2ft per cent. in Widnes. Mr. Proud does nothingof that work. He has to look after the repairs of the property,and I report when any property wants repairing. Mr. SHAW : What is the amount of money it has cost the society in street making oc general repairs to pro- perty owned by the Society? The CHAIRMAN : I have asked for that return from Mr. Proud for the past six months and have not got it yet. Mr. SHAW : 'Then how can you produce the report you have issued? The CHAIRMAN : I have asked Mr. Proud for the re-</p>
        <p>port. You are too fond of making rash statements, Mr. Saaav : It is quite time I did. MR. DEWHURST SHOUTED DYNN. Mr. DEWHGRST : I hopo' you will give the chairman a proper opportunity of speaking. I think he has answered your questions. (Loud cries of "sit down.") All I ask for is this—(continued cries of " sit down " aad uproar.) Mr. Dewaurst had at length to resume his seat. The CHAIRMAN said the directors had nothing to hide, nothing to shirk, and nothing to keep from then. A SEIAREFIOLDEa ; Way did you receive members' subscriptions ? The CHALRAIAN said the directors had passeda resoln- tioa refusing to receive subscriptions. If any little subscriptions had been paid they had been paid to m Oslo up shares to certain amounts Another SHAREHOLDER: (Laughter.) • The Caaartst.ta said he wartied to be straight with them. They could not legally take the votes of those who had given notice for all their money. ("Shame.") The CHAIRMAN then put the proposition to the meet- ing, and asked for a show of hands. A SHAREHOLDER,: - We will hold up two if you like. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN declared the proposition to be carried "practically unanimously." THE QUESTION OF WITHDRAWALS. Mr. JOSEPH POTTER: May I ask how many mem- bers have given notice of withdrawal, and what is the date of the first in order? The CHAIRMAN: The first on the list is Mr. Sergeant Brayshaw, and he gave notice, and WAS the only one to do so, in January 1899. ("Shame.") Mr. POTTER: Have any of the directors had with- drawals since- that? (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN : No. Mr. POTTER: How m any have given notice of with- drawal? The CHAIRMAN : The amount is £lO,OOO. • Mr. POTTER: Never mind the amount. I want the number of persons? The CHAIRMAN said he would have to count, and he proceeded to do so. A SHAREHOLDER: Do you mean to say no diriactors have withdrawif one penny since 1899? The CHAIRMAN: Not to my knowledge. A SHAREHOLDER: Have they borrowed any money? The CHAIRMAN: No, they have not. As near as we can get at it by going through the books the number of notices for withdrawal is about 95—(cries of "Oh ") out of nearly 400. members. Mr. F. LEIGH: May I ask what is the date of the next withdrawn, and of all dates down to March? The CHAIRMAN said they could not go into that. The bulk of the notices came in three years ago when a reduction of interest to 2i per cent took niece. Mr. SEIAW asked if they were not on the right side of the bank twelve months ago by a sum of .190,which they showed on the balance sheet? The CHAIRMAN: No. There was a balance of £52 on the wrong side, and three years ago there was a balance in hand of over £3,000. Mr. Shaw said he supposed they were aware there was a deficit of £52 when they suggested a dividend of 2i per cent. Perhaps the Chairman would tell the deposi-' tors where they got the money from ai'pay that dividend? TheCHAIRMAN: We got it from the bank. (" Shame.") , Mr. SHAW : And you were £52 on the wrong side? The CHAIRMAN said that was so, but they paid the dividend oat of the profits of the year, amounting to over £l,OOO. Mr. SHAW : If you paid the dividend out of the profits how is it you want to the bank for money ? (Hear, hear) The CHAIRMAN said they lent money on mortgage. They had also borrowed money repeatedly for paying interest when they lent more money and paid out with- drawals. • Mr. SHAW said it was a very unusual thing to go to the bank andpay five per cent for borrowing to pay 2i per cent dividend. (Hear, hear and applause) He be- lieved if the investors had been told the society was not in a position two years ago to pay a dividend they would have accepted it with good grace, and if they-had done so they would have been in a different position from what they were to-day. (Hear, hear) • The CHAIRMAN said the shareholders were asking questions that he, as a director, had nothing to do with. The accounts were submitted to them when they met, the auditors audited them, and they expected they were °correct. Mr. Sisavv asked if all the properties named in the balance sheet were now in the hands of the society? If that was so he was perfectly satisfied, but.he wanted to know if there was any truth in the rumour he had heard that there was certain property that had passed from their hands twenty years ago that now appeared in the balance sheet. (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN said no doUbt Mr. Shaw and other members would remember that last year he (the chair- man) pointed out a discrepanay in the balance sheet in the table of properties in their possession. Certain properties had been sold, but the amounts a the advance at the commencement were not reduced in proportion to what they ought to have been,and they discovered this. The directors received the printed balance sheet exactly at the same time as the shareholders did. When the draft ' balance sheet was submitted this year he turned specially to see that the properties were reduced as they ought to have been, and he found four were not reduced. They, as directors, gave instructions for those properties to be reduced to their proper figure and the directors were promised that that would be done. When he saw the printed balance sheet he found that what had been in- structed had been done with the exception of one case, but there was printed at the end so many houses and shops sold, so that it would not be misleading. Mr. SHAW : Is it honest to put such things on the ' balance sheet, and have you not a right to go through it before it goes to the printer's hands? The CHAIRMAN said that was what had been done. (Laughter). Mr. SHAW : With your eyes open you have consented to this ? Mr. FRED LEIGH: You said you did not look through the balance sheet, and now you say you do. •.The CHAIRMAN said he called the Secretary's atten- tion to discrepancies in the balance sheet, and they were o rdered to be corrected. . Mr. FRED LEIGH: But you ought to correct the balance sheet yourself. You gave me the impressiou that you received the balance sheet at the same time we did, but on the other hand we find you have the balance sheet before the printer. The CHAIRMAN : That is a written balance sheet. Mr. FRED. LEIGH : The printed balance sheet ought to be the same as it is passed. The CHAIRMAN said they expected corrections to be made if the written balance sheet showed discrepancies. MR. SHAW AND DUAL OFFICES. THE CHAIRMAN AND B OARD OF TRADE REGULATIONS. Mr. SHAW -said that although he did not generally agree with dual positions, he moved that it be recom- mended to the directors to elect a man for the joint offices of secretary and collector. (Hear, hear.) He found these offices had practically absorbed £3OO or upwards a year, and he thought a very material saving could be made by joining them together. He would also suggest that the directors forego their fee of 2s. 6d. a meeting. He did not know what number of meetings, were held each year,but if they were monthly meetings the 2s. 6d. per meeting was equal so 11 percent. on£loo. (Hear, hear.) He suggested that the directors might forego that amount. (Hear, hear.) Mr. TIMPERLEY asked how the difference in the num- ber of registered members of the society-377 in 1902 as against 362 this year—was accounted for. The CHAIRMAN said they were reduced by members who had borrowed money paying off their mortgages, but they must net accept his statement as strictly accurate. He pointed out that the balance sheets were according tie the Beard of Trade regulations. They could not make "top nor tail" of them without a great deal of labour, but according to Act of Parliament they could not make out their balance sheets in any other way. THE BREAKING OF RULES. Mr. TIMPERLEY called attention to rule 18, relating to payment by cheque when sums were over a certain amount. How long, he asked, had this rule been broken? The CHAIRMAN: We have occasionally known of cases and spoken to the secretary about it. Mr. TIMPERLEY: You mean to tell me you knew about it and couldn't stop it. I think it is disgraceful. (Applause). Mr. ANNETT of Runcorn complained that he had never been notified of any alteration in the rules. The CHAIRMAN observed that there had been a vast amount of disorderly conduct that evening and for a man now to get up and find fault with the new rules that were passed by a meeting of shareholders specially ordered for the purpose,. was simply "cooking mis- chief." "NOW GENTLEMEN, TO BUSINESS PLEASE," Mr. ANNE'TT said he had been a member of the society for 20 years but he never had notice of the meeting at which the rules were altered. The, CHAIRMAN said this must have been the result of not notifying a change of address. It had been custom- ary to issue an advertisement of the meetings, because it saved expense. (Oh, oh! and "Question.") s Mr. Straav moved that they have new rules for the society, that the new board of directors meet to make such and to submit them to a geneeal meeting for ap- proval. The CHAIRMAN said Mr. Shaw was making a mistake when he thought the directors had drawn up the rules. Mr. SHAW contended that they were part makers of them when they were altered in 1896. The CHAIRMAN said three-fourths of the rules had to be in accordance with the Board of Trade regulations. Mr. HARRISON said it had been said members had been stopped front investing money. Did they know money had been paid out by the society up to within about two months ago? . The CHAIRMAN: I did not know. Mr. HARRISON: It is a fact, and I should Like to know what fund it has been paid out of. The CHAIRMAN: it has not been paid out of the funds of the society. Mr. HARRISON: It has. I emphatically assert it, and you ought to know what fund it has been paid out of,</p>
        <p>After yaa got. my £5O.</p>
        <p>The CILAIRMIN : Now, gentlemen, to business please, (" I think so," and laughter.) Mr. Stis.w said iere was more startling news.. A shareholder said that last Saturday mere was a pay- ment of £2 10s. from this society. There was no Aeny lag the fact. Where did the money come from? The : I cannot tell you. I Mr. SHAw : Is it not time there was a change ? (4 yes.”)</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Yes, I quite approve of the resolu- tion you have moved. I believe there is need for a change, 'out we cannot be responsible for acts done be- hind our backs, and of which we have not the slightest knowledge.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERT YOUNG asked where was the. property that, according to the balance sheet, had been aban- doned.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said it was at Ditton Junction. A street of houses came into their possession over 25 years ago. There were 32 houses, and at the present time only one was tenanted. The property had not paid them for three years, and they decided it should be abandoned.</p>
        <p>Mr. SiLtw : Have the directors power to abandon property? The CHAIRM AN : Oh yes. UNDIVIDED PROFIT. Mr. LONGSHAW stated that under the head of undivided prolit,including reserve, there was a sum of £2,502 9s. sd. He would • like to ask what was the reserve fund at the present time. The CHAIRMAN : That is the reserve fund. Mr. LONGSH ANY : Where is it invested ? The CHAIRMAN : In property ("Oh, oh.") APPOINTMENT OF SPECIAL •AUDITORS. Mr. COOPER moved that Messrs E. Bradshaw and W. H. Woodcock be auditors in lieu of Messrs James Bol- ton and William Dick.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN :Are they bol investing members? Mr. Coomt : No. The CHAIRMAN : The rules state that all officers must be investing members. A SHAREHOLDER : Was Mr. Bolton one? Mr. BOLTON: Yes unfortunately. Mr. F. LEIGH (to the Chairman) : You yourself suggested Mr. Bradshaw. 11111 b The CHAIRM AN : • Yes but that was for a special purpose, not for the position of auditors. Mr. SHAW : I will transfer one of my shares to Mr. Bradshaw- in order to make him a shareholder. The share is not much•use (Laughter.) Mr. MAYBURY then proposed that Mr. E. Bradshaw and Mr. W. H. Woodcock be appointed as special auditors to investigate the accounts of the society. The CHAIRMAN said that proposition was in order as it was not necessary for the special auditors to. be shareholders.</p>
        <p>Mr. COOPER seconded the proposition and it was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>NEW DIRECTORS ELECTED.</p>
        <p>CHAIRMAN DEFEATED BY A LARGE MAJORITY.</p>
        <p>The election of three directors to take the places of the retiring directors (Messrs. Kenworthy, Dewhurst, an Legge) was then proceeded with. Mr. ANNETT proposed, and Mr. PARTRIDGE seconded, the appointment of Mr. W. J. Shaw.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKES proposed, and Mr. ANNETT seconded, that Mr.S. W. Dallow be one of the directors.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fred TIMPERLEY was proposed on the of Mr..ANNETT, seconded by Mr. John Smith. Mr. JOHN' CRITCHLEY, a member of the board of directors, proposed that Mr. Kenworthy be one of the directors. He said Mr. Kenworthy had been the best chairman the society had ever had, and he had voted most honestly and honourably. Considering the diffi- culties they were going through at the present time, he thought it would be very dishonourable on the part of the meeting to leave him off. He was in it. (A share- holder! "We all are in it" and laughter.) The Chair- man knew all the affairs of the society. The meeting did not know what the directors had to contend with. A Voice: We all know enough now. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN: Order, please ; don't interrupt. A SHAREHOLDER: We know something more than the directors.</p>
        <p>Mr, RICHARD Cool( said he had a considerable stake in the concern, ' and he thought it would be wise to appoint the Chairman considering that they were pass- ing through this crisis. He seconded the motion which Mr. Critchley had submitted. The CH A/RMAN said Mr. Draper (solicitor) would put the vote to the meeting as he (the Chairman) was pro- posed amongst others. Mr. TRUSLOVR : Is the solicitor an investing mein- ber ? The CHAIRMAN: A heavy shareholder.</p>
        <p>Three scrutineers were then appointed.and voting re- sulted as follows :—Mr. Shaw, 86; Mr. Dallow, 78; Mr. Timperley, 83 ; and Mr. Kenworthy, 37. Mr. DRAPER announced that Messrs: Shaw Dallow, and Timperley were elected, the statement being re- ce fved with loud applause. The CHAIRMAN asked the shareholders if they were anxious to go on with the remainder of the business or leave it over. If he understood it rightly the feeling of the majority was that Mr. Woodcock and Mr. Brad- shaw should be the auditors, and as one was not an investing member they could not elect him that evening. Mr. SHAvy : Cannot I declare a share of mine to be transferred to Mr. Bradshaw?</p>
        <p>The CLIAIRM:tN.: No, It could not be registered.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. JONES said he was sorry Mr. Kenworthy had not be.en re-elected, and ha thought they were making u mistake in throwing him out. Perhaps one of the other directors would retire in his favour.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN (warmly) : I shall not allow that. Mr. JOHN CRITCHLEY : The Chairman ought to be hnpressed with- The CHAIRMAN :</p>
        <p>I am Chairman:</p>
        <p>Mr. CRITCHLEY : I think it is the greatest mistktke you ever made in not appointing Mr. Kenvrorthy. shall be pleased to retire in his favour.</p>
        <p>The CiIAIRMAN (to the meeting) : Will you elect the auditors now, or at the next meeting? Mr. TIMPERLEY proposed that the appointment be left over to the next meeting. Mr. GRIFFITHS seconded and the motion was carried. COMMITTEE OF INSPEC rioN APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH POTTER proposed "That a committee of inspection be appointed to have power to visit all the property and place a value upon it; to have power to examine all books and the accounts; to question all the officers and directors; and to report to the adjourned meeting of members as to the bgst method of dealing with the society's affairs; the committee to consist of Messrs. W. J. Shaw, S. W. Dallow, John Cooper, Richard Shackleton, Thomas Ince, Thomas Truslove, with power to add to their Humber.'</p>
        <p>Mr. HARRISON seconded, and remarked that, this was a most vital matter. The motion was carried unanimously. THE TREASURER RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. TIMPERLEY proposed the re-election of Mr. Aquila Cook as treasurer of the society. This was seconded by Mr. GRIFFITHS, and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ADJOURNED MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN COOPER moved the adjournment of the meeting, the date to be fixed by the special committee, and the auditor. Mr. YOUNG seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. HARRISON: Do you take it now that you have found out irregularities; the balance sheet is no good at all? Will there be a new one issued?</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: NO. The, irregularities do not affect the balance sheet except to a certain extent. It is practically accurate, and has been audited, but there are certain irregularities that have cropped up since which affect, to a certain extent, the balance sheet. Still it cannot alter the sheet, which is practically correct. Difficulties have arisen which will have to be investi- gated. A SHAREHOLDER: Do the irregularities affect the financial position of the society? The CHAIRMAN: I cannot answer any question in reference to that. I have been advised not to answer any question in referente to that, because of the diffi- culty which might arise. The motion for the adjournment of the meeting was carred unanimously. The proceedings which had occu- pied about two hours then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Union of Lancashire and Cheshire institutes.</head>
        <p>Union of Lancashire and Cheshire institutes.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Council was held at the offices, Deansgate, Manchester, on Tuesday, Mr. W. Hewitt presiding. The Examinations Committee presented a report Of.a conference of the examiners of modern languages and of representative teachers from institutions sending up candidates for examination in those subjects, together with the Examinations Com- znittee of the Council. They also reported that the total application for papers, was 55,745, an increase of 4,583 on any previous record. The increase ap- peared to be general; thus in commercial subjects there was an increase of 2,934, in languages 237, ix subjects of science and technology 436, an in domestic subjects 976.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
