      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the number of New Bankruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending March 28th, was 97. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 88, showing an in crease of 9, being a net increase in 1903, to date, of 13. - •</p>
        <p>On April 4 a new serial story will be commenced in the "Queen," entitled "Katherine Frensham," by Beatrice Harraden, the clever authoress of "Ships that Pass in the Night," and "The Fowler." In this new work of hers she undoubtedly forges another chain of strong sympathy between herself and her readers. It is a tale of modern life, with a mystic touch here and there. Powerfully written, it keeps up the interest well from page to page. The drift of the narrative is fore- shadowed in George Meredith's linos: To them it was revealed how they had found The kindred nature and the needed mind. They were two hungry souls that needed it. • •</p>
        <p>Dalkeith Palace, the Midlothian seat of the Duke of Bucclench, which is to be placed at the disposal of their Majesties during their visit to Scotland in May, is a more desirable place of residence than is Holyrood, which is both small and gloomy, and in Jose proximity to undesirable surroundings. It was at Dalkeith that Queen Victoria and Prince Albert stayed when they first visited Scotland in 1842 and the Queen referred in her "Journals" to her first acquaintance with the familiar Scottish fare of porridge of Finnan haddocks. On that oocasion the Royal party travelled by sea from the Thames to the Forth, in the yacht Royal George and the Queen held a Drawing Room at Dalkeith Palace. During the Royal visit next month, the State 'functions will be held at "Ancient Holyrood," and Dalkeith Palace will be entirely given over to the Royal Household. Servants from Buckingham Palace will be in charge, and horses and carriages sent down from the Royal Mews.</p>
        <p>•rt ..,tivin, k IPIR . :•':W '6 V. 4',. ~.'.''P A1&amp;.`1'.,V.. . , . ...., 4,- ... ,_ ......„ ~, 4. ? • it,„ .4,9 ----,, ..: •:. MODEL 18:10. *s..-K/f4- •/ •' / ' . ,:. -... . - e bi: / - 1 , . . .1 / 141)ILeader / .•::: *V,* The Best on the ,f,••••*; Market at the .sO// . *4,Ni Price. 1.,., s•••,‘• .. %, " ' ,•,,, . lei Selling in hut r . . 'Sk dredsevery day. _1•. ~,,,,,„,gi...ef .1,34. A This fact alone -,...4,1C _ , ~/ )..4.. ...1. speak sfor itself It .1,, • .r..:: pleases to perfection. Notice the wide .itSll 1 flare at the foot, and th9n.slillatexlLi, cut. Seams Vtie iiii Pracfusceectrienfugovert-ilkoohittgjil __albrd...'sereni.ing : • Serge 03 ack and Navy), neatly trimmed vith. ip, pif. Ss' Rows of Braid. Inverted Pleat at bacl,t, !...:.1.4 ..:1... site fastenings. JOHN NOBLE'S price on.y .„:r. :$4.: aiiii. carriage sd. ..1.1.4 LATEST CATALOGUE OF F.ASHICII - . • .:,,,.!-, ...... POST FREE. ~••••; • •,...-..5,,,,,,44. '.. ~,,,,,, - ...... ~.. . . . - -,...6..,... ' EVR 0' icir'''''4*) 47' —i- i.."- '-'4"-`::' '.:,•••4•1 412 k T.AA1L.....1 g",.;, ~. ~44. . .. •." . 'Um , ... ~,,, ...•... -.. , ~,, .</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>The riding aocident which the German Empress has ! sustained is fortunately not so serious as it might have , been, seeing that Her Majesty is no light weight to be I thrown from a horse. The Empress has long been engaged in a constant struggle to keep down her weight, I and not only takes a great deal of exercise, but has I undergone several" cures " with this object in view. She cares little for Court pageants, and her .chief interest irrlife lies in domestic affairs. A woman who I can cook a dinner, the Kaiser once said, is worth more I than a woman who can discuss political affairs. The , Kaiserin is however a splendid horsewoman, and is a I more graceful rider than the Emperor, who has been on more than once cession, pitched from his horse.' Lady Sybil PTimrose's wedding presents, which were arranged in the drawing‘rooms of Lord- Rosebery's house in London, were the most magnificent seen since the remarkable displayof Lady Helen Stavordales last I year. They included gifts from the King and Queen.! the Prince and Princess of Wales, and those from Lord Rosebery, and Lord Rothchilds, were exceedingly ' costly, and beautiful. But though the presents were as numerous, the wedding itself was in striking contrast to that of Lord Rosebery's younger daughter, who married Lord Crewe in 1899, at St. Margarets' Church, West- minster. King, and Duke of Cambridge were pre- sent at Lady "Peggy's" wedding, which evoked great popular interest, but that of Lady Sybil's was quietness and simplicity itself. So far all attempts of women to enter the legal pro- fession have been unsuccessful in this country. There was the case of Miss Margaret Hall, in Edinburgh, who claimed the right to qualify for admission into the In- corporated Society of Law Agents. That claim was rejected, as was also that of Miss Johnson.who, in 1891, sought to be admitted to the Irish Bar. The matter is now to be put to the test in London, where a lady has applied to be admitted a student of Grays Inn. The application is scarcely likely to be successful,but 'there is no doubt that the time will come when we shall have fully qualified women lawyers,as we haVe fully qualified women doctors. This is already the case in Canada and New Zealand, and it has been found in France that the admission of women to the Bar hai.- not been attended with any of those:evils which ar 3 always predicted for startling reforms. Mr. Henry Norman, M.P. who is an authority on the subject, thinks it quite possible to keep up a motor car. for £5O or £tlo a year. There are, he says, good makes on the market, that cost from £175 to £2OO, and these would be worth fully half their money after 3 years' wear. This means £33 a year for depreciation,and cost of petrol—allowing for 4,000 miles at 20 miles per Is gallon Mr. Norman puts at £lo.Then he estimates cost of tyres at £l5, lubricating oils and sundries at £lO, and licence and repairs at £7. Altogether about £75 a year, but from this Mr. Norman deducts £l5 for cab and railway fares saved, so that provided no extra cost is incurred for housing and washing, he puts the annual cost at about £OO, and even believes it could be done for £5O. Mr. Norman does not think it necessary to allow any- thing for fines or accidents, but though some motor enthusiasts find these items no inconsiderable addition to the expense, there is na doubt that the motor car is rapidly beooming a possibility to people of compare., Lively small means. One object of the All-Linea ball held the ether thy at the Ulster Hall, Bel trst, was charity, yet another, to popularise linen as a dress fabric. Beyond the condition that nothing but linen was to be worn, no rules were laid down for the women, • and the most exquisite creations:appear-ed. Regulation evening dress—with a linen vest—was permitted for the men, but many donned entire linen suits, both fancy and plain. I The most beautiful colon/lugs are to be had this spring in the new washing linens, and those "silk surfaced" will unquestionably enjoy a great vogue for garden party and out-door Nressing " gowns. For 'harder wear, linens of the coarse crash kind are ava'—lable, and hese wash and wear splendidly. Raised linen spra vs of ttlowers which are sold ready to applique on to linen gown:, are finding much favour with modistes just now, so also are the coloured linen pions worked with a pattern in flourishing thread. Washing poplin is a novelty, and one likely to be much appreciated. It has a soft silky surface, and neither crushes or tumbles i</p>
        <p>Once upon a time we donned only flowers inour headgear that were in season, then we began to be tre- vious, and looked fully a season ahead, .nowadays we take no heed of the seasons, and wear autumnal flowers in the spring, and summer blooms in the winter, and vice versa. Just now there is quite a craze for poppies, both large and small, and in colours quite unknown to Nature. Fruit, which the milliners usually reserve for autumn is appearing on some of the smartest hats and • toques, the favourite of the moment being clusters of j j small Alpine strawberries, with their small white flowers and foliage. The new narrow toque that scoops Over the face is by no means so becoming to the average face as the round ti at variety, and if we are wise we shall leave the idea 1 to our French sisters whom it suits particularly well. The wide round toque is being worn tilted over the, face in front, and bent down Oightly over the hair behind, but if preferred it can he worn straight, raised by a I bandeau a little on the left side. Lace draped picture hats are much seen with the picturesque type of dress- ing; and these are almost always in black, the ends be- hind being considerably shorter than has been. the case for some time past, barely now covering the hair. Mr. C. L. Brownell's " The Heart of Japan (Meltruen 65.) brings' one " near enough to native life to hear the heart beat." Mr. Brownell spent five years in the in- terior of this keenly interesting country, living as the natives live, and this knowledge of his subject,together with his own charming, unaffected manner, results in a most delightful, and instructive book. The picture he gives US of the little wife, of Toyo San, is pathetic to a degree, but others al, in lighter vein. The "Heart of Japan" is a book ffot to be read once but many times.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-E)AY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-E)AY.</p>
        <p>It is too early in the season yet to indulge in very elaborate or thin toilettes, and the requirements of the moment are satisfied by smart tailor-made gowns with skirts of a useful walking length. The smartest cos- tumes of this style are made with a tight- fitting double-breasted coat with a loose front, the sides and back being held in to the figure by a shaped belt. The most fashion- able skirt which accompanies this coat is plain and well cut, and does not fit very tightly .over the hips, but flows out straight from the band at the back. _</p>
        <p>Frieze, serge, and cloths are the materials par exce'lence at present, and occasionally</p>
        <p>the seventy of the costumes which they con- stitute is broken by some military braiding in wide widths and many buttons either large or small as preferred. The costume illustrated is expressed in a soft tone of mauve frieze, and is trimmed very little, . only a narrow cording or piping of pale green silk appearing at the seams while the collar is of blurred chine silk with a cream ground and a floral pattern in mauve and green. The toque worn is of Parma violets, with a crown of the leaves of the violets alone.</p>
        <p>For an Easter Sunday such a toilette com- pleted by delicate cre m kid gloves and a blouse of cream silk and lace worn underneath the smart Coat would prove ideal. It is remarkable, however, how easily the eye can be trained to admire a combination of colours which" had previously been con- sidered hideous. A few- years ago a blue and green toilette would have occasioned a shudder of horror among all women of taste and fashion, and yet nowadays it is considered as both pretty and smart, and is much in request both for gowns and hats. Nature has set the --fashions as she intermingles one colour with another in the most reckless fashion, and yet there is nothing to offend the eye even in the most gorgeous contrasts.</p>
        <p>• e V lir, • -,...p... ,r,....,-t, -...., ~.-,,,7,., Li), ~,.. 4.....'4, -.4. , , - rv.•,,i5,.., ..- ~. --.' ......T..,.,re.,,,,,, i, , • ../7 ,r.;;:,,, ~ _,__T. =._ --- - ~ ------, , ---..7-----, .-- ," .: B. 1 -17---'--- r-n . 1 _ NN. \ . i I,' ~ , '—..-----------------, ~, i r t . . 1 1 • \V\ / A SMART TOILETTE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SPRING HAT.</head>
        <p>SPRING HAT.</p>
        <p>Green in delicate ades is highly fashion- able in the realms of millinery, whether Used simply as a trimming or forming the hat itself. In the last case it takes the form of satin straw which is bent and curved into shape to suit the wearer, or tulle or taffetas go to make daintY toques of smart and fashionable aspect. The hat illustrated, however, is made of a light shade of biscuit straw, with the brim which Curves back from the face bound with emerald green velvet, while four tiny wings of the same tone of green spring from a rosette of the straw itself arranged at the left side. The crown, which is broad and very low, is trimmed only by a twist of 'green tulle. The. hat depends, of course, entirely on its shape for its smartness, and nowadays there are no two hats alike, for every modiste makes a speciality, of some particular mode, and in- variably has the shapes made in her work- rooms, under her personal supervision to suit the face of the future wearer.</p>
        <p>That is why some of the exceedingly simple- looking hats have such an exceedingly long price, for the triinming itself is one of the least important points in its valuation, the originality of the style accounting for its expensiveness. The high-class milliner rarely buys ready-made shapes from the manufac- turer, as she finds her customers prefnr a hat made to suit their individuality.</p>
