        <p>, • 1.,.•, , •-,-- , • i \, '! \ ••••.*'-.,, • , i .'":•:•.,a 1 l' A ; '• / , ,:.-•:..ez4 , i / • -.0;., , • , ''...;.1.i.,4„ • fiot t, tel ~' lig , E . Itl \t , . , / ~. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>NEAT COATEE.</head>
        <p>A NEAT COATEE.</p>
        <p>The neat - little coatee sketched is of pale putty-coloured cloth, strapped with silk • of the same sliade. It •would suit equally well for morning wear with a plain tailor-made skirt or for cycling, for in spite of the popu- larity of motoring, the cycle is still popular even amongst those who can afford the more expensive means of conveyance. It .is only the very well-to-do who can afford to risk a large sum of money, _not to mention themselves, in a motor-car—winch, no C-iubt, has its charms, but does not give the same amount of healthful exercise as an easily running cycle of the latest build. A very smart cycling dress. I recently saw was made of coarse oatmeal-coloured ,Irish 'frieze with strapped seams. The coat was short and" loose in front, the back .and sides being held in to the waist by a shaped band of silk fastened in front by a dull gold buckle; slides to match the • buckle kept the band down at the sides. - -</p>
        <p>A quantity of small gold buttons brightened up the front of the coat, the collar of which was of bright reddish brown silk covered with coarse twine-coloured lace. It was com- pleted by a simple hat of Panama straw, 'trimmed with a scarf of the same tone as the silk on the collar, fastened at one side by a gold buckle,</p>
        <p>COQUETTEIJ</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, April 3, 1903. BAD NEWS FOR RATEPAYERSI I</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 3, 1903. BAD NEWS FOR RATEPAYERSI I</p>
        <p>Startling Increase of Rates for Educa- tional Purposes.</p>
        <p>The working of the new Education Act in War- rington is likly to prove very expensive to the.. ratepayers. The estimates of the probable ex- penditure for educational requirements in Warrington during the ensuing twelve months were laid before the members of the Education Coinmittee at a specially convened meeting held at the Town Hall on Fridry. The estimates had been carefull. prepared by the Borough Treasurer (Mr. J. Fairhurst- in conjunction with the Director of Education (Mr Murray) and the large sum required came as an unpleasant surprise to the members of the Committee, especially considering the present state of trade in the town. That there would be a substantial addition to the rates for educational expenditure was gener- ally anticipated, but few were prepared for a probable increase of rates amounting to is. 2d in the 1. - Mr. F. MONKS was voted to the chair and there was a representative attendance. The Borough Treasurer submitted an estimate for the year ending 31st March, 1904. The amount required may be summarised as follows :</p>
        <p>Secondary and Technical Instruction Elementary Educati0n...........</p>
        <p>£4,546 129,828</p>
        <p>Total ... The estimated receipts are as follows Secondary and Technical Education .. Elementary Education ... ... • •</p>
        <p>34.374</p>
        <p>£2,325 £16,600</p>
        <p>Total ... Deficiency to be raised by Rate</p>
        <p>115,449</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>34,374</p>
        <p>From the above figures it will be seen that the amount to be paid out of the ratepayers pockets is £15,449. This deficiency includes a sum of £2,193 which has in previous years been chargeable against the borough rate for school attendance and other purposes,so that the nett increase for educational purposes is £13,256. Presuming that the improvement rate, poor rate and sewers rate, which last year amounted to Gs. 6d. in the £ are the same this year, the increase upon the rates will be is. 2d. in the £, making a total rate of is. Bd. in the 1.</p>
        <p>It may be stated that there is one crumb of com- fort for the sorely taxed ratepayer. The deficiency includes an abnormal sum of £8,250 which will not recur next year, and is only included in this year's estimate owing to the Govern- ment not paying the grants earned during the finan- cial year. This sum of £8,250 represents a rate of about ninepence in the pound so that next year's rate will be smaller. It must, however, be borne in mind that new schools probably, three in number, will have to be erected, and nothing is included in this year's estimate for new schools. It is therefore Almost safe to predict that the rate for educational purposes for 1905 will be Bd. in the £ against the increase of is. 2d. this year.</p>
        <p>w - It was decided by the committee to have their first financ'..al meeting under the order on Thursday, the 2nd of Aptil,at six pm.</p>
      </div>
