      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Thelwall Nuisance.</head>
        <p>Thelwall Nuisance.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 have reld your. note on this question in your last Saturday's issue and you seem to tAke the only common sense view of the position of the Runcorn Rural District Council. This Council has now before it two special reports,by distinguished experts, prepared at the public expense. No. 1 is by Dr. John Penny, M.B.D.Sc. (Public Health) a Fellow of the Royal Society of Edinburgh &amp;c. No. 2, is by Professor Sheriden Delepine, 'Director of the Public Health Laboratory, Manchester. Both these gentlemen are men of science, and honourable members of the medical profession who are highly competent to 43,xpress opinions, which we are bound to receive with respect. The reports,however, differ so materially that any one reading the two together must come to the conclusion, that if both are correct they must deal with entirely different conditions. .</p>
        <p>• I do not wish to fully analyse these reports, at present, but simply to give a single instance of their divergent views. lst.—Dr. Penny states :—" Yeast, or brewery ' effluent, is one of the most objectionable known,and this mixed with the waste liquors from soakes, tan- pits, Scc., in my opinion must produce an effluvium nuisance, having all the characters of the smell com- plained of when let off itito any tank or drain." 2nd.—Professor Delepine states :—"I was quite unable to detect an 3 effluvium or irritating vapour in connection with the yeast tan-pit, nor with the treated or untreated effluent into which some of the spent liquor found its way." Dr. Adams, the Council's own Medical Officer, seems to favour Dr. Penny's conclusions, for here are his findings. ist.—"lt was an undoubted fact that there had been a lot of sickness in this particular neigbour- hood." 2nd.—lt was also an undoubted fact that the con- dition of affairs at the tannery was not what it ought to be." 3rd.—" He did not think they would get such an effluent as he now submitted from any other Tanneries." ' I leave these doctors to set,tle their own differences and your readers to draw their own conclusions.— Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>R. A. NAYLOR, St. Miehmls House, Egerton Gardens, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>WARRINGTON GYMNASIUM,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GYMNASIUM,</p>
        <p>Children's Drill and Gymnastic Display.</p>
        <p>Alderman Franconth and Compensation</p>
        <p>to Publicans.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln an address at a Conservative Meeting at Padgate Alderman Fraucomb referred to the difficult question of compensation in the case of a publican de- prived of his license because the magistrates thought that the licensed house was not required. I am disposed , to agree with the Alderman that compensation should I be given, at any rate the claim ought to be fairly and justly considered. It is true the publican has no legal right, his license being only given for one year, and this was known to him when he took the house, and he is reminded of it year by year as the license is renewed At the same time there is something in the plea that custom gives a kind of vested interest, and it has been the custom for a considerable period only to refuse the renewal of the license when the trust has been in some wayabused. Where I differ from Mr. Francomb is in the source from which the compensation should come. He considers the ratepayers ought to compensate. Mr. Fraucomb's own words suggest the proper source. He says, "he believed it would be a good thine for the sake of the publican to reduce the number oehouses. In some areas there were six where three would be sufficient—the other three on starvation profits. If the number of houses was reduced the same amount of trade would be doae." If justice is appealed to, and the demand for ,compensation put forward on behalf of the man who suffers by the loss of his license (if the profits are at " starvation " point there is little or no claim) by the same principle, if by the action of the magistrates representing the ratepayers , the custom goes to the remaining houses " So that ' the same amount of trade is done," then is it not reason- able that those whose profits are enhanced in value, by the closing of a neighbouring house should compen- sate the one who has to suffer? The ratepayers' burden is heavy enough already without adding to it the recouping of an individual loss, which on Alderman Francomb's showing is no loss to the trade as a whole. What is nowknown as Lord Peel's Commission reported that if compensation was paid at all it should be paid by those who get the benefit of the diverted' trade, or that in lieu of compensation a seven years' notice should be given to licence holders, after which time a reduc- tion should be made on a definite scale. There was also the suggestion, that annual rentals should be charged for licenses, and that these shouldaceumulate towards a compensation fund. In regard to the big Brewery Companies controlling a great number of licenses, such would probablywelcome, rather than otherwise,the enforced closing of the houses which are not needed, are doing badly; and are, to use the Alderman words, "at starvation profits." The con- centration'of business by the transfer of the trade to other houses effects an economy of expenditurewhich is a clear gain. And the chief reason for the retention of these houses "at starvation profits" is that the surrender of them may be used in a kind of barter to induce magistrates to give new licenses to largo premises in other localities, say by the surrender of two or it may be three for one. Penketh, March 2.lth, [903.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Suggested High-Leve? Bridge at Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Suggested High-Leve? Bridge at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Among the various proposals for rentedyine the increasing delays arising at Stockton Heath Swing Bridge, I do not remember to have yet heard of anyone publicly advocating the early construction of a high- level bridge, on the Cantilever or other system, so, with your permission, lam opening-up the subject trusting that it may dead to useful discussion, among those in- terested in the development of Stockton Heath district. Having seen so many So-called 'Visionary schemes" become practical and accomplished facts, I do not at all mind either criticism or ridicule--more especially as I have thought out the subject very carefully, in my leisure time, before venturing to submit my views to your readers. Very well, there is what George Eliot calls an inevitable law of consequences; and we have, at Stockton Heath, two distinct, powerful, and perma- nent sets of " consequence's," working together—in what I would term an ever-increasing momentum of anta- gonism : (a) We have, on the Cheshire side of the Canal, a phenomenal growth of what may be described as a. . nomadic population of Warrington and district busi- nessmen, travelling daily, and sometimes several . times a day, between their businesses and their residences. (b) Concurrent with the set of circumstances summarised under section "a," we have a marked growth of traffic, in the Ship Canal, resulting in more and more demand upon the Stoc!;ton Heath Swing Bridge, and with the probability, before us, of this tendency increasing, almost every month,for many future years. To the busy business-man, almost every moment of the working-day. is preeious, and the frequent delay at the Stockton Heath Swine?' Bridge, coupled, as it often is, with the "double event" (that is, the Railway: gates being closed, at Wilderspool Causeway) must, in the aggregate, be a source of serious loss and inconvenience in the course of a year. I believe that there is only one remedy worth considering, and that is, the early con- struction of ahigh-levelbridge. Without pretending to any technical engineering knowledge, allow me to sub- mit a solution of this bridge problem, just as it strikes a plain man in the street : ' My idea would be to leave the Wilderspool Causeway, and the Stockton Heath Swing Bridge, entirely as they i are now; and also to leave the "New Road," and the "Stag" Swing Bridge alone, my object being to follow alhe line of least resistance, and to save all unnecessary cost in construction. A little way down the present "New Road," my suggestion would be to strike-out, on the left-hand side, a wide thoroughfare (capable of meeting future requirements of traffic) working along- side of7or actually in,the riverbed; and utilising,as far as possible, the soil of old Ship Canal embankment, as part of the necessary material for gradient. By start- ing well towards the Warringten end of the "New Road," we should get a reasonably easy, gentle gradient (something similar to the easy rise between Arpley Station and Latchford Station) up to the required height over the Ship Canal. Then, on the Cheshire side, to secure a comfort- able gradient, we could build part of our high-level bridge across Ellesmere-road, exactly as was done over Fairfield-road, in the case of the Latchford Cantilever bridge; but I think, that, with forethought, we could make our Stockton Heath gradients far more easy than the Latchford ones. There appears to be enough un- built land, at the back of Ellesmere-road, tO carry our new gradient, cheaply, well into Stockton Heath vil- lage, and to join conveniently into the existing London- road. A high-level bridge, such as I suggest, would relieve the congestion which,even though already considerable, is actually only beginning its troubles at Stockton Heath swing bridge; it would open out the residential health resort of Stockton Heath to the masses as well as to the classes; it would solve the electric-car prob- lem, in regard to making a complete car-circuit between Warrington, Latchford, and Stockton Heath; and also in regard to connecting, by electric-car,—Warrington with Stretton, Antrobus, and other local Cheshire resi- dential plaices; and it would further bring the North- wich Light Railway System within easy possibility of stretching across to Warrington side of the canal. In- deed, it might even go so far as to induce the postal authorities to let, us post letters at Stockton Heath till say 10 at night (the morning postal service is admir- able) ; and might bring us a branch-line from one of the great railways. There is no limit to the possibili- ties of development of Stockton Heath, when once the problem of the ditch or open sewer (as some of our Liverpool friends unkindly term it) is solved by a per- manent bridge, at a high-level, and a low gradient. So far, I have been assuming forethought and economy as governing the question of a high-level bridge—but, the probability is, that the matter will be shelved for several years, unless public opinion is aroused. If deferred, for any length of time, it will cost as much to acquire land and property as would, under present con- elitions, build the bridge. Sooner or later, a high-level structure is simply unavoidable, then why not have an ,expert survey, and acquire the necessary land and pro- perty, before inflated values set in, even if the gradient and bridge are not actually commenced just yet? The question of adjusting proportion of cost, as between Warrington Corporation and the Ship Canal Company, I am just mentioning—as a record—as a point for mutual arrangement. With nearly thirty years..oxperience: of financial</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 3, 1903.</p>
        <p>present, there , being quite a lull More the Eister holiday. The Jolly Anglers' Society at their last meeting, on Monday evening, decided to enter four teams in the Shield Competition in connection with the Anglers' Association. The Comidttee of the Association met last night and some important busi- ness was transacted. Applications for members con- tinue to be steady and well up to the average of 'past years. The boisterous weather, which prevailed last week-end -greatly interfered with angling operations and little worthy of mention was recorded. The General Wolfe A.S., the Hersey A.S. and the Working- men's A. A. meet on M mday evening and the Persever- ance A.S. will meet on Tuesday. 'Angling prospects for Easter are fairly good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. • " By Ovalist.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. • " By Ovalist.</p>
        <p>We have entered upon th last month of the foot- ball season, and yet not one of- the Northern Union championships are decithd. In this respect the sea- son is proving remarkably Successful, the sustained interest in competition games being a feature which appeals not only to the crowd, but through them to the ?lub,managers. The Warrington F.C. enters upon • the closing scene with prospects of a mach brighter finish than at one time seemed ' possible. The Club should_ at - least fill its aocuStOuael position in the middle of the League, and it is almost certain now that the aggregate of points gained in League matches will eventually exceed any previous total in the history lof the Club. There are yet four matches at home and I two away.</p>
        <p>The fishing' in the Bridgewater Canal, from Broad- heath to Preston Brook has been let to the Worsley Canal Fishery Beard. Arrangements are in progress whereby the anglers of Warrington and district as a body, will be enabled to participate in the privileges of fishing that water. • It is expected that these , i arrangements m ents will be cople'ed for the opening of the Last Saturday Hanslet received their quietus at I coarse,' fishing season in June. . W.arrington, and the victory more than atonect-f ,r the reverse Warringtoii sustained at Parkside on . Harriers. . November 29th. Sixteen points to seven is the best I thing in the way of scoring the Warrington team has I On Saturday Snaky Harriers finished their cross- done at Wilderspool during the present season, and I country season with a five-and-aAialf miles handicapl from the headquarters of the club, Black Horse Hotel, on this occasion it barely represents the superiority I Sankey Bridges. - The trail was laid by Potts and a of the homesters, whose opponents would have looked i very small indeed but for Goldthorpe's emple of goals. I novice, over three miles of heavy cotratry, and then Warrington were again well represented except for- i on t 1 Liverpool high road, for the remaining distance. ward, and the fine form of the backs delighted the i After a fast race the C iptain, T. 'Devlin, who started crowd. The whole of the four dies came from the front scratch, won with comparative ease, by a hun- three-quarters, whose display was super-excellent. t dred yards ; S. Parsonage (45 seconds start), second; They ran and passed with the utmost speed judg. I John Riley (4)),. third. . The following also ran : inent, • and precision, and the way the. scoring i Green (15 seconds), P. Wilding (45 sec)ads), F. was accomplished would have been an object lesson tc , Cowap (2min.),- - • Warren (2min. 45sees.), J. Princep 99 Rugby clubs in 100. The Hunslet backs were I (3mins.), A. Cleave • (3inins. 30secs.), Joseph Perdue quite incapable of coping with the combination of the (3min. 30secs.), R. Allen (smin.) After the race the home men, and but for a big advantage forward the Secretary (Ur. •E. Hodkinson) presented the medal visiting team must have got an awful trouncin. s for good attendance and championship honours. cr 'There was only one regret present in the minds of the The first was won by L. Green, second by John Riley, home . supporters, and tnat was in respect third, P. Wilding. The club gave six medals for to the the weakness of the home front rank attendance, and these were won by L. Green, first ; -, who were quite • outelassed in the pack, as John. Riley, second ; P. Wilding, third Warren,' they have been throughout the cam • , nt fourth ; Harrison, fifth ; and Fairhurst, sixth. with a few solitary exceptional occasions. The _ _ _ _ _ pas</p>
        <p>misfortune of the Club in so seldom being able to put a complete back team in th,3 field was also much com- mented upon. How much better would the club's record have been had Fish, Isherwood, and Dickenson</p>
        <p>not been suffering from enforced idleness on so many occasions? They are only •iust getting familiar with</p>
        <p>• ~ one another's play—at the fag-end of the season.</p>
        <p>The play on Saturday was, of course, very attractive It was also sensational, for the crowd experienced the uncommon—though pleasant—shock of seeing the home team score two trios in the first five minutes. Fish raced down from centre, circrenvente,d the full back, and scored almost before the teams had got into ship-shape. Hunslet came up smiling, however, only to show signs of despondency the next minute, when Harris took advantage of Edmund's neat cross-kick and got in at the other corner. Warringtonians were naturally on good terms with themselves, and the stand simply reverberated with audible . smiles and -clitaff at the expense of the Hunslet officials. Things took a slight change very soon after, and the smile passed on to the faces of the visi- tors. A piece of good, combined running took the "whites" to the home line,and from the first "scrum" Burnett forced his way over despite the clutches of the lightly-built Warrington halves. Goldthorpe. could not manage a goal against the wind, but suffi- cient anxiety was imparted to the palpitating and patriotic breasts of the local/enthusiasts. It was not very far from half-time, and visions of a constant stream of dropped goals over the Warrington cross-bar danced before the distorted vision of those who looked ahead. .The interval really did arrive with Hunslet only a try behind, thanks to the inability of the home forwards to get the ball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <p>There was a distinct air of superiority abont.the visiting officials as the second half commenced with a gale behind the versatile "Albert" and his men. Nobody had any doubt that those dropped goals would material- ise—and they did, some of them. But Warrington were not idle in the meantime. The wind helped the oval through the tired legs of the home van-guard, and ' once it got among the backs progress was made. Duckworth made a brilliant break-away from the "scrum" and passed to Dickeason. The latter, ran nearly to touch and then, as the ball was expected to pass on to Harris, who had a fine onening, the tricky centre doubled inside and ran tinder the cross-bar amid mighty cheers, which were redoubled as Fish kicked a goal. The aforesaid smile again changed ivarters.l Men " Ahr Albert" dropped a g )11. "But that didn't matter," for War4figton participated in another swin,in, run which ended in Fish filleting Place and sprinting round the goal, WILIt time he cast sweet glances to :the rear where his would-be captors were putting forth great efforts. The scorer also, kicked a, second goal, and everybody felt supremely indifferent as to what Ffunslet did subsequently. Goldthorpe dropped another goal, just to keep the pot boiling, and then the referee mercifully drew the veil over the , scene of Hunslet's downfall.</p>
        <p>It is gratifying to see that the facilities for physical culture afforded by the Warrington Gymnasium are being largely taken advantage of by the rising genera- tion. In addition to the school children attending the classes as a part of their usual instruction, many boys and girls have been enrolled as members and thus have extra advantage of cultivating physique. If any evidence of the good work done by the institution were needed, it was certainly forthcoming on Monday evening when a large number of the children who have had the benefit of Mr. J. Kinsley's valuable instruction gave •several drills and a gymnastic display. There was a large attendance of the public who thoroughly appreciated the capital programme submitted. Mr. E. DRAPER presided and a,t the outset spoke of the useful purpose which the inititution serves. What a benefit it was to a young man; in the dull, dreary miserable evenings, to go there and indulge in a pleas- ant and healthy recreation. He had been connected with the institution for some years. He had pig done very much in the way of gymnasties,but he had olserved the effects of the . attendance on others and in some caSes they were positively startling. Nothing should appeal to them more strongly than an institution which had for its object the making of the youngsters to grow up to be healthy boys and girls instead of comparative weaklings. He hoped public interest in the Gymnasium would increase, and it was certainly a pleasure to know that the Gymnasium had more members now than at any time since its formation. The number of members on the books was men 63, ladies 15, boys 46, girls 52, or a, total of 176. Last season the numbers were—men 42, ladies four, boys 29, girls 17, total 95 ; or an increase of 84. There were also 1,707 boys who attended last season on Saturday nights; this season, up to Saturday last, they had had 1,879, or an increase of 190, and they had eight weeks before the season ended. There were also upwards of 3,500 school children attending weekly. Last year the total attendance at all classes was 68,949 ; this season from September Ist to Saturday last, for seven months. the attendance had been 90,003. (Applause.) Notwithstanding the lack of support, the Gymnasium was going ahead through sheer merit, and lie trusted that all present wOuld do their best to increase an interest in it, as it .was doing an excellent work in the town. (Applause.) The children then proceeded with their entertainment which proved exceedingly enjoyable. Girls as well as boys seemed thoroughly at home in the various physical tests and it was really surprising to find • many of the girls quite adepts at rope climbing and high-jumping. The drills were executed with precision and called forth hearty applause. A novel item was the skipping ring . for girls. The programme concluded with a hand-ball -- match • for the boys, who entered into the contest with To-day, Warrington are due at Wigan, where they great zest. Mr. Kinsley must he congzatnlated upon should have a good chance of victory. It is essential the admirable results of his training and the efficient that the team should win this engagement if they are manner in which he directed the exercises. to have any voice in the destination of the South During the eveniag the CHAIRMAN distributed medals West Lancashire cup, which is at present being kept to the following :—Girls : 1, Marion Jolley; 2,Winnie bright by Mr. Neil. BY beating Wigan they will have Lawton ; 3, A. Furnival ; 4, Bessie Lee; 5. Mary Jones. . • a chance of equalling Runcorn's record, and a deciding Ashcroft ;4, Arthur Davenport. Men: 1 C.;Sinclair Boys: 1, Gordon Fitchett ; 2, Fred Jolley 3 , Percy battle should then be necessary. A defeat to-day , ; 2. A. Parr • 3. T. McWhirter ; 4, H. Holloway; 5, 0. means that Runcorn secure the cup, so there is every Corbett ; 6'. Alf Sinclair ; 7, Arthur Sinclair; and 8, T. incentive for Warrington to put forth their best Blower. , efforts. Councillor TENNION, in proposing a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Draper. for his attendance, said the Gym- I The following week will be .a busy and momentous !lasi= was doing a great amount of good unertbe I one for Warrington—and some • Other elubs also! agency of the Corporation, and they were very proud to Ai:bit:ix are due on ay, an t , , . d Good Friday, dhe ‘Varrinaton have such an institution. The committee were greatly indebted to gentlemen like Mr. Draper for their active , men have already promised them a licking. The support. Mr. Draper had assisted them in various ways, ; destination of the League championship is largely de- and he did not think there was anyone in Warrington pendent on this matcla, for a slip by the leaders will who had the interests of physical training and physical put Salford ahead. Given fine weather there should' education more at heart than had their Chairman. be a mighty tussle. On Saturday and Monday Widnes The Rev. W. BuxcEcimr seconded, and the motion and Wigin are due, so that Warrington not Only stand was carried with acclamation, a chance of getting six, League points, bat o.f infiu- Mr. DRAPER, in seconding, said he would he glad to continue to assist the Gymnasium in every possible encing the destination of two championships. The direction, week-end games conclude Warrington's S.W. I •incashire programme and will thus be additionally . , interesting if the team gets through to-day.</p>
