      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>ELECTRICITY DEVELOPMENTS AT WARRINGTON. £2OOOO MORE TO BE SPENT. Inquiry at the Town Hall.</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY DEVELOP- MENTS AT WARRINGTON. £2OOOO MORE TO BE SPENT. Inquiry at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON ASKS A FEW QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>The subject .o f the proposed additions to the plant at the electric light station at Howley was under discus- sion at a Local Government Board inquiry held In the Town Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday morning. Mr. F. _ . .</p>
        <p>H. TitHoch, M. Inst. C. E., condticted the inquiry on behalf of the Board. Amongst those present were the Mayor (Mr. J. C. Parr), Alderman Monks (chairman of the Electricity and Tramways Committee), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Pemberton, Lord and Brophy. Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk),Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. T. Longdin (borough engineer and surveyor), Mr. Grimsdale (electrical engineer), Mr. Matthias (chief assistant), Mr. J. H. Woodward (Messrs. Preece and Oardew.) OPENING STATEMENT BY THE DEPUTY TOWN</p>
        <p>CLERK. Mr. Wirr.sos explained that the application was made under the Warrington Ele,ctric Lighting Order,lB9B,which empowered the Corporation (with the consent of the Local Government Board) to borrow money for supply- ing the town with electric light and power. The Local Government Board had already sanctioned the borrow- ing of £59,916, the whole of which had been taken up and the money spent in erecting the generating station and the provision of plant and mains for the supply of current. The whole of the compulsory area had been supplied, and the present application for £20,000 was ' for extensions to the generating plant so as to enable the Corporation to meet the growing requirements of the town. The dernandfor current had far exceeded all expectations, and in addition the Corporation had established a traMway system. That would be proved by Mr. Grimsdale, the electrical engineer to the Cor- poration, w!io would give in detail the necessity for the present application. EVIDENCE OF THE ELECTRICAL ENGINEER.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. GRIAISDALE was then called and stated that at the present time the plant at present installed at the station at Howley was: Steam dynamos, No. 1, 100 kwts. ; No. 2, 250 ditto ; No. 3, 250 ditto on trac- tion; and No. 4, 500 ditto; making a total of 1,100 kwts.' or 850kwts. available for lighting load. Boilers: No. 1, hand fired, 6,0001 b. evaporation: No. 2, destruc- tor, ditto ; No. 3, hand fired, ditto • No. 4, hand fired, ditto ; No. 5, destructor, ditto ; and No. 6, hand fired, ' B.ooolb. (all evaporation), making a total of 38,0001 b. Alllowing one destructor down for. cleaning each week and all hand fired boilers in working order, there was available for steaming 32,0001 b. per hour, or in the. event of one hand fired boiler being down for repairs 26,0001 b. per hour. The maximum load observed this winter was on November 2.lth,and amounted to 762kwts., of which 196kwts. was for traction and 536kwts. for lighting, which was taken up on No. 4 500kwt. machine, leaving a stand-by of 350kwts. to make up a load of 566kwts. in the event of a breakdown, in which case a great part of the consumers would have to be cut off until the necessary repairs were made. On this date during heavy load, Nos. 1,2, 3, 4 and 6 boilers took up the load. The normal evaporation of these boilers was 32,0001 b. per hour, and the steam required for 762kw1s. at 451 b. per kwt. hour including all losses was 34,2901 b. per hour. The only spare boiler was No. 5; which could not be used, as it is necessary to clean the destructor boilers after one week's run and they could not be shut down and cleaned and started up under three days, besides which on no ocbasion this winter had they had sufficient refuse to keep going two destructors. The above figures were worked out on the maximpm load observed, the average maximum load for the heavy load period being about 740kwts. The num- ber of lamps was as follows :—At time of startin.,l,979 Sc p.; 31st March, 1902, 14,518 do.; 31st March, 1903, 23,527 do. The above figures were motors and lamps in 3 c.p. equivalent. Besides the above, three works .were supplied with power as below :—Ono of 130 horsepower; one of 50 horse power; and one of 40 horse power; or a total of 220 horse power, 164kwts. •MR. PEIVIBERTON'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON asked the electrical engineer how often the load exceeded 500 kwts ? WITNES'S : On four occasions in the winter. Councillor PEMBERTON : The engines are not likely to break down? _</p>
        <p>7-WITNE3s: I have never seen an engine yet that did not need repairs. Councillor T'Eminurros : I don't think we ought to have a double amount of power stinding by because of the risk of breaking down. The INSPECTOR : It is only a question of having the work done now or in 15 months' time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE. TTS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE. TTS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE</p>
        <p>STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BOWELS. SEAWEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, far greater than other known remedies. This is now conceded by important authori- ties. It was first introduced by Mr. Veno. and Veno's Seaweed Tonic is the only remedy in the world contain- ing seaweed, for that reason Veno's Seaweed Tonic is used in hospitals and by doctors themselves because of its superior curative properties. Its most brilliant effect is produced in stomach, liver aml kidney diseases, and the eKtrnordinary cures it performs, even in the worst cases, is positive proof of its efficacy. It is a god-send to sufferers from indigestion, wind, headache, general weakness, kidney trouble, weak and painful back, torpid liver, female troubles, poorness of blood, and HABITUAL CONSTIPATION. Ask for VENO'S SEA- NEED TONIC. Prices is. 1-!.d. and 2s. 9d. at chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
