      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>COLLIER'S TROUBLE..-</head>
        <p>A COLLIER'S TROUBLE..-</p>
        <p>A man whose work causes him to bend, and who "suffers from backache, must have a cruel life of suffer.- A collier in such a condition is pitiable. Thou- sands of workers Who have to bend and stoop, suffer. It is agony, every time they move. William Lavison, of North Seaton Colliery, Northum- berland, suffered from pain in hack and loins. When he stooped for any length of time he had the greatest difficulty in getting straight again. In such work it was agony. To make 'matters worse, Mr. Levison's digestion went wrong, the food he ate did not agree with him, his appetite went, and work became a burden to him..</p>
        <p>That is what he says. Every symptom he had was a symptom of kidney disease. Treating each symptom as a disease, didn't lhelp—he - wasn't treating the cause. But .when he did treat the cause, the kidneys, with a sure cure for ailing kidners; Dodd's Kidney Pills, he was cured, and has not suffered in any way since. Yo u should apply this experienceto your own case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Trap Accident, On Tuesday nylrning, Mr. William Greenwood, 40, Baxter-street, was driving along Liverpool-road in a trap when he. was thrown out of the vehicle and sus- tained injuries to the head and side. Mr. Greenwood, who is 62 years of age, was conveyed to the Infirmary and detained in the institution.</p>
        <p>"Christianity and Pane." This was the subject ot au addr,3s; heti at the Friends' Meeting •House on Sunday by Dr. E. Vipont Brown, of Manchester. There was a large attendance, the meeting being opened with prayer by Mr.AshwOrth of Manchester. Dr. Brown dealt wttlt hi subject in .an interesting manner and pointed out the foolishness of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-03_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <p>The Toreador. M. George Edwards' Coinomv in this cele)ratei musical play commence an engtgetn.ent it the Royal' Court Theatre, Warrington, on Monday next April 6th, for five nights ; with a sacred concert on Good Friday. Radiantly attractive, with a fond of humour, and many bright musical numbers, the first visit of " The Toreador" cannot fail to attract large audiences; while the dernand for (~ats:atrSfeisrs. Pigot',i, indict tes that this -I.nusital play Will add one more to its li of unbroken successes. The company visiting Warring- ton is a straw one with it powerful chorus, while the orcheitra is to be c7m3iderably augtneate'l r the visit. Concert in Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>With the commendable object of augmenting the</p>
        <p>funds of -tit?, Railway Benevolent Institution a concert was held at the Parr Hall nt Monlty evening awl was fairly \vol.). atondel An ex.:tellent prQgramme was submitte:l by the following artistes Arline Harrison. (sT)pran )), Miss Nellie Anierton (contralt.)), Mr. Paid Waring, Liverpool (tenor), Mr. Geprge Hartley Ditchburn, Nlanchester(bass),Mr. R. H. Pearson (violin solo), Mr. Frank Coops (elocutionist) and ;.Ir. William Whittle (humorist). Mt tho artistes acquitted themselves admirably and • their efforts met with cordial appreciation. Mr. W. H. Payton accompanied with his usual taste and skill. Dr. Markel and the Dasseldort Exhibition.</p>
        <p>At the 0!il Aeldenly the other evening an intereit-</p>
        <p>_ • _ ing addresA on the recent Dusseldorf Exhibition was delivored to the members of the Polyglot Society by Dr. Markel. Speaking conversatio ially in (3-3r.nan, Dr. Markel mentioned th fact that towards the,end of the 19th century the opinion had become establishel, that the time of international exhibitions for the pre- sent at any rate, had come to an end, and the general interest looked more and more towards those exhibi- tions which were locally- or -g3ographically The experience of Chicag) and Paris taught th 3 lesson tint the mass of detail shown was so great that even a specialist loses too much time in collecting out of this mass of details that which interests him specially If an exhibition is to be useful it must not come doWn to the rank of •a fair, bet must b 3 geographically limited so that it m ty bo possible to compare the pro- ducts with those of other nations. Dr. Ifarkel then described various branches of the exhibition and re- ferred to the progress mtde of late wears in various industries in the Rhino province and Westphalia.— The meeting was brought to a conclusion by a vote of hanks to Dr. Markel, proposed by I\lr- OrAel, and tsecolided by Mr. H. M. Ashton.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON: I am a member of the Electricity and Tramways , Cominittee, and I am of opinion thlt too much money is being asked for. - • The IsspEc ron : There is no harm in that ; none. On the ether hand, if the money is care fully looked after and not spent till it is wanted there is no harm done. Then it must also be borne in mind that it is not neces- sary to raise the whole of the loan. You knowthat as well as Id°. Councillor PEMBERTON : Yes, what increase has there been in the power during the last three months—l mean in the demani for power: -- WITNE4S : I have no figures here. COUTICiIIOr PEMBERTON : We have to take it for granted. In anew industry like this, in a new station, the increase would be very great for the first 18 months., WITNESS : I have gone into that matter very carefully and 1 have no doubt that the maximum load will be over 750 kwts next winter. Councillor PEMBERTON: plant for 1500 kwt. The INSPECTOR.: 'I don't think one need pursue th ! matter any further. I don't suppose the Corporation [ will have any objection to having the loan for one ma- chine and the main boiler now, and a loan for , the re- mainder afterwails. it is only aquestion of writing to the Board again. . Catmcillor PEMBERTON ; The only point I have to press is that too much machinery should net be put downtill it is actually required. The Inspector : I dont want to pursue the matter any further. The Board will be quite willing to sanc- tion a part of this loan now and the other part later. CoUncillor PEMBERTON : That arrangement Will meet my views. The increase will not be so great afterwards ; It' isil well understood that for street lighting. gas, 1 lighting is so much improved that it is very much cheaper than electric light and therefore, there will be \ no increase on that account. . THE BOROUGH TREASURER AND THE TOWN'S INDEBTEDNESS. THE BOROUGH DEBT. £1,014,603 OUTSTANDING.</p>
